window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "computation-theory", "lessons": {"alphabets-strings-languages": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-talk-about-strings-at-all\" data-h=\"3\">Why we talk about strings at all</h3>\n<p>Every computer program you have ever used reads some input and produces some output. A spell checker reads a word and answers \"is this spelled correctly?\" A compiler reads source code and answers \"is this a valid program?\" A login page reads a password and answers \"does this match the rules: at least 8 characters, one digit, one capital letter?\" Underneath very different-looking tasks there is one common shape: some input, written as a sequence of symbols, and a yes-or-no question about it.</p>\n<p>The theory of computation studies exactly that shape. Instead of worrying about Python, phones or graphics cards, it strips computation down to the bare bones: a finite set of symbols, finite sequences of those symbols, and sets of sequences that we want a machine to recognise. Once you have this vocabulary, you can ask very precise questions. Which sets of strings can be recognised by a machine with a fixed, small amount of memory? Which need a stack? Which can be recognised by any program at all? Which can be recognised quickly? This whole course answers those questions, and it all starts with three words: alphabet, string and language.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-door-keypad\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a door keypad</h3>\n<p>Imagine the keypad on an office door. It has the keys 0 to 9 and nothing else. Every attempt to open the door is a finite sequence of key presses, like 4, 0, 7, 1. The door opens for some sequences (the correct code) and stays shut for everything else. The keys are the <strong>alphabet</strong>, each attempt is a <strong>string</strong>, and the set of attempts that open the door is a <strong>language</strong>. That is the whole picture in miniature, and every idea in this lesson is just a careful version of it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"alphabets\" data-h=\"3\">Alphabets</h3>\n<p>An <strong>alphabet</strong> is any finite, non-empty set of symbols. We usually call it <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> (capital sigma). Some examples:</p>\n<p>The binary alphabet is <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma = \\{0, 1\\}</span>. The alphabet of DNA is <span class=\"m\">\\{A, C, G, T\\}</span>. The alphabet of the door keypad is <span class=\"m\">\\{0, 1, 2, \\dots, 9\\}</span>, which has 10 symbols. The alphabet of English lowercase letters has 26 symbols. What a symbol \"means\" does not matter to the theory; only that we can tell symbols apart, and that there are finitely many of them.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Alphabet</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A finite, non-empty set of symbols, usually written <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>. Example: <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma = \\{0,1\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"strings-length-and-the-empty-string\" data-h=\"3\">Strings, length and the empty string</h3>\n<p>A <strong>string</strong> (also called a word) over <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> is a finite sequence of symbols from <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, written next to each other. Over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span>, the strings <span class=\"m\">0110</span>, <span class=\"m\">1</span> and <span class=\"m\">00000</span> are all fine. The <strong>length</strong> of a string <span class=\"m\">w</span>, written <span class=\"m\">|w|</span>, is the number of symbols in it, counting repeats: <span class=\"m\">|0110| = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">|1| = 1</span>.</p>\n<p>There is one special string that confuses almost everyone at first: the <strong>empty string</strong>, written <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> (epsilon). It is the sequence with no symbols at all, so <span class=\"m\">|\\varepsilon| = 0</span>. Think of it as the door attempt where nobody pressed a key. It is a perfectly real string, the same way 0 is a perfectly real number.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Empty string (<span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The unique string with no symbols. Its length is <span class=\"m\">|\\varepsilon| = 0</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon w = w\\varepsilon = w</span> for every string <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"building-new-strings-concatenation-powers-and-reversal\" data-h=\"3\">Building new strings: concatenation, powers and reversal</h3>\n<p>The most important operation on strings is <strong>concatenation</strong>: writing one string right after another. If <span class=\"m\">u = 01</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = 110</span> then <span class=\"m\">uv = 01110</span>. Lengths simply add: <span class=\"m\">|uv| = |u| + |v| = 2 + 3 = 5</span>. Concatenation is associative, <span class=\"m\">(uv)w = u(vw)</span>, but not commutative: <span class=\"m\">vu = 11001</span>, which is a different string from <span class=\"m\">uv</span>. The empty string behaves like the number 1 in multiplication: <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon u = u</span>.</p>\n<p>Repeated concatenation gives <strong>powers</strong>: <span class=\"m\">w^0 = \\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">w^1 = w</span>, <span class=\"m\">w^2 = ww</span>, and so on. So <span class=\"m\">(01)^3 = 010101</span>, and its length is <span class=\"m\">3 \\times 2 = 6</span>. The <strong>reversal</strong> <span class=\"m\">w^R</span> writes <span class=\"m\">w</span> backwards: <span class=\"m\">(0111)^R = 1110</span>. A string that equals its own reversal, like <span class=\"m\">0110</span> or <span class=\"m\">10101</span>, is a <strong>palindrome</strong>.</p>\n<p>Three more words describe pieces of strings. A <strong>prefix</strong> of <span class=\"m\">w</span> is a piece taken from the start, a <strong>suffix</strong> is a piece taken from the end, and a <strong>substring</strong> is any consecutive piece. For <span class=\"m\">w = 0110</span>, the prefixes are <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon, 0, 01, 011, 0110</span>. A string of length <span class=\"m\">n</span> always has exactly <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> prefixes, because you can cut it at any of <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> places.</p>\n<h3 id=\"counting-strings\" data-h=\"3\">Counting strings</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^k</span> be the set of all strings of length exactly <span class=\"m\">k</span>. Each of the <span class=\"m\">k</span> positions can hold any of the <span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma|</span> symbols independently, so by the multiplication rule\n<span class=\"mb\">|\\Sigma^k| = |\\Sigma|^k.</span>\nOver <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> there are <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> strings of length 3: <span class=\"m\">000, 001, 010, 011, 100, 101, 110, 111</span>. Over DNA there are <span class=\"m\">4^3 = 64</span> strings of length 3, which is exactly why biology has 64 codons. And <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^0 = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span> has one element, matching <span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma|^0 = 1</span>.</p>\n<p>The set of all strings of every finite length is written <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> (read \"sigma star\"):\n<span class=\"mb\">\\Sigma^* = \\Sigma^0 \\cup \\Sigma^1 \\cup \\Sigma^2 \\cup \\cdots</span>\nIt is infinite, but every single string in it is finite. That distinction matters: there is no \"infinitely long string\" in <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"languages\" data-h=\"3\">Languages</h3>\n<p>A <strong>language</strong> over <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> is any set of strings over <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, that is, any subset <span class=\"m\">L \\subseteq \\Sigma^*</span>. It can be finite, like the set of correct door codes <span class=\"m\">\\{4071\\}</span>, or infinite, like \"all binary strings with an even number of 1s\". It can even be empty.</p>\n<p>Here is the key move of the entire subject: every yes-or-no question about inputs is a language. The question \"is this binary number divisible by 3?\" is the language <span class=\"m\">\\{0, 11, 110, 1001, \\dots\\}</span> of strings whose value is a multiple of 3. \"Is this a syntactically valid Python program?\" is the language of all valid Python files. Solving the problem means building a machine that, given any string, correctly says whether it belongs to the language.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Any set of strings over an alphabet <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, i.e. a subset <span class=\"m\">L \\subseteq \\Sigma^*</span>. A decision problem is the same thing as a language: the set of inputs whose answer is yes.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"operations-on-languages\" data-h=\"3\">Operations on languages</h3>\n<p>Because languages are sets, you can take unions, intersections and complements: <span class=\"m\">\\overline{L} = \\Sigma^* \\setminus L</span> is everything not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>. Languages also inherit operations from strings. The <strong>concatenation</strong> of two languages is\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1 L_2 = \\{xy : x \\in L_1,\\ y \\in L_2\\}.</span>\nFor <span class=\"m\">L_1 = \\{0, 01\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2 = \\{1, 11\\}</span> you get <span class=\"m\">\\{01, 011, 011, 0111\\}</span>, and since sets ignore duplicates, <span class=\"m\">L_1L_2 = \\{01, 011, 0111\\}</span>, only 3 strings even though <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 2 = 4</span> pairs were tried.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Kleene star</strong> <span class=\"m\">L^*</span> is the set of all strings formed by gluing together zero or more strings from <span class=\"m\">L</span>: <span class=\"m\">L^* = L^0 \\cup L^1 \\cup L^2 \\cup \\cdots</span> where <span class=\"m\">L^0 = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>. If <span class=\"m\">L = \\{01\\}</span> then <span class=\"m\">L^* = \\{\\varepsilon, 01, 0101, 010101, \\dots\\}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kleene star</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a language <span class=\"m\">L</span>, <span class=\"m\">L^* = \\bigcup_{k \\ge 0} L^k</span>: all concatenations of zero or more strings from <span class=\"m\">L</span>. It always contains <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works-this-way-and-common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works this way, and common mistakes</h3>\n<p>These definitions are chosen so that strings behave like a number system for text: concatenation is the \"multiplication\", <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is the \"1\", and length turns concatenation into addition. That makes counting and induction proofs about strings straightforward.</p>\n<p>Three sets are easy to mix up. <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is a string. <span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span> is a language containing exactly one string, the empty one. <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is the empty language, with no strings at all. They behave very differently: <span class=\"m\">L\\{\\varepsilon\\} = L</span>, but <span class=\"m\">L\\varnothing = \\varnothing</span>, because there is no string in <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> to glue onto. Another classic trap: <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing^* = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>, not <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span>, because zero pieces glued together always gives the empty string. Finally, remember that <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> contains infinitely many strings, yet each one has finite length.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An alphabet is a finite set of symbols, a string is a finite sequence of them, and a language is a set of strings. Concatenation glues strings, <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is the empty string, <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^k</span> has <span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma|^k</span> members, and <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> is all strings. Every decision problem is a language, so \"can a machine solve this problem?\" becomes \"can a machine recognise this set of strings?\" The rest of the course is about which machines can recognise which languages.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;regex&quot;, &quot;regex&quot;: &quot;(0+1)*&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0110&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The regular expression (0+1)* describes every binary string. The table lists them by length.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Look at the \"matches\" column: count how many strings of each length appear.\n2. Change the expression to <code>(0+1)(0+1)(0+1)</code> to see only <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^3</span>.\n3. Change it to <code>(01)*</code> to see the Kleene star of the language <span class=\"m\">\\{01\\}</span>.\n4. Type different strings in the String box and watch whether they are \"in the language\".</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the counts for <code>(0+1)*</code> are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32: exactly <span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma|^k = 2^k</span>, with the single string of length 0 being <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>. With <code>(01)*</code> only even lengths appear, one string each, because <span class=\"m\">L^*</span> only contains glued copies of <span class=\"m\">01</span>. This is the counting rule and the Kleene star from the article, made visible.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">|uv| = |u| + |v|, \\qquad |w^k| = k\\,|w|, \\qquad (uv)^R = v^R u^R</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|\\Sigma^k| = |\\Sigma|^k, \\qquad \\Sigma^* = \\bigcup_{k\\ge0} \\Sigma^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{binary strings of length} \\le n:\\ \\ 1 + 2 + \\cdots + 2^n = 2^{n+1} - 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1L_2 = \\{xy : x\\in L_1, y\\in L_2\\}, \\qquad L^* = \\bigcup_{k\\ge0} L^k,\\ L^0 = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\"><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> is countably infinite</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any alphabet <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, the strings in <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> can be listed in a single infinite sequence that contains every string exactly once.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>List strings in <strong>shortlex order</strong>: first by length, and within each length alphabetically. Over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> this gives <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, 000, \\dots</span> Each length class <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^k</span> is finite (<span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma|^k</span> strings), so any particular string <span class=\"m\">w</span> of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> appears after at most <span class=\"m\">1 + |\\Sigma| + \\cdots + |\\Sigma|^k</span> entries. Every string therefore gets a finite position in the list, which is what countable means.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reversal of a concatenation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For all strings <span class=\"m\">u, v</span>: <span class=\"m\">(uv)^R = v^R u^R</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">u = a_1\\cdots a_m</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = b_1 \\cdots b_n</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">uv = a_1\\cdots a_m b_1 \\cdots b_n</span>, and reading it backwards gives <span class=\"m\">b_n \\cdots b_1 a_m \\cdots a_1 = v^R u^R</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count the strings of length at most 3 over <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma = \\{a, b, c\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Strings of length 0: <span class=\"m\">3^0 = 1</span> (just <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>).</li>\n<li>Length 1: <span class=\"m\">3^1 = 3</span>. Length 2: <span class=\"m\">3^2 = 9</span>. Length 3: <span class=\"m\">3^3 = 27</span>.</li>\n<li>Add the disjoint groups: <span class=\"m\">1 + 3 + 9 + 27 = 40</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">L_1 L_2</span> for <span class=\"m\">L_1 = \\{a, ab\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2 = \\{b, bb\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Glue every <span class=\"m\">x \\in L_1</span> to every <span class=\"m\">y \\in L_2</span>: <span class=\"m\">a\\cdot b = ab</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\cdot bb = abb</span>, <span class=\"m\">ab \\cdot b = abb</span>, <span class=\"m\">ab \\cdot bb = abbb</span>.</li>\n<li>Remove duplicates, since a language is a set: <span class=\"m\">abb</span> appears twice.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L_1L_2 = \\{ab, abb, abbb\\}</span>, which has 3 strings, not 4.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">List the first 8 strings of <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}^*</span> in shortlex order</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Length 0: <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>.</li>\n<li>Length 1, alphabetical: <span class=\"m\">0, 1</span>.</li>\n<li>Length 2: <span class=\"m\">00, 01, 10, 11</span>.</li>\n<li>Length 3 starts with <span class=\"m\">000</span>. So the list begins <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10, 11, 000</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">L = \\{0, 01\\}</span>. (a) List all strings in <span class=\"m\">L^2</span>. (b) Is <span class=\"m\">0010 \\in L^*</span>? (c) Is <span class=\"m\">0110 \\in L^*</span>?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">L^2 = \\{00, 001, 010, 0101\\}</span> (four distinct strings).\n(b) Yes: <span class=\"m\">0010 = 0 \\cdot 01 \\cdot 0</span>, three pieces from <span class=\"m\">L</span>, so it is in <span class=\"m\">L^3 \\subseteq L^*</span>.\n(c) No. Every piece from <span class=\"m\">L</span> starts with <span class=\"m\">0</span> and contains at most one <span class=\"m\">1</span>, which must directly follow a <span class=\"m\">0</span>. In <span class=\"m\">0110</span> the second <span class=\"m\">1</span> follows a <span class=\"m\">1</span>, so no split into pieces from <span class=\"m\">L</span> exists.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compilers start with a <strong>lexer</strong> that splits source code into tokens: the alphabet is the character set, and each token type (identifiers, numbers, keywords) is a language. Every decision problem in complexity theory, from \"is this graph 3-colourable?\" to \"does this program halt?\", is formally a language of encoded inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Language models work over an alphabet of <strong>tokens</strong> (a vocabulary of perhaps 50,000 pieces). A sequence of <span class=\"m\">k</span> tokens is a string in <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^k</span>, and there are <span class=\"m\">50{,}000^k</span> of them, which is why models must generalise instead of memorising. Byte-pair encoding builds that vocabulary by repeatedly concatenating frequent pairs of symbols.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>DNA is a string over <span class=\"m\">\\{A, C, G, T\\}</span>. Genes are read in substrings of length 3 called codons, and there are <span class=\"m\">4^3 = 64</span> of them, coding for 20 amino acids plus stop signals. Searching a genome for a motif is a substring problem on a string with billions of symbols.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many binary strings have length exactly 4?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|\\Sigma^4| = 2^4 = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the length of the empty string <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> has no symbols, so <span class=\"m\">|\\varepsilon| = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For any language <span class=\"m\">L</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">L\\varnothing</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">L</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">L^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>To form a string in <span class=\"m\">L\\varnothing</span> you need a string from <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> to glue on, and there are none. Compare <span class=\"m\">L\\{\\varepsilon\\} = L</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">L = \\{a, bb\\}</span>, how many strings are in <span class=\"m\">L^2</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">aa, abb, bba, bbbb</span>: all four combinations are different strings.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many strings over <span class=\"m\">\\{a, b, c\\}</span> have length at most 2?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"13\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 + 3 + 9 = 13</span>, counting <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing^*</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">L^0 = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span> for every <span class=\"m\">L</span>, and every higher power of <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is empty. So <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing^* = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "dfa": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-machine-with-almost-no-memory\" data-h=\"3\">A machine with almost no memory</h3>\n<p>Think about a subway turnstile. It has only two situations it can be in: locked or unlocked. Put in a coin and it unlocks. Push through and it locks again. Push while locked and nothing happens. The turnstile does not remember how many people went through yesterday or what coins it has seen; it only remembers which of its two situations it is currently in. Yet that tiny memory is enough to do its job perfectly.</p>\n<p>A <strong>deterministic finite automaton</strong> (DFA) is the mathematical version of the turnstile. It is the simplest model of a computer that reads input: a machine with a fixed, finite number of internal situations, called <strong>states</strong>, which reads a string one symbol at a time and moves between states according to fixed rules. When the string ends, the machine says yes or no depending on which state it finished in. DFAs sound almost too simple to be useful, but they power text search, network packet filters, lexical analysers in every compiler, and the controllers in vending machines and traffic lights.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-parts-of-a-dfa\" data-h=\"3\">The parts of a DFA</h3>\n<p>Formally a DFA is a 5-tuple <span class=\"m\">M = (Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)</span>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"m\">Q</span> is a finite set of <strong>states</strong>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> is the input alphabet.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\delta : Q \\times \\Sigma \\to Q</span> is the <strong>transition function</strong>: in state <span class=\"m\">q</span>, reading symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span>, go to state <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q, a)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q_0 \\in Q</span> is the <strong>start state</strong>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">F \\subseteq Q</span> is the set of <strong>accepting</strong> (or final) states.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We draw DFAs as diagrams: circles for states, an arrow from nowhere into the start state, a double circle for each accepting state, and a labelled arrow from <span class=\"m\">q</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q,a)</span> for every state and symbol. The word <strong>deterministic</strong> means that for every state and every symbol there is exactly one arrow to follow. The machine never has a choice and never gets stuck.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Deterministic finite automaton (DFA)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A 5-tuple <span class=\"m\">(Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)</span> with finite state set <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, alphabet <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>, total transition function <span class=\"m\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma\\to Q</span>, start state <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> and accepting states <span class=\"m\">F \\subseteq Q</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"running-a-dfa-on-a-string\" data-h=\"3\">Running a DFA on a string</h3>\n<p>To run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on a string <span class=\"m\">w = a_1 a_2 \\cdots a_n</span>, start in <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, read <span class=\"m\">a_1</span> and move to <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q_0, a_1)</span>, read <span class=\"m\">a_2</span> and move again, and so on until the string is used up. If the final state is in <span class=\"m\">F</span>, the DFA <strong>accepts</strong> <span class=\"m\">w</span>; otherwise it <strong>rejects</strong>. The set of all strings <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts is the <strong>language of</strong> <span class=\"m\">M</span>, written <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span>.</p>\n<p>Let us build one. Suppose we want to accept binary strings with an even number of 1s. What does the machine need to remember while reading? Not the whole string, and not even the count of 1s: just whether the count so far is even or odd. That gives two states, <em>even</em> (the start state, and accepting, since zero 1s is an even number) and <em>odd</em>. Reading a 0 leaves the parity alone, so 0 loops back to the same state. Reading a 1 flips the parity, so 1 swaps the states. On input <span class=\"m\">1011</span>: even <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> odd (read 1) <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> odd (read 0) <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> even (read 1) <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> odd (read 1). It ends in <em>odd</em>, so it rejects, and indeed <span class=\"m\">1011</span> has three 1s.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"dfa\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;even # of 1s&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A two-state DFA for \"even number of 1s\". Type strings and watch the path.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"states-are-summaries-of-the-past\" data-h=\"3\">States are summaries of the past</h3>\n<p>The big design idea is this: <strong>a state is a summary of everything about the input so far that you still need</strong>. To design a DFA, ask \"what is the minimum I must remember to decide the answer at the end?\" and make one state for each possible value of that memory.</p>\n<p>Here is a more surprising example: accept binary numbers divisible by 3. You might think the machine needs to remember the whole number, which can be arbitrarily large. But it only needs the remainder mod 3, which is 0, 1 or 2. When you append a bit <span class=\"m\">b</span> to a binary number with value <span class=\"m\">v</span>, the new value is <span class=\"m\">2v + b</span>. So if the old remainder was <span class=\"m\">r</span>, the new remainder is <span class=\"m\">(2r + b) \\bmod 3</span>. That gives three states <span class=\"m\">r_0, r_1, r_2</span> and the rule <span class=\"m\">\\delta(r, b) = (2r+b) \\bmod 3</span>. Try <span class=\"m\">110</span>, which is 6: <span class=\"m\">r_0 \\xrightarrow{1} r_1 \\xrightarrow{1} r_0 \\xrightarrow{0} r_0</span>. It ends in <span class=\"m\">r_0</span>, so 6 is accepted. Try <span class=\"m\">1101</span>, which is 13: <span class=\"m\">r_0 \\to r_1 \\to r_0 \\to r_0 \\to r_1</span>, rejected, and indeed <span class=\"m\">13 = 4\\times 3 + 1</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-extended-transition-function\" data-h=\"3\">The extended transition function</h3>\n<p>It is handy to have notation for \"where does a whole string take me?\" Define <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta(q, w)</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta(q, \\varepsilon) = q</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta(q, wa) = \\delta(\\hat\\delta(q, w), a)</span>. This just says: to process <span class=\"m\">wa</span>, first process <span class=\"m\">w</span>, then take one more step on <span class=\"m\">a</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">L(M) = \\{ w : \\hat\\delta(q_0, w) \\in F \\}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"regular-languages\" data-h=\"3\">Regular languages</h3>\n<p>A language is called <strong>regular</strong> if some DFA recognises it. \"Even number of 1s\", \"divisible by 3\" and \"contains 11\" are all regular. Later we will see that regular languages are exactly the ones described by regular expressions, and we will also prove that some languages, like <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n : n \\ge 0\\}</span>, are not regular, because no finite memory can count unboundedly high.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Regular language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular if <span class=\"m\">L = L(M)</span> for some DFA <span class=\"m\">M</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"building-new-dfas-from-old-ones\" data-h=\"3\">Building new DFAs from old ones</h3>\n<p>Because a DFA always finishes in exactly one state, <strong>complement</strong> is easy: swap accepting and non-accepting states. The new machine accepts exactly the strings the old one rejected.</p>\n<p>For <strong>intersection</strong> we use the <strong>product construction</strong>. To accept strings that have an even number of 1s <em>and</em> are divisible by 3, run both machines at the same time. A state of the combined machine is a pair (parity state, remainder state), so there are <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 3 = 6</span> states. On each symbol, both parts move according to their own rules. Accept when both parts are accepting. For union, accept when at least one part is accepting. This shows that the regular languages are closed under complement, intersection and union.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most frequent error is a <strong>missing transition</strong>. A DFA must have exactly one arrow for every state and every symbol. If some situation should lead to \"reject forever\", add a <strong>dead state</strong> (also called a trap state): a non-accepting state that loops to itself on every symbol. Second, check the empty string: <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is accepted exactly when the start state is accepting. Third, remember that accepting states do not stop the machine. A DFA always reads the whole input; passing through an accepting state in the middle means nothing. Finally, complementing by swapping states only works when every transition is present; with a missing arrow, strings that would \"fall off\" the machine are rejected both before and after the swap.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A DFA is a finite set of states with exactly one transition per state and symbol, a start state and a set of accepting states. It reads its input left to right and accepts if it ends in an accepting state. Each state stands for a summary of the input so far, and good design means choosing the smallest summary that is enough to answer the question. Languages recognised by DFAs are called regular, and they are closed under complement, union and intersection.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"dfa\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;divisible by 3&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Type a binary number. The DFA tracks its remainder mod 3.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Type <span class=\"m\">1</span>, then <span class=\"m\">11</span>, then <span class=\"m\">110</span>, then <span class=\"m\">1101</span>. Before each step, predict the state using <span class=\"m\">(2r+b) \\bmod 3</span>.\n2. Type the binary for 9 (<span class=\"m\">1001</span>) and for 10 (<span class=\"m\">1010</span>). Which is accepted?\n3. Switch the machine to \"contains 11\" and find the state that, once entered, is never left.\n4. Switch to \"even # of 1s\" and try the empty string.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the state is always the remainder of the number read so far, exactly as in the article, so the machine handles numbers of any size with three states. In \"contains 11\" the accepting state loops to itself on both symbols, because once you have seen <span class=\"m\">11</span> nothing can undo it. And the empty string is accepted by \"even # of 1s\" because zero is even and the start state is accepting.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">M = (Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F), \\qquad \\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma \\to Q</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat\\delta(q,\\varepsilon) = q, \\qquad \\hat\\delta(q, wa) = \\delta(\\hat\\delta(q,w), a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(M) = \\{w \\in \\Sigma^* : \\hat\\delta(q_0, w) \\in F\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{mod-}m \\text{ DFA: } \\delta(r, b) = (2r + b) \\bmod m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{product DFA: } Q = Q_1 \\times Q_2,\\ \\ \\delta((p,q),a) = (\\delta_1(p,a), \\delta_2(q,a))</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Closure under complement, union and intersection</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> are regular, so are <span class=\"m\">\\overline{L_1}</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cup L_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cap L_2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Complement: take a DFA for <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> and replace <span class=\"m\">F</span> by <span class=\"m\">Q \\setminus F</span>. Since the run on any <span class=\"m\">w</span> ends in exactly one state, <span class=\"m\">w</span> is accepted by the new machine iff it was rejected by the old one.\nIntersection and union: given <span class=\"m\">M_1, M_2</span>, build the product DFA with states <span class=\"m\">Q_1 \\times Q_2</span>, start <span class=\"m\">(q_1, q_2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\delta((p,q),a) = (\\delta_1(p,a), \\delta_2(q,a))</span>. By induction on <span class=\"m\">|w|</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta((q_1,q_2), w) = (\\hat\\delta_1(q_1, w), \\hat\\delta_2(q_2, w))</span>. Choosing accepting states <span class=\"m\">F_1 \\times F_2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cap L_2</span>; choosing <span class=\"m\">\\{(p,q) : p\\in F_1 \\text{ or } q \\in F_2\\}</span> gives <span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cup L_2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Trace the divisible-by-3 DFA on <span class=\"m\">10010</span> (the number 18)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start in <span class=\"m\">r_0</span>. Read 1: <span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot0+1) \\bmod 3 = 1</span>, go to <span class=\"m\">r_1</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot1+0) \\bmod 3 = 2</span>, go to <span class=\"m\">r_2</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot2+0) \\bmod 3 = 1</span>, go to <span class=\"m\">r_1</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 1: <span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot1+1) \\bmod 3 = 0</span>, go to <span class=\"m\">r_0</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">(0+0) \\bmod 3 = 0</span>, stay in <span class=\"m\">r_0</span>. It ends in <span class=\"m\">r_0</span>, so accept. Check: <span class=\"m\">18 = 6 \\times 3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Design a DFA for \"even number of 0s and odd number of 1s\"</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Memory needed: parity of 0s (2 options) and parity of 1s (2 options), so <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 2 = 4</span> states: <span class=\"m\">(E,E), (E,O), (O,E), (O,O)</span>, where the first letter is the 0-parity.</li>\n<li>Start state <span class=\"m\">(E,E)</span>, since the empty string has zero of each.</li>\n<li>Reading 0 flips the first coordinate; reading 1 flips the second. For example <span class=\"m\">\\delta((E,O), 0) = (O,O)</span>.</li>\n<li>The only accepting state is <span class=\"m\">(E,O)</span>. Check with <span class=\"m\">001</span>: <span class=\"m\">(E,E)\\to(O,E)\\to(E,E)\\to(E,O)</span>, accept. It has two 0s and one 1.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many states does the product construction use for \"length divisible by 4\" <span class=\"m\">\\cap</span> \"divisible by 5 in binary\"?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Length mod 4 needs 4 states.</li>\n<li>Value mod 5 needs 5 states (remainders 0 to 4).</li>\n<li>The product has one state per pair: <span class=\"m\">4 \\times 5 = 20</span> states.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Give a DFA over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> for the language of strings that do <strong>not</strong> contain <span class=\"m\">11</span> as a substring, and trace it on <span class=\"m\">10101</span> and <span class=\"m\">0110</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>First build a DFA for \"contains 11\": state <span class=\"m\">a</span> (last symbol was not 1, or nothing read yet), <span class=\"m\">b</span> (last symbol was a 1, no <span class=\"m\">11</span> seen), <span class=\"m\">c</span> (<span class=\"m\">11</span> seen). Transitions: <span class=\"m\">a \\xrightarrow0 a</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\xrightarrow1 b</span>, <span class=\"m\">b\\xrightarrow0 a</span>, <span class=\"m\">b \\xrightarrow1 c</span>, <span class=\"m\">c \\xrightarrow{0,1} c</span>; accepting <span class=\"m\">\\{c\\}</span>.\nComplement it by swapping: accepting states become <span class=\"m\">\\{a, b\\}</span>.\n<span class=\"m\">10101</span>: <span class=\"m\">a\\to b\\to a\\to b\\to a\\to b</span>, ends in <span class=\"m\">b</span>, accept (no <span class=\"m\">11</span>). <span class=\"m\">0110</span>: <span class=\"m\">a\\to a\\to b\\to c\\to c</span>, ends in <span class=\"m\">c</span>, reject.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>Lexers</strong> in compilers (tools like <code>lex</code> and <code>flex</code>) turn token patterns into one big DFA and scan source code in a single left-to-right pass. Network intrusion detection, <code>grep</code>, protocol parsers and hardware controllers (traffic lights, elevators, vending machines) are DFAs too, because a fixed number of states means constant memory and one step per input symbol.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Simple sequence models are finite-state: a <strong>hidden Markov model</strong> is a probabilistic finite automaton, and weighted finite-state transducers are used in speech recognition pipelines. Researchers also probe how well recurrent networks and transformers can learn to simulate DFAs such as \"parity of 1s\", a classic hard case for generalising to longer inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A <strong>vending machine</strong> that sells a 30-cent item and takes 10-cent coins is a DFA with states 0, 10, 20 and \"dispense\": each coin moves it to the next state, and it only needs to remember the amount inserted so far, never the history of which coins.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a DFA, how many transitions leave each state for each input symbol?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">zero or one</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">exactly one</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">one or more</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">any number</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Deterministic means total and unique: <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q,a)</span> is defined and is a single state for every <span class=\"m\">q</span> and <span class=\"m\">a</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">After reading <span class=\"m\">1011</span> (the number 11), which remainder state is the divisible-by-3 DFA in? Give the remainder.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">11 = 3 \\times 3 + 2</span>. Step by step: <span class=\"m\">r_0\\to r_1\\to r_2\\to r_2\\to r_2</span>, since <span class=\"m\">(2\\cdot2+1) \\bmod 3 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A DFA accepts <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> exactly when...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">its start state is accepting</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it has a dead state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every state is accepting</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>On the empty input the machine never moves, so it ends where it started.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The product of a 4-state DFA and a 5-state DFA has how many states?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Product states are pairs: <span class=\"m\">4 \\times 5 = 20</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How do you get a DFA for the complement of <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">reverse every arrow</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">swap accepting and non-accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">add a dead state</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each run ends in exactly one state, so swapping <span class=\"m\">F</span> with <span class=\"m\">Q\\setminus F</span> flips every answer.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the fewest states a DFA needs to accept strings whose length is a multiple of 3?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It must remember length mod 3, which has 3 possible values, and those three situations really are different.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "nfa": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"what-if-a-machine-could-guess\" data-h=\"3\">What if a machine could guess?</h3>\n<p>Suppose you are scanning a long list of binary strings and want to accept those that end in <span class=\"m\">01</span>. Reading left to right, you face an annoying problem: every time you see a <span class=\"m\">0</span>, it <em>might</em> be the second-to-last symbol, or it might not. A DFA handles this by carefully tracking what the last couple of symbols were. But imagine a machine that could simply <strong>guess</strong>: \"this 0 is the start of the final <span class=\"m\">01</span>\". If the guess is right, the rest of the input confirms it; if the guess is wrong, that line of reasoning just dies. The machine accepts if <em>any</em> sequence of guesses works.</p>\n<p>That is a <strong>nondeterministic finite automaton</strong> (NFA). Nondeterminism is not randomness and it is not a physical machine; it is a way of describing computations in which several possibilities are explored at once. NFAs are often far smaller and easier to design than DFAs, and, remarkably, they recognise exactly the same languages. They are also the bridge between regular expressions and DFAs, and nondeterminism comes back later as the heart of the P versus NP question.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>An NFA is a 5-tuple <span class=\"m\">N = (Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)</span>, just like a DFA, except the transition function returns a <strong>set</strong> of states:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\delta : Q \\times \\Sigma_\\varepsilon \\to \\mathcal{P}(Q), \\qquad \\Sigma_\\varepsilon = \\Sigma \\cup \\{\\varepsilon\\}.</span>\nHere <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal{P}(Q)</span> is the power set, the set of all subsets of <span class=\"m\">Q</span>. Three new things can happen. A state can have <strong>several</strong> arrows with the same label, so the machine has a choice. A state can have <strong>no</strong> arrow for some symbol; a computation path that needs that arrow simply dies. And there can be <strong>ε-transitions</strong>: arrows labelled <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> that the machine may follow without reading any input.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Nondeterministic finite automaton (NFA)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A 5-tuple <span class=\"m\">(Q,\\Sigma,\\delta,q_0,F)</span> where <span class=\"m\">\\delta: Q \\times (\\Sigma\\cup\\{\\varepsilon\\}) \\to \\mathcal P(Q)</span>. It accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> if at least one way of following transitions while reading all of <span class=\"m\">w</span> ends in an accepting state.</p></div></div>\n<p>An NFA <strong>accepts</strong> <span class=\"m\">w</span> if there is at least one path from <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> that reads all of <span class=\"m\">w</span> (possibly taking ε-moves in between) and ends in an accepting state. It <strong>rejects</strong> <span class=\"m\">w</span> only if every possible path either dies or ends in a non-accepting state. The asymmetry is important: acceptance needs one witness; rejection needs all paths to fail.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-ends-in-01-nfa\" data-h=\"3\">The \"ends in 01\" NFA</h3>\n<p>Here is the guessing machine: states <span class=\"m\">q_0, q_1, q_2</span> with <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> accepting. In <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, on 0 you may stay in <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> or jump to <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> (the guess \"this is the final 0\"); on 1 you stay in <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>. From <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>, a 1 leads to <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> has no arrow on 0. State <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> has no outgoing arrows at all, so if any input remains after reaching it, that path dies. Three states, and the design follows directly from the description of the language.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simulating-an-nfa-track-the-set-of-possible-states\" data-h=\"3\">Simulating an NFA: track the set of possible states</h3>\n<p>How do we actually run an NFA? Picture each choice splitting the machine into clones, one per option. Instead of following clones individually, keep track of the <strong>set</strong> of states that some clone is in. Start with <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>. For each symbol, the new set is everything reachable from any state in the current set on that symbol.</p>\n<p>Run the \"ends in 01\" NFA on <span class=\"m\">1101</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow{1} \\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow{1} \\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow{0} \\{q_0, q_1\\} \\xrightarrow{1} \\{q_0, q_2\\}.</span>\nThe final set contains the accepting state <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, so the NFA accepts. On <span class=\"m\">0110</span> the sets are <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}, \\{q_0,q_1\\}, \\{q_0,q_2\\}, \\{q_0\\}, \\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>, and since no state in the final set is accepting, it rejects. Notice that <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> appeared in the middle, but that does not matter: only the set after the last symbol counts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"transitions-and-closure\" data-h=\"3\">ε-transitions and ε-closure</h3>\n<p>An ε-transition lets the machine change state \"for free\". This is very useful for combining machines. Suppose you have one machine for \"even number of 0s\" and another for \"even number of 1s\", and you want their union. Add a new start state <span class=\"m\">s</span> with ε-arrows to both old start states. From <span class=\"m\">s</span> the machine instantly becomes both machines at once, and it accepts if either one accepts.</p>\n<p>To handle ε-moves in the set simulation, we use the <strong>ε-closure</strong> <span class=\"m\">E(S)</span>: all states reachable from states in <span class=\"m\">S</span> using zero or more ε-arrows. The start set is <span class=\"m\">E(\\{q_0\\})</span>, and after each symbol we take the ε-closure of the result. In the union machine, <span class=\"m\">E(\\{s\\}) = \\{s, a_0, b_0\\}</span>, so before reading anything the machine is already in both start states.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">ε-closure</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a set of NFA states <span class=\"m\">S</span>, <span class=\"m\">E(S)</span> is the set of states reachable from <span class=\"m\">S</span> by following zero or more ε-transitions. It always contains <span class=\"m\">S</span> itself.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-nfas-are-not-magic\" data-h=\"3\">Why NFAs are not \"magic\"</h3>\n<p>It can feel like cheating that an NFA always guesses right. But the set simulation shows there is nothing magical: a deterministic computer can follow all guesses at once, because a finite machine has only finitely many states, so the set of possible states is one of finitely many subsets. In the next lesson we turn that observation into the <strong>subset construction</strong>, which converts any NFA into an equivalent DFA. So NFAs give convenience and compactness, not extra power.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not think of nondeterminism as \"picking randomly\"; an NFA accepts if <strong>some</strong> choice works, no matter how unlikely. Do not forget that a missing arrow kills only that path, not the whole computation. Always apply ε-closure both at the start and after every symbol; the most common bug in hand simulations is forgetting the closure at the start. And remember that complementing an NFA by swapping accepting states does <strong>not</strong> work in general, because \"some path accepts\" flipped is not \"some path rejects\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>An NFA may have several, one or no transitions on a symbol, and may take ε-moves without reading input. It accepts if at least one path ends in an accepting state after reading the whole string. You simulate it by tracking the set of all states it could be in, closing under ε-moves at each step. NFAs are often much smaller than DFAs and make constructions like union easy, yet they recognise exactly the regular languages.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;nfa&quot;, &quot;machine&quot;: &quot;ends in 01&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1101}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Pink states are all the states the NFA could be in. Use step to read one symbol at a time.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press \"start\", then \"step ▶\" four times on <span class=\"m\">1101</span>. Compare each set with the trace in the article.\n2. Type <span class=\"m\">0110</span> and step through. Watch <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> light up and then disappear.\n3. Switch the NFA to \"ε-NFA: even # of 0s OR even # of 1s\" and press start. Count the states that are active before any symbol is read.\n4. On the ε-NFA, try <span class=\"m\">01</span>, <span class=\"m\">001</span> and <span class=\"m\">0101</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> an NFA's \"current state\" is really a set. In step 2 the machine passes through an accepting state but rejects, because only the final set counts. In step 3 three states are active at the start, <span class=\"m\">\\{s, a_0, b_0\\}</span>: that is the ε-closure. In step 4, <span class=\"m\">01</span> is rejected (one 0 and one 1, both odd), while <span class=\"m\">001</span> is accepted because it has two 0s.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma_\\varepsilon \\to \\mathcal P(Q)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S_0 = E(\\{q_0\\}), \\qquad S_{k+1} = E\\Big(\\bigcup_{q\\in S_k} \\delta(q, a_{k+1})\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N \\text{ accepts } a_1\\cdots a_n \\iff S_n \\cap F \\ne \\varnothing</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Union of regular languages via an NFA</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If NFAs <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">N_2</span> recognise <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2</span>, then the NFA with a new start state <span class=\"m\">s</span> and ε-transitions <span class=\"m\">s \\to q_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">s \\to q_2</span> (keeping all old states and arrows) recognises <span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cup L_2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Any accepting path of the new machine starts with an ε-move into <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> or <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, and from then on it is an accepting path of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> or of <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>, because the two machines share no states. Conversely, an accepting path of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> (or <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>) on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, with the extra ε-step in front, is an accepting path of the new machine. So it accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">w \\in L_1</span> or <span class=\"m\">w \\in L_2</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simulate the \"ends in 01\" NFA on <span class=\"m\">0101</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start: <span class=\"m\">S_0 = \\{q_0\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">S_1 = \\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 1: <span class=\"m\">q_0 \\to q_0</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_1 \\to q_2</span>. <span class=\"m\">S_2 = \\{q_0, q_2\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">q_0 \\to \\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> has no arrows. <span class=\"m\">S_3 = \\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 1: <span class=\"m\">S_4 = \\{q_0, q_2\\}</span>. It contains <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, so accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run the union ε-NFA on <span class=\"m\">01</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start set: <span class=\"m\">E(\\{s\\}) = \\{s, a_0, b_0\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0: <span class=\"m\">s</span> has no 0-arrow, <span class=\"m\">a_0 \\to a_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_0 \\to b_0</span>. New set <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1, b_0\\}</span> (no ε-moves to add).</li>\n<li>Read 1: <span class=\"m\">a_1 \\to a_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">b_0 \\to b_1</span>. New set <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1, b_1\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Neither <span class=\"m\">a_1</span> nor <span class=\"m\">b_1</span> is accepting, so reject. Check: <span class=\"m\">01</span> has an odd number of 0s and an odd number of 1s.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Design an NFA for strings containing <span class=\"m\">101</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>State <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> loops on 0 and 1 (wait for the pattern to start). On 1 it may also guess \"the pattern starts here\" and go to <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q_1 \\xrightarrow{0} q_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2 \\xrightarrow{1} q_3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q_3</span> is accepting and loops on 0 and 1, since anything may follow the pattern.</li>\n<li>Four states in total; wrong guesses die automatically when the next symbol does not match.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Design an NFA for binary strings whose <strong>second-to-last</strong> symbol is 1, and simulate it on <span class=\"m\">0110</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>States <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> (start), <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> (accepting). <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> loops on 0 and 1, and on 1 also moves to <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> (guess: this 1 is second to last). <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> moves to <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> on 0 or 1. <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> has no outgoing arrows.\nSimulation of <span class=\"m\">0110</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow0 \\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow1 \\{q_0,q_1\\} \\xrightarrow1 \\{q_0,q_1,q_2\\} \\xrightarrow0 \\{q_0, q_2\\}</span>. The final set contains <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, so accept. Indeed, the second-to-last symbol of <span class=\"m\">0110</span> is 1.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Regular-expression engines such as Thompson's algorithm (used in <code>grep</code>, RE2 and Go's <code>regexp</code>) compile a pattern into an NFA and simulate it with the set-of-states method. That guarantees time proportional to (pattern size) <span class=\"m\">\\times</span> (text length), avoiding the exponential blow-ups of backtracking engines.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Tokenizers and text-normalisation steps in ML pipelines use NFA-based regex matching to split and clean text. In speech recognition, decoding searches a huge nondeterministic network of word and phone states, keeping the most probable active states at each time step (beam search), a weighted cousin of NFA simulation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Think of reading a mystery novel and keeping a list of suspects. Each clue can add suspects or rule some out, and you keep the whole list rather than committing to one. At the end, the case is \"solved\" if at least one remaining suspect fits. That is exactly how an NFA keeps a set of possible states.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An NFA accepts a string when...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every computation path ends in an accepting state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">at least one computation path ends in an accepting state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the majority of paths accept</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Acceptance needs just one successful path. Rejection requires all paths to fail.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the \"ends in 01\" NFA, how many states are in the set after reading <span class=\"m\">10</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow1 \\{q_0\\} \\xrightarrow0 \\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>: two states.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What happens to a computation path that reaches a state with no arrow for the next symbol?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">that path dies, other paths continue</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the whole NFA rejects</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the NFA accepts</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A missing transition only ends that branch.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the second-to-last-is-1 NFA, how many states are active after reading <span class=\"m\">11</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\} \\to \\{q_0, q_1\\} \\to \\{q_0,q_1,q_2\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Does swapping accepting and non-accepting states of an NFA always give an NFA for the complement?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">No</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>An NFA may have both accepting and rejecting paths on the same string; after swapping, it would still accept. Convert to a DFA first, then swap.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The ε-closure <span class=\"m\">E(S)</span> always contains...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">S</span> itself</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the start state</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is the set reachable by zero or more ε-moves, and zero moves leaves you in <span class=\"m\">S</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "subset-construction": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-question\" data-h=\"3\">The question</h3>\n<p>In the last lesson NFAs looked more powerful than DFAs. They can guess, split into clones, and jump along ε-arrows. So a natural question is: are there languages an NFA can recognise but no DFA can? The answer is no, and the proof is one of the most useful constructions in computer science. It is called the <strong>subset construction</strong> (or powerset construction), and it is exactly how tools like <code>lex</code> turn the patterns you write into fast scanning code.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-detective-with-a-notebook\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a detective with a notebook</h3>\n<p>Picture a detective following a suspect through a city. At each crossroads the suspect might have turned left or right, so the detective cannot know the exact position. Instead, she writes in her notebook the <strong>set of places the suspect could be</strong>. At the next crossroads she updates the whole set at once. Her notebook entry is completely determined by the previous entry and the new clue: there is no guessing in her method at all. Our DFA will be that detective. Each of its states is one possible notebook entry, that is, one set of NFA states.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-idea-in-one-sentence\" data-h=\"3\">The idea in one sentence</h3>\n<p>When we simulated an NFA, the \"current situation\" was a set of states <span class=\"m\">S \\subseteq Q</span>, and the next set was completely determined by <span class=\"m\">S</span> and the next symbol. A machine whose situation is determined by the previous situation and the input is deterministic. Since <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is finite, there are only finitely many subsets: exactly <span class=\"m\">2^{|Q|}</span>. So we can make a DFA with one state per subset.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-construction\" data-h=\"3\">The construction</h3>\n<p>Start with an NFA <span class=\"m\">N = (Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F)</span>. Build a DFA <span class=\"m\">D</span> as follows.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>States:</strong> subsets of <span class=\"m\">Q</span>. (In practice we only build the ones we can actually reach.)</li>\n<li><strong>Start state:</strong> <span class=\"m\">E(\\{q_0\\})</span>, the ε-closure of the NFA's start state.</li>\n<li><strong>Transitions:</strong> for a subset <span class=\"m\">S</span> and symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\delta_D(S, a) = E\\big(\\bigcup_{q\\in S} \\delta(q, a)\\big)</span>: everywhere any member of <span class=\"m\">S</span> can go on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, then close under ε-moves.</li>\n<li><strong>Accepting states:</strong> every subset that contains at least one NFA accepting state, <span class=\"m\">\\{S : S \\cap F \\ne \\varnothing\\}</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Subset construction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The method that converts an NFA with state set <span class=\"m\">Q</span> into a DFA whose states are subsets of <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, whose start state is <span class=\"m\">E(\\{q_0\\})</span>, whose moves are <span class=\"m\">\\delta_D(S,a) = E(\\bigcup_{q \\in S}\\delta(q,a))</span>, and whose accepting states are the subsets containing an NFA accepting state.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"working-it-out-by-hand-ends-in-01\" data-h=\"3\">Working it out by hand: \"ends in 01\"</h3>\n<p>Recall the NFA: <span class=\"m\">q_0 \\xrightarrow{0} \\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_0 \\xrightarrow1 \\{q_0\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_1 \\xrightarrow{1} \\{q_2\\}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">q_2</span> accepting with no arrows. There are no ε-moves, so closures change nothing. We build a table, adding new subsets as they appear.</p>\n<p>Start with <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>. On 0 it goes to <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span> (new). On 1 it goes to <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>.</p>\n<p>Next row, <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>. On 0: <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> gives nothing, so <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>. On 1: <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">q_1</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\{q_2\\}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_2\\}</span> (new).</p>\n<p>Next row, <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_2\\}</span>. On 0: <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>. On 1: <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>. No new subsets appeared, so we stop.</p>\n<p>The DFA has <strong>3 states</strong> out of <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> possible subsets. Only <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_2\\}</span> contains <span class=\"m\">q_2</span>, so it is the single accepting state. Read the subsets as meanings: <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span> means \"haven't just seen a 0\", <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span> means \"just saw a 0\", and <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_2\\}</span> means \"just saw 01\". That is exactly the DFA you would have designed by hand.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-only-reachable-subsets\" data-h=\"3\">Why only reachable subsets?</h3>\n<p>Writing down all <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> subsets is wasteful. Most are never entered: for the \"ends in 01\" NFA, no reachable subset is missing <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, because <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> loops on everything. The standard algorithm is a search: put the start subset on a to-do list, and repeatedly take a subset off the list, compute its moves on every symbol, and add any subset not yet seen. This is breadth-first search over subsets, and it stops because there are finitely many.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-the-blow-up-is-real\" data-h=\"3\">When the blow-up is real</h3>\n<p>Sometimes the DFA really needs exponentially many states. Take the NFA for \"the 3rd symbol from the end is 1\": <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> loops on 0 and 1, on 1 it also guesses <span class=\"m\">q_1</span>, then <span class=\"m\">q_1 \\to q_2 \\to q_3</span> on any symbol, with <span class=\"m\">q_3</span> accepting. The NFA has 4 states. The subset construction produces <strong>8</strong> reachable DFA states: every subset that contains <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, one for each possible pattern of the last three symbols. You cannot do better: a DFA reading this language must remember the last three symbols, because any two different 3-symbol endings can be told apart by appending a suitable suffix. For \"the <span class=\"m\">n</span>th symbol from the end is 1\" the NFA has <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> states but the smallest DFA has <span class=\"m\">2^n</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>The proof is a careful version of the detective story. By induction on the length of <span class=\"m\">w</span>, the DFA state after reading <span class=\"m\">w</span> equals the set of NFA states reachable by reading <span class=\"m\">w</span>. For <span class=\"m\">w = \\varepsilon</span> both are <span class=\"m\">E(\\{q_0\\})</span>. If it holds for <span class=\"m\">w</span>, then reading one more symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span>, both sides take every state reachable from the current set on <span class=\"m\">a</span> and close under ε. So after the whole input, the DFA is in exactly the set of states the NFA could be in, and the DFA accepts iff that set contains an accepting state, which is precisely when the NFA accepts.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting the ε-closure of the start state is the number one error. The second is forgetting the empty set: if a subset has no moves on some symbol, its successor is <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span>, which becomes a dead state in the DFA. It is a real state and must be included. Third, a subset is accepting if it contains <strong>any</strong> NFA accepting state, not all of them. And finally, do not assume the result is minimal: the subset construction can produce redundant states that a minimisation step will merge.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The subset construction turns any NFA with <span class=\"m\">n</span> states into an equivalent DFA whose states are sets of NFA states, with at most <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> of them. The start state is the ε-closure of <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, transitions take all possible moves then close under ε, and accepting subsets are those containing an accepting NFA state. So NFAs and DFAs recognise exactly the same class of languages, the regular languages. The price of determinism can be an exponential number of states, and sometimes that price is unavoidable.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;subset&quot;, &quot;machine&quot;: &quot;ends in 01&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1101}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The table is the DFA built by the subset construction. The highlighted row is where the DFA ends on your string.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Check each row of the table against the hand computation in the article.\n2. Switch the NFA to \"3rd symbol from the end is 1\". Read off how many DFA states are reachable, and compare with <span class=\"m\">2^4</span>.\n3. Switch to \"contains 101\" and count the accepting rows.\n4. Switch to the ε-NFA and look at the start row: which states are in it?</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> for \"ends in 01\" only 3 of the 8 subsets are reachable. For \"3rd from the end\" there are 8 reachable states, every subset containing <span class=\"m\">q_0</span>, which is the real exponential blow-up. For \"contains 101\" three rows are accepting, and they all behave the same way, a hint that minimisation could merge them. The ε-NFA's start row is <span class=\"m\">\\{s, a_0, b_0\\}</span>, the ε-closure.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">Q_D \\subseteq \\mathcal P(Q), \\qquad |Q_D| \\le 2^{|Q|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">q_D = E(\\{q_0\\}), \\qquad \\delta_D(S,a) = E\\Big(\\bigcup_{q\\in S}\\delta(q,a)\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F_D = \\{S \\subseteq Q : S\\cap F \\ne\\varnothing\\}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">NFAs and DFAs are equivalent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every NFA <span class=\"m\">N</span> there is a DFA <span class=\"m\">D</span> with <span class=\"m\">L(D) = L(N)</span>. Hence a language is regular if and only if some NFA recognises it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Build <span class=\"m\">D</span> by the subset construction. Claim: for every string <span class=\"m\">w</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta_D(q_D, w)</span> equals the set <span class=\"m\">R(w)</span> of NFA states reachable from <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> while reading <span class=\"m\">w</span>. Base case: <span class=\"m\">R(\\varepsilon) = E(\\{q_0\\}) = q_D</span>. Step: <span class=\"m\">R(wa) = E(\\bigcup_{q \\in R(w)} \\delta(q,a)) = \\delta_D(R(w), a)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">D</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> iff <span class=\"m\">R(w) \\cap F \\ne \\varnothing</span> iff some path of <span class=\"m\">N</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> ends in <span class=\"m\">F</span> iff <span class=\"m\">N</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>. The other direction is trivial: every DFA is already an NFA.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The blow-up can be necessary</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The language \"the <span class=\"m\">n</span>th symbol from the end is 1\" has an NFA with <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> states, but every DFA for it has at least <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> states.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose a DFA has fewer than <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> states. There are <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> binary strings of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, so by the pigeonhole principle two different ones, <span class=\"m\">x \\ne y</span>, end in the same state. They differ in some position <span class=\"m\">i</span> (counting from 1 on the left); say <span class=\"m\">x</span> has 1 there and <span class=\"m\">y</span> has 0. Append <span class=\"m\">z = 0^{i-1}</span>. Now the symbol at position <span class=\"m\">i</span> is exactly <span class=\"m\">n</span>th from the end in both <span class=\"m\">xz</span> and <span class=\"m\">yz</span>, so <span class=\"m\">xz</span> is in the language and <span class=\"m\">yz</span> is not. But the DFA is in the same state after <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>, so it is also in the same state after <span class=\"m\">xz</span> and <span class=\"m\">yz</span>, and gives the same answer. Contradiction.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convert the \"contains 101\" NFA to a DFA</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>NFA: <span class=\"m\">q_0 \\xrightarrow0 \\{q_0\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_0\\xrightarrow1\\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_1 \\xrightarrow0 \\{q_2\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_2 \\xrightarrow1 \\{q_3\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">q_3 \\xrightarrow{0,1} \\{q_3\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_2\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_1\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_2\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1,q_3\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1,q_3\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_2,q_3\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1,q_3\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_2,q_3\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_3\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1,q_3\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_3\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0, q_3\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{q_0,q_1,q_3\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Result: 6 reachable states, 3 of them accepting (those containing <span class=\"m\">q_3</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Subset construction for the union ε-NFA (even 0s or even 1s)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start: <span class=\"m\">E(\\{s\\}) = \\{s, a_0, b_0\\}</span>, accepting.</li>\n<li>From the start: on 0 go to <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1, b_0\\}</span>, on 1 go to <span class=\"m\">\\{a_0, b_1\\}</span>. Both accepting (each contains <span class=\"m\">a_0</span> or <span class=\"m\">b_0</span>).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{a_1,b_0\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{a_0,b_0\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1,b_1\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">\\{a_0,b_1\\}</span>: on 0 <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1,b_1\\}</span>, on 1 <span class=\"m\">\\{a_0,b_0\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{a_0,b_0\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1,b_1\\}</span> produce no new subsets.</li>\n<li>Total: 5 states; only <span class=\"m\">\\{a_1, b_1\\}</span> (both parities odd) rejects.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An NFA over <span class=\"m\">\\{a,b\\}</span> has states <span class=\"m\">p</span> (start), <span class=\"m\">r</span> (accepting) with transitions <span class=\"m\">p \\xrightarrow{a} \\{p, r\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">p \\xrightarrow b \\{p\\}</span>, and no transitions out of <span class=\"m\">r</span>. Apply the subset construction, give the DFA table, and describe the language.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Start <span class=\"m\">\\{p\\}</span>: on <span class=\"m\">a</span> go to <span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span>, on <span class=\"m\">b</span> go to <span class=\"m\">\\{p\\}</span>.\n<span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span>: on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">p</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">r</span> gives nothing, so <span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span>; on <span class=\"m\">b</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{p\\}</span>.\nTwo DFA states; <span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span> is accepting. The DFA is in <span class=\"m\">\\{p,r\\}</span> exactly when the last symbol read was <span class=\"m\">a</span>, so the language is \"strings ending in <span class=\"m\">a</span>\".</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lexer generators (<code>lex</code>, <code>flex</code>, and the scanners in many compilers) translate each token's regular expression into an NFA, combine them with ε-arrows, and run the subset construction once at build time. The resulting DFA scans source code in one pass with a single table lookup per character.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Constrained decoding for language models (forcing output to match a JSON schema or a regex) compiles the constraint into an automaton, often determinised with the subset construction, and masks out tokens that would leave the DFA in a dead state.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>When you track a package that \"could be at any of these three depots\", each new scan updates the whole set of possibilities. Logistics software that keeps a set of possible locations and updates it deterministically is performing the subset construction in real time.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An NFA has 5 states. What is the maximum number of states the subset construction can produce?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"32\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One DFA state per subset: <span class=\"m\">2^5 = 32</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the subset construction, which DFA states are accepting?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">subsets containing only accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">subsets containing at least one accepting state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the full set <span class=\"m\">Q</span> only</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The NFA accepts if some path ends in <span class=\"m\">F</span>, i.e. if the current set meets <span class=\"m\">F</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many reachable DFA states does the subset construction give for the \"ends in 01\" NFA?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}, \\{q_0,q_1\\}, \\{q_0,q_2\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the DFA start state when the NFA has ε-moves?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{q_0\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">E(\\{q_0\\})</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The NFA can follow ε-arrows before reading anything, so the start set must be closed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The smallest DFA for \"the 4th symbol from the end is 1\" has how many states?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^n</span> with <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>. The NFA needs only 5.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If a subset has no moves on symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span>, its <span class=\"m\">a</span>-successor in the DFA is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the empty set, which acts as a dead state</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">undefined, so the DFA is incomplete</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the start state</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is a legitimate DFA state that loops to itself and rejects.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "regular-expressions": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"a-shorter-way-to-say-it\" data-h=\"3\">A shorter way to say it</h3>\n<p>Drawing a state diagram is a fine way to describe \"strings that end in 01\", but it is slow to write and hard to type into a search box. When you type a search pattern into a text editor, or validate an email address in a web form, you use a much shorter notation: a <strong>regular expression</strong>. In a regular expression, <span class=\"m\">(0 \\cup 1)^*01</span> says \"anything, followed by 01\" in eight characters. Regular expressions are an algebra for languages, the way ordinary algebra is a notation for numbers.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-describing-house-numbers\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine describing house numbers</h3>\n<p>Imagine telling a friend which house numbers on a street are valid: \"a nonzero digit, followed by any number of digits, optionally followed by a single letter\". You built the description from three ideas: <strong>choose</strong> one of several options, <strong>follow</strong> one thing with another, and <strong>repeat</strong> something any number of times. Those three ideas, union, concatenation and star, are the whole of regular expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>Regular expressions over an alphabet <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> are built up from small pieces, and each one describes a language <span class=\"m\">L(R)</span>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is a regular expression describing the empty language.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is a regular expression describing <span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Each symbol <span class=\"m\">a \\in \\Sigma</span> is a regular expression describing <span class=\"m\">\\{a\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">R_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">R_2</span> are regular expressions, so are <span class=\"m\">(R_1 \\cup R_2)</span>, describing <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2)</span>; <span class=\"m\">(R_1R_2)</span>, describing the concatenation <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)L(R_2)</span>; and <span class=\"m\">(R_1^*)</span>, describing <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)^*</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Nothing else is a regular expression. Many books write union as <span class=\"m\">+</span> (so <span class=\"m\">0+1</span>) and programming languages write it as <code>|</code> (so <code>0|1</code>); they all mean the same thing. The interactive visual on this page accepts all three.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Regular expression</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An expression built from <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> and single symbols using union <span class=\"m\">\\cup</span>, concatenation and Kleene star <span class=\"m\">^*</span>. It describes the language obtained by applying the corresponding set operations.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"precedence\" data-h=\"3\">Precedence</h3>\n<p>Just as multiplication binds tighter than addition in <span class=\"m\">2 + 3 \\times 4</span>, regular expressions have an order of operations: <strong>star first, then concatenation, then union</strong>. So <span class=\"m\">01^* \\cup 1</span> means <span class=\"m\">(0(1^*)) \\cup 1</span>: a 0 followed by any number of 1s, or the single string 1. If you want \"any number of copies of 01\", you need brackets: <span class=\"m\">(01)^*</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-expressions\" data-h=\"3\">Reading expressions</h3>\n<p>Let us read some examples over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span>, writing <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> for <span class=\"m\">(0\\cup1)</span>.</p>\n<p><span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span> is \"some 0s, then some 1s\": <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon, 0, 1, 00, 01, 11, 001, \\dots</span> but not <span class=\"m\">10</span>. Of length 3 there are exactly 4 such strings: <span class=\"m\">000, 001, 011, 111</span>. In general a length-<span class=\"m\">n</span> string of this form is decided by where the 1s start, so there are <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> of them.</p>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*01</span> is \"ends in 01\". <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*11\\Sigma^*</span> is \"contains 11\". <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*1\\Sigma\\Sigma</span> is \"the 3rd symbol from the end is 1\". <span class=\"m\">(\\Sigma\\Sigma)^*</span> is \"even length\". And <span class=\"m\">1^*(01^*01^*)^*</span> is \"an even number of 0s\": the 0s come in pairs, each pair possibly separated and followed by 1s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"writing-expressions\" data-h=\"3\">Writing expressions</h3>\n<p>To write a regular expression for a language, describe its strings as a pattern of choices, sequences and repetitions. \"Strings that start and end with the same symbol\" over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span>: either it is a single symbol, or it starts with 0 and ends with 0, or it starts with 1 and ends with 1. That gives\n<span class=\"mb\">0 \\cup 1 \\cup 0\\Sigma^*0 \\cup 1\\Sigma^*1.</span>\n\"Strings with no two consecutive 1s\": every 1 except possibly the last must be followed by a 0, so think in blocks of <span class=\"m\">0</span> and <span class=\"m\">10</span>, with an optional 1 at the end: <span class=\"m\">(0 \\cup 10)^*(\\varepsilon \\cup 1)</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">0101</span>: it is <span class=\"m\">0 \\cdot 10 \\cdot 1</span>, matching. Check <span class=\"m\">0110</span>: after the block <span class=\"m\">0</span>, the next block would have to start with <span class=\"m\">1</span> and then need a <span class=\"m\">0</span>, but the next symbol is another 1. No match, as required.</p>\n<h3 id=\"useful-identities\" data-h=\"3\">Useful identities</h3>\n<p>Regular expressions obey algebraic laws. Union is commutative and associative, and concatenation distributes over union: <span class=\"m\">R(S\\cup T) = RS \\cup RT</span>. The empty set and empty string behave like 0 and 1: <span class=\"m\">R \\cup \\varnothing = R</span>, <span class=\"m\">R\\varepsilon = \\varepsilon R = R</span>, and <span class=\"m\">R\\varnothing = \\varnothing</span>. For stars, <span class=\"m\">(R^*)^* = R^*</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing^* = \\varepsilon</span>, and <span class=\"m\">R^+ = RR^*</span> (one or more copies). These laws let you simplify expressions, and they are the same facts about languages you met in the first lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-regular-expressions-matter\" data-h=\"3\">Why regular expressions matter</h3>\n<p>Regular expressions are the human-friendly language; DFAs are the machine-friendly one. In the next lesson, <strong>Kleene's theorem</strong> shows that they describe exactly the same languages: every regular expression can be converted to an NFA (and then to a DFA), and every DFA can be converted back to a regular expression. So \"regular language\" can mean any of three equivalent things.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Watch precedence: <span class=\"m\">ab^*</span> is not <span class=\"m\">(ab)^*</span>. Do not confuse <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> matches nothing, while <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> matches only the empty string. Remember that <span class=\"m\">R^*</span> always includes <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, so <span class=\"m\">0^*</span> matches the empty string. Be careful with programming-language regexes: tools like Perl, Python and JavaScript add <strong>backreferences</strong> such as <code>(a*)b\\1</code>, which can describe languages that are <em>not</em> regular. In this course, \"regular expression\" means only the three operations. Finally, when testing your expression, check both strings that should match and strings that should not; most wrong expressions accept too much.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A regular expression is built from <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> and single symbols with union, concatenation and star, with star binding tightest and union loosest. Each expression describes a language. Read expressions by turning them into words (\"some 0s then some 1s\"), write them by breaking a language into choices, sequences and repetitions, and simplify them with algebraic identities. They describe exactly the regular languages.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;regex&quot;, &quot;regex&quot;: &quot;0*1*&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0011&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Type any regular expression over 0 and 1. The table lists every matching string up to length 5.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <code>0*1*</code>, check that the counts for lengths 0 to 5 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.\n2. Type <code>1*(01*01*)*</code> and confirm every listed string has an even number of 0s.\n3. Compare <code>01*</code> with <code>(01)*</code>, and <code>0+1*</code> with <code>(0+1)*</code>.\n4. Type <code>(0+10)*(ε+1)</code> and test the strings <span class=\"m\">0101</span> and <span class=\"m\">0110</span> in the String box.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the count for <code>0*1*</code> is <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> at length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, as argued in the article. The pairs in step 3 show precedence: star binds before concatenation and union, so brackets change the language completely. Step 4 confirms the \"no two consecutive 1s\" expression: <span class=\"m\">0101</span> matches and <span class=\"m\">0110</span> does not.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">L(R_1\\cup R_2) = L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2), \\quad L(R_1R_2) = L(R_1)L(R_2), \\quad L(R^*) = L(R)^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{precedence: } {}^* \\ &gt; \\ \\text{concatenation} \\ &gt; \\ \\cup</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R\\cup\\varnothing = R,\\quad R\\varepsilon = R,\\quad R\\varnothing = \\varnothing,\\quad \\varnothing^* = \\varepsilon,\\quad (R^*)^* = R^*,\\quad R^+ = RR^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R(S\\cup T) = RS\\cup RT, \\qquad (S\\cup T)R = SR\\cup TR</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Every finite language is regular</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L = \\{w_1, \\dots, w_k\\}</span> is finite, then <span class=\"m\">R = w_1 \\cup w_2 \\cup \\cdots \\cup w_k</span> is a regular expression for <span class=\"m\">L</span> (with <span class=\"m\">R = \\varnothing</span> when <span class=\"m\">k = 0</span>).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Each string <span class=\"m\">w_i = a_1\\cdots a_m</span> is the concatenation of single-symbol expressions, which describes exactly <span class=\"m\">\\{w_i\\}</span> (and <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> describes <span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>). The union of these <span class=\"m\">k</span> expressions describes <span class=\"m\">\\{w_1\\}\\cup\\cdots\\cup\\{w_k\\} = L</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write a regular expression for binary strings of length at least 2 that start and end with the same symbol</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Split by the first symbol: either it is 0 or it is 1.</li>\n<li>If it starts with 0 it must also end with 0, with anything in between: <span class=\"m\">0\\Sigma^*0</span>.</li>\n<li>Similarly <span class=\"m\">1\\Sigma^*1</span>.</li>\n<li>Combine: <span class=\"m\">0(0\\cup1)^*0 \\ \\cup\\ 1(0\\cup1)^*1</span>. (Add <span class=\"m\">0 \\cup 1</span> if single symbols should count.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many strings of length 4 match <span class=\"m\">(0\\cup1)^*1(0\\cup1)</span>?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The pattern says the second-to-last symbol is 1, and the others are free.</li>\n<li>Length 4 has positions 1 to 4; position 3 is fixed to 1.</li>\n<li>Positions 1, 2 and 4 are free: <span class=\"m\">2^3 = 8</span> strings.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simplify <span class=\"m\">(\\varepsilon \\cup 0)0^*\\ \\cup\\ \\varnothing 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing 1 = \\varnothing</span>, and <span class=\"m\">R\\cup\\varnothing = R</span>, so the second part vanishes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">(\\varepsilon\\cup0)0^* = \\varepsilon0^* \\cup 00^* = 0^* \\cup 0^+</span>.</li>\n<li>Every string in <span class=\"m\">0^+</span> is already in <span class=\"m\">0^*</span>, so the answer is <span class=\"m\">0^*</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Give a regular expression for binary strings containing <strong>exactly two</strong> 1s, and list all such strings of length 4.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Any number of 0s, a 1, any number of 0s, a 1, any number of 0s: <span class=\"m\">0^*10^*10^*</span>.\nLength 4 strings with exactly two 1s: choose 2 of 4 positions, <span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span>: <span class=\"m\">1100, 1010, 1001, 0110, 0101, 0011</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Regular expressions are everywhere in software: <code>grep</code> and text editors for search, form validation, log parsing, URL routing in web frameworks, and the token definitions of every programming language. Knowing that true regular expressions compile to DFAs explains why they can be matched in linear time, while engines with backreferences can take exponential time on unlucky inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Data cleaning for ML relies heavily on regexes: stripping HTML, extracting numbers and dates, normalising whitespace, and <strong>pre-tokenisation</strong> in GPT-style tokenizers, which use a regular expression to split text into chunks before byte-pair encoding.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A UK postcode, a phone number like <code>+44 20 7946 0958</code>, or a product code like <code>AB-1234</code> all have fixed shapes made of choices, sequences and repetitions. Websites check them with regular expressions before you can press Submit.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which strings does <span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span> match?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">10</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">0011</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0101</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Some 0s and then some 1s. <span class=\"m\">10</span> and <span class=\"m\">0101</span> have a 0 after a 1.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many strings of length 3 match <span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">000, 001, 011, 111</span>: <span class=\"m\">n + 1 = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In <span class=\"m\">01^* \\cup 1</span>, which operation is applied first?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the star</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the concatenation</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the union</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Star binds tightest, then concatenation, then union.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What language does <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing^*</span> describe?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">all strings</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Zero copies of anything is <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, and there is nothing else to glue.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many strings of length 5 match <span class=\"m\">(0\\cup1)^*1</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The last symbol is fixed as 1; the other four are free: <span class=\"m\">2^4 = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which expression describes binary strings of even length?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(0\\cup1)^*(0\\cup1)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">((0\\cup1)(0\\cup1))^*</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Gluing any number of length-2 blocks gives exactly the even lengths, including 0.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "kleene-theorem": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"three-descriptions-one-idea\" data-h=\"3\">Three descriptions, one idea</h3>\n<p>We now have three ways to describe a language: a DFA, an NFA, and a regular expression. We already proved that DFAs and NFAs are equally powerful. <strong>Kleene's theorem</strong>, proved by Stephen Kleene in 1956, closes the circle: a language can be described by a regular expression if and only if some finite automaton recognises it. This is a little like learning that a map, a list of directions, and a GPS route all describe the same journey. Each is convenient for something different, and you can always translate between them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-we-care\" data-h=\"3\">Why we care</h3>\n<p>The theorem is practical. People like writing regular expressions, because they are short. Computers like running DFAs, because they are fast. Kleene's theorem guarantees that every expression a person writes can be compiled into a machine, and that every machine can be described back as an expression. It also gives us powerful <strong>closure properties</strong>: ways of combining regular languages that always produce regular languages.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kleene's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language is regular (recognised by some DFA or NFA) if and only if it is described by some regular expression.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"direction-1-regular-expression-to-nfa\" data-h=\"3\">Direction 1: regular expression to NFA</h3>\n<p>We build an NFA by following the structure of the expression, one piece at a time. This is called <strong>Thompson's construction</strong>. For the base cases: <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is an NFA with one non-accepting start state and no arrows; <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is a single state that is both start and accepting; a symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span> is two states with one arrow labelled <span class=\"m\">a</span> from the start to the accepting state.</p>\n<p>For the three operations, assume we already have NFAs <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Union</strong> <span class=\"m\">R_1 \\cup R_2</span>: add a new start state with ε-arrows to the start states of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>. The machine guesses which branch to follow.</li>\n<li><strong>Concatenation</strong> <span class=\"m\">R_1R_2</span>: add ε-arrows from every accepting state of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> to the start of <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>, and make only <span class=\"m\">N_2</span>'s accepting states accept. The machine guesses where the first part ends.</li>\n<li><strong>Star</strong> <span class=\"m\">R_1^*</span>: add a new start state that is also accepting (so <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is accepted), with an ε-arrow to <span class=\"m\">N_1</span>'s start, and ε-arrows from <span class=\"m\">N_1</span>'s accepting states back to <span class=\"m\">N_1</span>'s start, so the machine can go round again.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each step is small, and the NFA grows by only a constant number of states per symbol or operator. Let us count for <span class=\"m\">(0\\cup1)^*01</span> with these rules. The NFAs for <span class=\"m\">0</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span> have 2 states each; their union adds one start state, giving 5; the star adds one more, giving 6; the final <span class=\"m\">0</span> and <span class=\"m\">1</span> add 2 states each, giving <span class=\"m\">6 + 2 + 2 = 10</span> states. The result is bigger than the 3-state NFA you would design by hand, but it is produced mechanically, and that is the point.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;nfa&quot;, &quot;machine&quot;: &quot;ends in 01&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1101}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A hand-made 3-state NFA for (0∪1)*01. Thompson's construction gives a larger but equivalent machine.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"direction-2-dfa-to-regular-expression\" data-h=\"3\">Direction 2: DFA to regular expression</h3>\n<p>The reverse direction uses <strong>state elimination</strong>. First turn the DFA into a <em>generalised</em> NFA, where arrows may be labelled by regular expressions instead of single symbols. Add a new start state with an ε-arrow to the old start, and a new single accepting state with ε-arrows from every old accepting state. Then remove the original states one at a time. When you remove a state <span class=\"m\">q</span> that has an incoming arrow from <span class=\"m\">p</span> labelled <span class=\"m\">R_1</span>, a self-loop labelled <span class=\"m\">R_2</span>, and an outgoing arrow to <span class=\"m\">r</span> labelled <span class=\"m\">R_3</span>, you replace the path <span class=\"m\">p \\to q \\to r</span> by a direct arrow\n<span class=\"mb\">p \\xrightarrow{R_1R_2^*R_3} r,</span>\ncombining it with union if an arrow from <span class=\"m\">p</span> to <span class=\"m\">r</span> already existed. When only the new start and the new accepting state are left, the label on the arrow between them is the answer.</p>\n<p>Here is a small example. The DFA for \"even number of 1s\" has states <span class=\"m\">A</span> (start, accepting) and <span class=\"m\">B</span>, with <span class=\"m\">A \\xrightarrow0 A</span>, <span class=\"m\">A \\xrightarrow1 B</span>, <span class=\"m\">B \\xrightarrow0 B</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\xrightarrow1 A</span>. Eliminate <span class=\"m\">B</span>: there is a path <span class=\"m\">A \\xrightarrow{1} B \\xrightarrow{1} A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> has a self-loop labelled <span class=\"m\">0</span>, so we get a new loop on <span class=\"m\">A</span> labelled <span class=\"m\">10^*1</span>. Combined with the existing loop, <span class=\"m\">A</span> now loops on <span class=\"m\">0 \\cup 10^*1</span>. Eliminating <span class=\"m\">A</span> between the new start and the new accepting state gives the regular expression\n<span class=\"mb\">(0 \\cup 10^*1)^*.</span>\nRead it aloud: repeatedly, either a 0, or a 1 followed by some 0s and another 1. The 1s come in pairs, so their number is even.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closure-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Closure properties</h3>\n<p>A class of languages is <strong>closed</strong> under an operation if applying the operation to members of the class always gives a member. Regular languages are closed under many operations, and Kleene's theorem lets us prove it in whichever description is easiest.</p>\n<p><strong>Union, concatenation and star</strong> are immediate from regular expressions: if <span class=\"m\">L_1 = L(R_1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2 = L(R_2)</span> then <span class=\"m\">R_1\\cup R_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">R_1R_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">R_1^*</span> describe the results.</p>\n<p><strong>Complement</strong> is easy with a DFA: swap accepting and non-accepting states. Every string ends in exactly one state, so it is now accepted exactly when it was rejected before. (This needs a <em>complete DFA</em> with a dead state; swapping on an NFA does not work.)</p>\n<p><strong>Intersection</strong> follows from De Morgan's law, <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cap L_2 = \\overline{\\overline{L_1}\\cup\\overline{L_2}}</span>, or directly with the <strong>product construction</strong>: run both DFAs at once, using pairs of states <span class=\"m\">(p, q)</span>, and accept when both components accept. Two DFAs with 2 and 3 states give a product with <span class=\"m\">2\\times3 = 6</span> states. <strong>Reversal</strong> is also regular: reverse every arrow of an NFA and swap the roles of start and accepting states.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not complement an NFA by swapping states: an NFA rejects only when <em>every</em> path rejects, so swapping does not flip its answers. Do not forget the dead state when complementing a DFA with missing arrows. When eliminating states, remember to handle <em>every</em> pair of incoming and outgoing arrows, including loops on the remaining states. And note that the expression you get from elimination depends on the order you remove states; different orders give different-looking but equivalent expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Kleene's theorem says regular expressions and finite automata describe exactly the same languages. Thompson's construction turns an expression into an ε-NFA piece by piece, and state elimination turns a DFA back into an expression using the rule <span class=\"m\">R_1R_2^*R_3</span>. As a result, regular languages are closed under union, concatenation, star, complement, intersection and reversal, which gives us a toolkit for building complicated regular languages from simple ones.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;regex&quot;, &quot;regex&quot;: &quot;(0+10*1)*&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1001}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The expression obtained by state elimination. Every listed string should have an even number of 1s.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Scan the listed strings and count the 1s in each: are they all even?\n2. Compare the counts per length with <span class=\"m\">2^{n-1}</span> (half of all strings of length <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 1</span>).\n3. Edit the expression to <code>(1+01*0)*</code> and decide which DFA it came from.\n4. Type <code>1001</code> and <code>1011</code> in the String box.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every match has an even number of 1s, and for <span class=\"m\">n \\ge 1</span> exactly half of the <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> strings match, because flipping the last bit changes the parity. The expression in step 3 is the same idea with the roles of 0 and 1 swapped: an even number of 0s. <span class=\"m\">1001</span> matches and <span class=\"m\">1011</span> does not, exactly as the DFA in the article would decide.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"dfa\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;even # of 1s&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The DFA we eliminated. Feed it the same strings and compare with the regular expression.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Type <code>1001</code>, then <code>1011</code>.\n2. Find the path <span class=\"m\">A \\to B \\to \\cdots \\to A</span> that the expression <span class=\"m\">10^*1</span> describes.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each return to the start state corresponds to one round of the star in <span class=\"m\">(0\\cup10^*1)^*</span>: either a single 0 loop, or a trip out on a 1, some 0 loops at the other state, and back on a 1.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{state elimination: } p \\xrightarrow{R_1} q \\xrightarrow{R_2 \\text{ (loop)}} q \\xrightarrow{R_3} r \\ \\leadsto\\ p \\xrightarrow{R_1R_2^*R_3 \\,\\cup\\, R_4} r</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{complement: } F' = Q\\setminus F \\quad(\\text{complete DFA})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{product: } Q = Q_1\\times Q_2,\\ \\ \\delta((p,q),a) = (\\delta_1(p,a),\\delta_2(q,a)),\\ \\ F = F_1\\times F_2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1\\cap L_2 = \\overline{\\overline{L_1}\\cup\\overline{L_2}}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Kleene's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every regular expression <span class=\"m\">R</span> there is an NFA <span class=\"m\">N</span> with <span class=\"m\">L(N) = L(R)</span>, and for every DFA <span class=\"m\">D</span> there is a regular expression <span class=\"m\">R</span> with <span class=\"m\">L(R) = L(D)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(<span class=\"m\">R \\Rightarrow N</span>) Induction on the structure of <span class=\"m\">R</span>. The base cases <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">a</span> have the small NFAs described in the article. If <span class=\"m\">N_1, N_2</span> recognise <span class=\"m\">L(R_1), L(R_2)</span>, the union, concatenation and star constructions recognise <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)L(R_2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">L(R_1)^*</span>: an accepting path of the new machine is exactly an accepting path of one machine (union), an accepting path of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> followed by one of <span class=\"m\">N_2</span> (concatenation), or zero or more accepting paths of <span class=\"m\">N_1</span> joined end to end (star).</p>\n<p>(<span class=\"m\">D \\Rightarrow R</span>) Convert <span class=\"m\">D</span> to a generalised NFA with a fresh start and a fresh single accepting state. Eliminating a state <span class=\"m\">q</span> with the rule <span class=\"m\">R_1R_2^*R_3</span> preserves the language: any path through <span class=\"m\">q</span> enters on <span class=\"m\">R_1</span>, loops some number of times on <span class=\"m\">R_2</span>, and leaves on <span class=\"m\">R_3</span>. After all original states are gone, one arrow remains, and its label describes exactly the strings on accepting paths of <span class=\"m\">D</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Closure of regular languages</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> are regular, so are <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cup L_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1L_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1^*</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\overline{L_1}</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cap L_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_1^R</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Build the product DFA for \"even number of 0s AND ends in 1\"</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>DFA 1 tracks the parity of 0s: states <span class=\"m\">E, O</span> (start <span class=\"m\">E</span>, accept <span class=\"m\">E</span>).</li>\n<li>DFA 2 tracks the last symbol: states <span class=\"m\">N</span> (\"not ending in 1\", start) and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> (\"ends in 1\", accept).</li>\n<li>Product states: <span class=\"m\">(E,N), (E,Y), (O,N), (O,Y)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">2 \\times 2 = 4</span> states.</li>\n<li>On 0: parity flips and the second component goes to <span class=\"m\">N</span>. On 1: parity stays and the second component goes to <span class=\"m\">Y</span>.</li>\n<li>Accepting: only <span class=\"m\">(E, Y)</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">011</span>: <span class=\"m\">(E,N)\\xrightarrow0(O,N)\\xrightarrow1(O,Y)\\xrightarrow1(O,Y)</span>, reject (one 0). Check <span class=\"m\">0011</span>: ends in <span class=\"m\">(E,Y)</span>, accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count Thompson's construction states for <span class=\"m\">(ab\\cup b)^*</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">b</span>, <span class=\"m\">b</span>: 2 states each, 6 in total.</li>\n<li>Concatenation <span class=\"m\">ab</span> joins two NFAs with an ε-arrow, adding no states: 4 states.</li>\n<li>Union with <span class=\"m\">b</span> adds a new start: <span class=\"m\">4 + 2 + 1 = 7</span>.</li>\n<li>Star adds a new accepting start: <span class=\"m\">8</span> states.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A DFA over <span class=\"m\">\\{a,b\\}</span> has states <span class=\"m\">1</span> (start) and <span class=\"m\">2</span> (accepting), with <span class=\"m\">1\\xrightarrow{a}2</span>, <span class=\"m\">1\\xrightarrow{b}1</span>, <span class=\"m\">2\\xrightarrow{a}2</span>, <span class=\"m\">2\\xrightarrow{b}1</span>. Use state elimination to find a regular expression for its language, and describe the language in words.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Add new start <span class=\"m\">s \\xrightarrow\\varepsilon 1</span> and new accept <span class=\"m\">2 \\xrightarrow\\varepsilon f</span>.\nEliminate state 2: it is entered from 1 on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, loops on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, and leaves to 1 on <span class=\"m\">b</span> and to <span class=\"m\">f</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>. New arrows: <span class=\"m\">1 \\xrightarrow{aa^*b} 1</span> (combined with the loop <span class=\"m\">b</span>: <span class=\"m\">1</span> loops on <span class=\"m\">b\\cup aa^*b</span>) and <span class=\"m\">1\\xrightarrow{aa^*} f</span>.\nEliminate state 1: <span class=\"m\">s \\xrightarrow{(b\\cup aa^*b)^*aa^*} f</span>.\nAnswer: <span class=\"m\">(b\\cup aa^*b)^*aa^*</span>, which is the set of strings ending in <span class=\"m\">a</span>. (Equivalently <span class=\"m\">(a\\cup b)^*a</span>.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compilers and search tools use both directions of Kleene's theorem: Thompson's construction compiles patterns to NFAs (as in RE2 and many <code>grep</code> implementations), and closure under intersection and complement lets model checkers combine \"the system's behaviour\" with \"the bad behaviours\" and check whether the intersection is empty.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weighted finite-state transducers, built using the same union, concatenation, star and product constructions, were the backbone of classical speech recognition: separate automata for pronunciations, words and grammar were composed into one large machine and searched for the best path.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A spam filter rule like \"subject contains <em>free</em> AND body does not contain <em>unsubscribe</em>\" combines regular languages using intersection and complement. Closure properties guarantee that the combined rule can still be checked by a single fast automaton.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Kleene's theorem states that regular expressions describe exactly the languages recognised by...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">finite automata</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">pushdown automata</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Turing machines</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Regular expressions, DFAs and NFAs are three equivalent descriptions of the regular languages.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In state elimination, a path <span class=\"m\">p\\xrightarrow{a}q\\xrightarrow{b\\text{ loop}}q\\xrightarrow{c}r</span> becomes which label?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">abc</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">ab^*c</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(abc)^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Enter on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, loop any number of times on <span class=\"m\">b</span>, leave on <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The product of a 3-state DFA and a 4-state DFA has how many states?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Pairs of states: <span class=\"m\">3 \\times 4 = 12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To complement a language using an automaton, you can swap accepting and non-accepting states of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a complete DFA</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">any NFA</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">either, it does not matter</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>In an NFA, swapping does not flip the answer, since acceptance means \"some path accepts\".</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using the counting rules in the article (2 states per symbol, +1 for union, +1 for star, +0 for concatenation), how many states does Thompson's construction give for <span class=\"m\">a^*</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>2 for <span class=\"m\">a</span>, plus 1 new accepting start state for the star.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> is regular and <span class=\"m\">L_2</span> is regular, which of these is NOT guaranteed by the closure properties in this lesson?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">L_1 \\cap L_2</span> is regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\overline{L_1}</span> is regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">every subset of <span class=\"m\">L_1</span> is regular</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Subsets of regular languages can be non-regular: <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> is a subset of the regular language <span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "pumping-lemma-regular": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"some-things-a-finite-machine-cannot-do\" data-h=\"3\">Some things a finite machine cannot do</h3>\n<p>DFAs are powerful, but they have a strict limit: a fixed, finite number of states. Think of a bouncer who must check that the number of people who have entered a club equals the number who have left, but who can only remember one of five hand signals. After six people have come in, he has run out of signals and can no longer tell exactly how many are inside. A DFA trying to recognise <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n : n \\ge 0\\}</span> (some 0s followed by <em>the same number</em> of 1s) is in the same position. It would need to count the 0s with no upper limit, and finite memory cannot do that.</p>\n<p>The <strong>pumping lemma</strong> turns this intuition into a proof technique. It gives a property that every regular language must have. If a language lacks the property, it cannot be regular.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-key-observation-long-paths-must-loop\" data-h=\"3\">The key observation: long paths must loop</h3>\n<p>Suppose a DFA has <span class=\"m\">p</span> states, and you feed it a string <span class=\"m\">w</span> of length at least <span class=\"m\">p</span>. While reading the first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols, the machine visits <span class=\"m\">p+1</span> states (counting the start). There are only <span class=\"m\">p</span> different states, so by the <strong>pigeonhole principle</strong> some state appears twice. The part of the string read between the two visits takes the machine around a loop and back to the same state.</p>\n<p>Call the part before the loop <span class=\"m\">x</span>, the loop itself <span class=\"m\">y</span>, and the rest <span class=\"m\">z</span>, so <span class=\"m\">w = xyz</span>. Because the machine is back in the same state after <span class=\"m\">xy</span> as after <span class=\"m\">x</span>, it does not \"know\" whether it went around the loop. So it can go around the loop zero times, twice, or a hundred times, and still end in the same final state. If <span class=\"m\">w</span> was accepted, so is <span class=\"m\">xz</span>, <span class=\"m\">xyyz</span>, <span class=\"m\">xyyyz</span>, and so on.</p>\n<p>For instance, the 3-state DFA for \"binary number divisible by 3\" reads <span class=\"m\">110</span> (six) through remainders <span class=\"m\">0 \\to 1 \\to 0 \\to 0</span>. Remainder <span class=\"m\">0</span> repeats after reading <span class=\"m\">11</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = \\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 11</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = 0</span>. Pumping gives <span class=\"m\">0</span> (zero), <span class=\"m\">11110</span> (thirty) and <span class=\"m\">1111110</span> (one hundred and twenty-six), all divisible by 3.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pumping length</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a regular language <span class=\"m\">L</span>, a number <span class=\"m\">p</span> such that every string in <span class=\"m\">L</span> of length at least <span class=\"m\">p</span> can be pumped. If a DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> has <span class=\"m\">p</span> states, <span class=\"m\">p</span> works.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-statement\" data-h=\"3\">The statement</h3>\n<p><strong>Pumping lemma.</strong> If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular, there is a number <span class=\"m\">p \\ge 1</span> such that every string <span class=\"m\">w\\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|w| \\ge p</span> can be split as <span class=\"m\">w = xyz</span> where</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|y| &gt; 0</span> (the loop is not empty),</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|xy| \\le p</span> (the loop happens within the first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols),</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">xy^iz \\in L</span> for every <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 0</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Condition 2 comes from where we found the loop: in the first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols. It is the condition that makes most proofs work.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pumping lemma (regular)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular then there exists <span class=\"m\">p</span> such that every <span class=\"m\">w \\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|w|\\ge p</span> splits as <span class=\"m\">w = xyz</span> with <span class=\"m\">|y|&gt;0</span>, <span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span>, and <span class=\"m\">xy^iz\\in L</span> for all <span class=\"m\">i\\ge0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"using-it-a-game-against-an-opponent\" data-h=\"3\">Using it: a game against an opponent</h3>\n<p>To prove a language <span class=\"m\">L</span> is <strong>not</strong> regular, we argue by contradiction, and it helps to think of it as a game.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Assume <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular. Then the opponent hands you some pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>. You do not get to choose <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n<li><strong>You</strong> choose a string <span class=\"m\">w \\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|w| \\ge p</span>, cleverly, in terms of <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n<li>The opponent chooses any split <span class=\"m\">w = xyz</span> obeying <span class=\"m\">|y| &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">|xy| \\le p</span>. You must handle every possible split.</li>\n<li><strong>You</strong> choose an <span class=\"m\">i</span> so that <span class=\"m\">xy^iz \\notin L</span>. That contradicts the lemma.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"example-mathph70x-is-not-regular\" data-h=\"3\">Example: <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> is not regular</h3>\n<p>Assume it is regular with pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Choose <span class=\"m\">w = 0^p1^p</span>, which is in the language and has length <span class=\"m\">2p \\ge p</span>. Any split has <span class=\"m\">|xy| \\le p</span>, and the first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols of <span class=\"m\">w</span> are all 0s, so <span class=\"m\">y</span> consists only of 0s: say <span class=\"m\">y = 0^k</span> with <span class=\"m\">k \\ge 1</span>. Pump with <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">xy^2z = 0^{p+k}1^p</span>. It has more 0s than 1s, so it is not in the language. Contradiction, so <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> is not regular. With concrete numbers: if <span class=\"m\">p = 4</span>, then <span class=\"m\">w = 00001111</span>; whatever split is chosen, <span class=\"m\">y</span> is one to four 0s, and pumping gives something like <span class=\"m\">000001111</span>, which is unbalanced.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-perfect-squares\" data-h=\"3\">Example: perfect squares</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">L = \\{1^{n^2} : n\\ge0\\}</span>, strings of 1s whose length is a perfect square. Choose <span class=\"m\">w = 1^{p^2}</span>. Any split has <span class=\"m\">1 \\le |y| \\le p</span>. Pumping with <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span> gives length <span class=\"m\">p^2 + |y|</span>, and\n<span class=\"mb\">p^2 &lt; p^2 + |y| \\le p^2 + p &lt; p^2 + 2p + 1 = (p+1)^2.</span>\nThe new length lies strictly between two consecutive squares, so it is not a square. Contradiction again.</p>\n<h3 id=\"combining-with-closure-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Combining with closure properties</h3>\n<p>Sometimes it is easier to use closure. The language <span class=\"m\">L</span> of binary strings with equally many 0s and 1s is not regular: if it were, then <span class=\"m\">L\\cap 0^*1^*</span> would be regular too (intersection of regular languages), but <span class=\"m\">L \\cap 0^*1^* = \\{0^n1^n\\}</span>, which we just showed is not regular.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The pumping lemma is a <strong>one-way</strong> statement: every regular language satisfies it, but some non-regular languages satisfy it too. So you can use it only to prove a language is <strong>not</strong> regular, never to prove that it is. You cannot choose <span class=\"m\">p</span> or the split; you must defeat every split. Choose <span class=\"m\">w</span> so that the condition <span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span> pins down what <span class=\"m\">y</span> looks like; a careless choice like <span class=\"m\">w = 0^{p/2}1^{p/2}</span> lets <span class=\"m\">y</span> contain 1s and makes the argument messy. Remember that <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span> (deleting <span class=\"m\">y</span>) is allowed and is sometimes the winning move. And to show a language <em>is</em> regular, just build a DFA, NFA or regular expression for it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Because a DFA has finitely many states, any accepted string of length at least <span class=\"m\">p</span> (the number of states) drives the machine around a loop within its first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols. Repeating or deleting the loop keeps the string in the language. To prove a language is not regular, assume a pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>, choose a clever string of length at least <span class=\"m\">p</span>, show that every legal split can be pumped out of the language, and conclude by contradiction. Closure properties can reduce new problems to known non-regular languages.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;pump&quot;, &quot;machine&quot;: &quot;binary number divisible by 3&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 110}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The DFA has p = 3 states. The path of states shows where a state repeats; that loop is y.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With input <code>110</code>, find the repeated state and read off <span class=\"m\">x</span>, <span class=\"m\">y</span>, <span class=\"m\">z</span>.\n2. Move the \"pump i\" slider from 0 to 4 and check that each pumped string is divisible by 3.\n3. Type <code>1001</code> (nine) and <code>10010</code> (eighteen) and find their loops.\n4. Switch to the DFA \"contains 11\" and pump a string such as <code>0110</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the repeated state always appears within the first <span class=\"m\">p = 3</span> symbols, which is condition <span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span>. Pumping never changes the answer: the machine ends in the same state whatever <span class=\"m\">i</span> is, exactly as the proof argues. This is why the pumping lemma holds for <em>every</em> regular language, and why a language whose strings cannot all be pumped has no DFA.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ regular} \\Rightarrow \\exists p\\ \\forall w\\in L,\\ |w|\\ge p:\\ \\exists\\, w = xyz,\\ |y|&gt;0,\\ |xy|\\le p,\\ \\forall i\\ge 0:\\ xy^iz\\in L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p \\le \\text{number of states of any DFA for } L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{reading } p \\text{ symbols visits } p+1 \\text{ states} \\Rightarrow \\text{a repeat (pigeonhole)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pumping lemma for regular languages</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every regular language <span class=\"m\">L</span> has a pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span> as in the statement above.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">D</span> be a DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> with <span class=\"m\">p</span> states, and let <span class=\"m\">w = w_1\\cdots w_n\\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">n\\ge p</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">r_0, r_1, \\dots, r_p</span> be the states after reading <span class=\"m\">0, 1, \\dots, p</span> symbols. These are <span class=\"m\">p+1</span> states from a set of size <span class=\"m\">p</span>, so <span class=\"m\">r_j = r_k</span> for some <span class=\"m\">0 \\le j &lt; k \\le p</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">x = w_1\\cdots w_j</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = w_{j+1}\\cdots w_k</span> and <span class=\"m\">z</span> the rest. Then <span class=\"m\">|y| = k - j &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">|xy| = k \\le p</span>. Reading <span class=\"m\">y</span> from state <span class=\"m\">r_j</span> returns to <span class=\"m\">r_j</span>, so reading <span class=\"m\">y^i</span> also returns to <span class=\"m\">r_j</span> for any <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 0</span>. Hence <span class=\"m\">xy^iz</span> ends in the same state as <span class=\"m\">w</span>, which is accepting.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Some languages are not regular</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n : n\\ge 0\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{1^{n^2}: n\\ge0\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{ww : w\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\}</span>, and the set of binary strings with equally many 0s and 1s are not regular.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prove <span class=\"m\">L = \\{ww : w \\in \\{0,1\\}^*\\}</span> is not regular</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Assume <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular with pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>.</li>\n<li>Choose <span class=\"m\">w = 0^p1\\,0^p1</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">uu</span> with <span class=\"m\">u = 0^p1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">w \\in L</span>, and <span class=\"m\">|w| = 2p+2 \\ge p</span>.</li>\n<li>Since <span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span>, <span class=\"m\">y</span> lies in the first block of 0s: <span class=\"m\">y = 0^k</span>, <span class=\"m\">k\\ge1</span>.</li>\n<li>Pump <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span>: <span class=\"m\">0^{p+k}1\\,0^p1</span>. Its length is <span class=\"m\">2p + 2 + k</span>. If <span class=\"m\">k</span> is odd it cannot be <span class=\"m\">uu</span>. If <span class=\"m\">k</span> is even, the first half is <span class=\"m\">0^{p + k/2}</span>, which contains no 1, while the second half ends in 1, so the halves differ.</li>\n<li>Either way <span class=\"m\">xy^2z \\notin L</span>, a contradiction.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prove <span class=\"m\">\\{0^i1^j : i &gt; j\\}</span> is not regular, using <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Assume pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>, and choose <span class=\"m\">w = 0^{p+1}1^p</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span> forces <span class=\"m\">y = 0^k</span> with <span class=\"m\">1 \\le k \\le p</span>.</li>\n<li>Pumping up only adds 0s, which keeps <span class=\"m\">i &gt; j</span>: no contradiction there.</li>\n<li>Pump <strong>down</strong>, <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">0^{p+1-k}1^p</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">p</span> zeros and <span class=\"m\">p</span> ones, so <span class=\"m\">i \\le j</span>. Not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>. Contradiction.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Use the pumping lemma to show that the language of balanced parentheses (strings over <span class=\"m\">\\{(,)\\}</span> in which brackets match correctly) is not regular. Then explain why the language <span class=\"m\">(()^*)</span>, i.e. the strings in <span class=\"m\">L\\big((\\,(\\,)\\,)^*\\big)</span>, <em>is</em> regular.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Assume the balanced language is regular with pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Choose <span class=\"m\">w = (^p)^p</span>, which is balanced and of length <span class=\"m\">2p</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">|xy| \\le p</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = (^k</span> with <span class=\"m\">k \\ge 1</span>. Pumping with <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(^{p+k})^p</span>, which has more opening than closing brackets, so it is not balanced. Contradiction.\nThe second language consists only of repetitions of the fixed block <span class=\"m\">()</span>; it is described by a regular expression, so it is regular. (It is a regular <em>subset</em> of the balanced strings; the non-regularity comes from unbounded nesting depth.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The pumping lemma explains why you cannot write a true regular expression that checks balanced brackets, matching HTML tags or nested comments. Parsers for programming languages therefore use context-free grammars (next unit), while regular expressions handle only the flat tokens.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Recurrent neural networks with finite precision behave like finite-state machines, and research on formal languages and neural networks uses languages such as <span class=\"m\">a^nb^n</span> and Dyck (bracket) languages as test cases precisely because they are non-regular and require counting or a stack.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A turnstile that only remembers \"locked\" or \"unlocked\" cannot report whether exactly as many people left a stadium as entered. Any device with fixed finite memory has the same limit, which is the everyday content of the pumping lemma.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> has 7 states. What pumping length does the proof give?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The proof uses <span class=\"m\">p</span> equal to the number of states.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The pumping lemma can be used to prove that a language is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">not regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">both, depending on the language</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Some non-regular languages also satisfy the pumping conditions, so it only works in one direction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the proof that <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> is not regular with <span class=\"m\">w = 0^p1^p</span>, what does <span class=\"m\">y</span> consist of?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">only 0s</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only 1s</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">some 0s and some 1s</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">|xy| \\le p</span> and the first <span class=\"m\">p</span> symbols are all 0s.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">p = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">w = 0^51^5</span>, suppose <span class=\"m\">y = 00</span>. How many 0s does <span class=\"m\">xy^0z</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Deleting <span class=\"m\">y</span> removes two 0s: <span class=\"m\">5 - 2 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the perfect-squares proof with <span class=\"m\">p = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">|y|</span> is at most 4. What is the largest possible length of <span class=\"m\">xy^2z</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">p^2 + |y| \\le 16 + 4 = 20</span>, which is less than <span class=\"m\">25 = 5^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which choice of <span class=\"m\">i</span> is allowed in the pumping lemma?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only <span class=\"m\">i = 1</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">any <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 0</span>, including <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Pumping down (<span class=\"m\">i = 0</span>) is allowed and sometimes needed.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "dfa-minimization": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-fewer-states\" data-h=\"3\">Why fewer states?</h3>\n<p>The subset construction and the product construction are mechanical, and mechanical methods often produce machines with more states than necessary. A DFA used in a network router or a lexer is stored as a table with one row per state, so a smaller machine means less memory and better use of the processor cache. There is a beautiful fact here: for every regular language there is a <strong>unique smallest DFA</strong>, and there is a simple algorithm to find it. Minimisation also answers a theoretical question: how much memory does a language <em>really</em> need?</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-two-receptionists\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine two receptionists</h3>\n<p>Imagine two receptionists at the same hotel who follow different rule books. One writes notes in blue ink and one in black. If every possible future conversation with a guest ends with the same answer from both, then as far as the guests can tell, they are the same receptionist; the colour of the ink is irrelevant. States of a DFA are the same. Two states that give the same accept or reject answer for <strong>every possible remaining input</strong> are indistinguishable, and we can merge them without changing the language.</p>\n<h3 id=\"equivalent-and-distinguishable-states\" data-h=\"3\">Equivalent and distinguishable states</h3>\n<p>Two states <span class=\"m\">p</span> and <span class=\"m\">q</span> of a DFA are <strong>equivalent</strong>, written <span class=\"m\">p \\equiv q</span>, if for every string <span class=\"m\">z</span>, reading <span class=\"m\">z</span> from <span class=\"m\">p</span> ends in an accepting state exactly when reading <span class=\"m\">z</span> from <span class=\"m\">q</span> does. If some string <span class=\"m\">z</span> makes one accept and the other reject, <span class=\"m\">z</span> <strong>distinguishes</strong> them.</p>\n<p>The empty string already distinguishes every accepting state from every non-accepting state. Longer strings find subtler differences. If <span class=\"m\">p</span> and <span class=\"m\">q</span> both move on symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span> to states that are distinguished by <span class=\"m\">z</span>, then <span class=\"m\">az</span> distinguishes <span class=\"m\">p</span> and <span class=\"m\">q</span>. This \"push back one symbol\" rule is the whole algorithm.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Equivalent states</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>States <span class=\"m\">p, q</span> of a DFA are equivalent if for every string <span class=\"m\">z</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta(p,z) \\in F \\iff \\hat\\delta(q,z)\\in F</span>. Otherwise some string <span class=\"m\">z</span> distinguishes them.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimal DFA</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> with the fewest possible states. It is unique up to renaming of states.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-algorithm-partition-refinement\" data-h=\"3\">The algorithm: partition refinement</h3>\n<p>First delete <strong>unreachable</strong> states, ones that no input can ever reach from the start. They can never affect the answer. Then refine a partition of the remaining states into groups:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Start with two groups: accepting and non-accepting states.</li>\n<li>For each group, look at where each state goes on each symbol, and record which <em>group</em> it lands in. If two states in the same group land in different groups on some symbol, split them.</li>\n<li>Repeat step 2 until a round produces no split.</li>\n<li>Each final group becomes one state of the minimal DFA.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is often called <strong>Moore's algorithm</strong>; an equivalent pencil-and-paper version is the <strong>table-filling algorithm</strong>, where you mark pairs of states as distinguishable, starting from (accepting, non-accepting) pairs and repeatedly marking <span class=\"m\">(p, q)</span> if some symbol takes them to a marked pair.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-worked-run\" data-h=\"3\">A worked run</h3>\n<p>Take the 7-state DFA used in the visual below, over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span>, with accepting states <span class=\"m\">A, B</span>:</p>\n<p><span class=\"m\">A</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">C</span>. <span class=\"m\">B</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">A</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">D</span>. <span class=\"m\">C</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">C</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">E</span>. <span class=\"m\">D</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">C</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">F</span>. <span class=\"m\">E</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">F</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">A</span>. <span class=\"m\">F</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">E</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">B</span>. <span class=\"m\">G</span> loops to itself on both symbols, and nothing points to <span class=\"m\">G</span>.</p>\n<p>Step 0: <span class=\"m\">G</span> is unreachable, so delete it. Six states remain.</p>\n<p>Round 1: the groups are <span class=\"m\">\\{A, B\\}</span> (accepting) and <span class=\"m\">\\{C, D, E, F\\}</span>. Look at the non-accepting group on symbol 1: <span class=\"m\">C \\to E</span> and <span class=\"m\">D \\to F</span> stay in the non-accepting group, but <span class=\"m\">E \\to A</span> and <span class=\"m\">F \\to B</span> land in the accepting group. So split into <span class=\"m\">\\{C, D\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{E, F\\}</span>. The pair <span class=\"m\">A, B</span> behaves the same: on 0 both stay in <span class=\"m\">\\{A, B\\}</span>, on 1 both go to the non-accepting group.</p>\n<p>Round 2: the groups are <span class=\"m\">\\{A, B\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{C, D\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{E, F\\}</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> go on 0 to <span class=\"m\">\\{A,B\\}</span> and on 1 to <span class=\"m\">\\{C,D\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">C</span> and <span class=\"m\">D</span> go on 0 to <span class=\"m\">\\{C,D\\}</span> and on 1 to <span class=\"m\">\\{E,F\\}</span>. <span class=\"m\">E</span> and <span class=\"m\">F</span> go on 0 to <span class=\"m\">\\{E,F\\}</span> and on 1 to <span class=\"m\">\\{A,B\\}</span>. No splits, so we stop.</p>\n<p>The minimal DFA has <strong>3 states</strong>. Reading the groups as meanings, they track the number of 1s modulo 3: <span class=\"m\">\\{A,B\\}</span> is remainder 0, <span class=\"m\">\\{C,D\\}</span> remainder 1, <span class=\"m\">\\{E,F\\}</span> remainder 2. The original machine was wastefully also remembering whether the number of 0s was odd or even, information that never affected the answer.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-myhill-nerode-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">The Myhill-Nerode theorem</h3>\n<p>Minimisation has a machine-free version. For a language <span class=\"m\">L</span>, call two strings <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> <strong>indistinguishable by <span class=\"m\">L</span></strong>, written <span class=\"m\">x \\equiv_L y</span>, if for every string <span class=\"m\">z</span>, <span class=\"m\">xz \\in L</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">yz\\in L</span>. This is an equivalence relation on strings, and its classes are the \"things a machine must remember\".</p>\n<p>The <strong>Myhill-Nerode theorem</strong> says: <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> has finitely many classes, and in that case the number of classes equals the number of states of the minimal DFA.</p>\n<p>It has two great uses. First, <strong>lower bounds</strong>: if you find <span class=\"m\">k</span> strings that are pairwise distinguishable, every DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> needs at least <span class=\"m\">k</span> states. For \"ends in 01\", the strings <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">0</span>, <span class=\"m\">01</span> are pairwise distinguishable (append <span class=\"m\">1</span> to separate <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> from <span class=\"m\">0</span>; <span class=\"m\">01</span> is in the language and the others are not), so 3 states are necessary, and the 3-state DFA is minimal. Second, <strong>non-regularity</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span>, the strings <span class=\"m\">0^i</span> and <span class=\"m\">0^j</span> with <span class=\"m\">i\\ne j</span> are distinguished by <span class=\"m\">z = 1^i</span>. That gives infinitely many classes, so the language is not regular. This argument is often cleaner than the pumping lemma.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Do not forget to remove unreachable states first: they can survive partition refinement as an extra group. Keep the dead state if there is one: a complete minimal DFA includes it. Do not stop after one round just because the first split looked final; you must do a full round with no change. When splitting, compare the <strong>groups</strong> that successors land in, not the successor states themselves: <span class=\"m\">A \\to B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B \\to A</span> are fine because <span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> are in the same group. Finally, minimisation works on DFAs; minimising NFAs is a much harder problem with no efficient general algorithm known.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Two DFA states are equivalent if no future input can tell them apart. Removing unreachable states and then refining the partition {accepting, non-accepting} until it is stable merges all equivalent states and produces the unique minimal DFA. The Myhill-Nerode theorem describes the same structure in terms of strings: the number of classes of <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> is the number of states the minimal DFA needs, and infinitely many classes means the language is not regular.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-automata\" data-args='{&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;minimize&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A 7-state DFA with redundant and unreachable states. Press step to run one round of partition refinement.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Before stepping, find the unreachable state.\n2. Press step once and check that the first partition is {accepting} versus {non-accepting}.\n3. Press step again and predict which group splits, using the article's round 1.\n4. Keep stepping until nothing changes, and count the final groups.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the state <span class=\"m\">G</span> is removed first because no arrow reaches it. The non-accepting group splits because <span class=\"m\">E</span> and <span class=\"m\">F</span> jump to an accepting state on 1 while <span class=\"m\">C</span> and <span class=\"m\">D</span> do not. The refinement stabilises at three groups, matching the three classes \"number of 1s mod 3\". This is the Myhill-Nerode count for that language.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">p\\equiv q \\iff \\forall z\\in\\Sigma^*:\\ \\big(\\hat\\delta(p,z)\\in F \\iff \\hat\\delta(q,z)\\in F\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{split } p,q \\text{ if } \\exists a:\\ [\\delta(p,a)] \\ne [\\delta(q,a)]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x\\equiv_L y \\iff \\forall z:\\ (xz\\in L\\iff yz\\in L)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{states of minimal DFA} = \\text{number of classes of } \\equiv_L</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Myhill-Nerode theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language <span class=\"m\">L</span> is regular if and only if <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> has finitely many equivalence classes. When it does, the minimal DFA for <span class=\"m\">L</span> has exactly that many states, and it is unique up to renaming.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If a DFA <span class=\"m\">D</span> with <span class=\"m\">k</span> states recognises <span class=\"m\">L</span>, strings that lead to the same state are indistinguishable by <span class=\"m\">L</span> (the same future happens from the same state), so <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">k</span> classes. Conversely, if <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> has finitely many classes, build a DFA whose states are the classes <span class=\"m\">[x]</span>, with start <span class=\"m\">[\\varepsilon]</span>, transitions <span class=\"m\">\\delta([x],a) = [xa]</span> and accepting states <span class=\"m\">\\{[x] : x\\in L\\}</span>. This is well defined: if <span class=\"m\">x\\equiv_L y</span> then <span class=\"m\">xa \\equiv_L ya</span> (any <span class=\"m\">z</span> distinguishing <span class=\"m\">xa</span> and <span class=\"m\">ya</span> would make <span class=\"m\">az</span> distinguish <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>), and <span class=\"m\">x\\in L \\iff y\\in L</span> (take <span class=\"m\">z = \\varepsilon</span>). After reading <span class=\"m\">w</span> it is in state <span class=\"m\">[w]</span>, so it accepts <span class=\"m\">L</span>. Its number of states equals the number of classes, which by the first part is the smallest possible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Partition refinement is correct</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The refinement algorithm stops after at most <span class=\"m\">|Q|</span> rounds, and two reachable states end in the same group exactly when they are equivalent.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimise the DFA <span class=\"m\">1\\xrightarrow{a}2, 1\\xrightarrow b 3, 2 \\xrightarrow{a,b} 4, 3 \\xrightarrow{a,b} 4, 4\\xrightarrow{a,b}4</span> with accepting state 4</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>All states are reachable from start state 1.</li>\n<li>Round 0: <span class=\"m\">\\{4\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{1, 2, 3\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Round 1: on <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">1\\to2</span> (non-accepting group) but <span class=\"m\">2\\to4</span> and <span class=\"m\">3\\to4</span> (accepting group). Split: <span class=\"m\">\\{1\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{2,3\\}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{4\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Round 2: <span class=\"m\">2</span> and <span class=\"m\">3</span> both go to <span class=\"m\">\\{4\\}</span> on both symbols. No split.</li>\n<li>Minimal DFA: 3 states. The language is \"length at least 2\".</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prove that any DFA for \"contains 11\" needs at least 3 states</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Choose the strings <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">1</span>, <span class=\"m\">11</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> vs <span class=\"m\">1</span>: append <span class=\"m\">z = 1</span>. <span class=\"m\">1 \\notin L</span>, <span class=\"m\">11\\in L</span>. Distinguished.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> vs <span class=\"m\">11</span>: <span class=\"m\">z = \\varepsilon</span>. Distinguished.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">1</span> vs <span class=\"m\">11</span>: <span class=\"m\">z = \\varepsilon</span>. Distinguished.</li>\n<li>Three pairwise distinguishable strings means at least 3 states, and the 3-state DFA from the DFA lesson is minimal.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A DFA over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> has states <span class=\"m\">P</span> (start), <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, <span class=\"m\">R</span>, <span class=\"m\">S</span> with accepting states <span class=\"m\">R</span> and <span class=\"m\">S</span>, and transitions <span class=\"m\">P\\xrightarrow0 Q</span>, <span class=\"m\">P\\xrightarrow1 R</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q\\xrightarrow0 Q</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q\\xrightarrow1 S</span>, <span class=\"m\">R\\xrightarrow{0} Q</span>, <span class=\"m\">R\\xrightarrow1 R</span>, <span class=\"m\">S\\xrightarrow0 Q</span>, <span class=\"m\">S\\xrightarrow1 S</span>. Minimise it and describe its language.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>All four states are reachable. Round 0: <span class=\"m\">\\{R,S\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{P,Q\\}</span>.\nRound 1: <span class=\"m\">P</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">Q</span> (non-acc), 1 to <span class=\"m\">R</span> (acc). <span class=\"m\">Q</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">Q</span> (non-acc), 1 to <span class=\"m\">S</span> (acc). Same pattern, no split. <span class=\"m\">R</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">R</span>; <span class=\"m\">S</span>: 0 to <span class=\"m\">Q</span>, 1 to <span class=\"m\">S</span>. Same, no split.\nStable after one round: 2 states, <span class=\"m\">\\{P,Q\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{R,S\\}</span>. The machine is in <span class=\"m\">\\{R,S\\}</span> exactly when the last symbol was 1, so the language is \"strings ending in 1\".</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lexer generators, regex engines and hardware designers minimise automata routinely: Hopcroft's refinement algorithm runs in <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> time for <span class=\"m\">n</span> states. In hardware, a smaller state machine means fewer flip-flops on the chip.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Myhill-Nerode classes are the theoretical basis of automata learning algorithms such as Angluin's <span class=\"m\">L^*</span>, which learns a minimal DFA by asking membership and equivalence queries, and which is used to extract interpretable automata from trained recurrent neural networks.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A vending machine designed by two engineers might have separate states for \"inserted a 5 then a 10\" and \"inserted a 10 then a 5\". Since every future behaviour is identical (15 cents credited), the states can be merged. Minimisation finds every such merge automatically.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which states should be removed before partition refinement?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">unreachable states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the dead state</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Unreachable states never affect the answer and can otherwise survive as an extra group.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does the first partition in Moore's algorithm separate?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the start state from the rest</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">accepting from non-accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">states with self-loops from the rest</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The empty string already distinguishes these.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many states does the minimal DFA have for the 7-state machine in this lesson?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Remove <span class=\"m\">G</span>, then the groups <span class=\"m\">\\{A,B\\}, \\{C,D\\}, \\{E,F\\}</span> remain.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For the language \"ends in 01\", how many pairwise distinguishable strings did we find?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">0</span>, <span class=\"m\">01</span>, so at least 3 states are needed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">L = \\{0^n1^n\\}</span>, which string distinguishes <span class=\"m\">0^2</span> from <span class=\"m\">0^5</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">11</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">00</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0^211 \\in L</span> while <span class=\"m\">0^511\\notin L</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A language whose relation <span class=\"m\">\\equiv_L</span> has infinitely many classes is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">regular but with a large DFA</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">not regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always context-free</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>By Myhill-Nerode, regular means finitely many classes.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "context-free-grammars": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"beyond-finite-memory\" data-h=\"3\">Beyond finite memory</h3>\n<p>The pumping lemma showed that finite automata cannot handle <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> or balanced brackets. Yet programming languages are full of nesting: brackets inside brackets, <code>if</code> statements inside loops inside functions. We need a description method that can express \"every opening has a matching closing\", at any depth. <strong>Context-free grammars</strong> do exactly that. Instead of describing a machine that reads strings, a grammar describes how to <em>generate</em> strings by repeatedly replacing symbols according to rules.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-building-sentences\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine building sentences</h3>\n<p>Think of how a child learns sentence patterns. A sentence is a noun phrase followed by a verb phrase. A noun phrase is \"the\" followed by a noun. A noun is \"cat\" or \"dog\". A verb phrase is a verb, or a verb followed by a noun phrase. From these few rules you can produce \"the cat sees the dog\", and with a rule like \"a noun phrase can be a noun phrase followed by <em>who</em> and a verb phrase\" you can produce sentences of unlimited length and nesting. Linguist Noam Chomsky formalised this idea in the 1950s, and it became the foundation of how compilers understand code.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A context-free grammar (CFG) is a 4-tuple <span class=\"m\">G = (V, \\Sigma, R, S)</span> where</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"m\">V</span> is a finite set of <strong>variables</strong> (also called non-terminals), usually capital letters;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> is a finite set of <strong>terminals</strong>, the alphabet of the strings we generate, disjoint from <span class=\"m\">V</span>;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">R</span> is a finite set of <strong>rules</strong> (productions) of the form <span class=\"m\">A \\to w</span>, with <span class=\"m\">A\\in V</span> and <span class=\"m\">w</span> a string of variables and terminals (possibly <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>);</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\in V</span> is the <strong>start variable</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>\"Context-free\" means the left side of each rule is a single variable: you may replace <span class=\"m\">A</span> by <span class=\"m\">w</span> wherever <span class=\"m\">A</span> appears, regardless of its surroundings. We write several rules for the same variable on one line with a bar: <span class=\"m\">S \\to 0S1 \\mid \\varepsilon</span> means the two rules <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1</span> and <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Context-free grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A 4-tuple <span class=\"m\">(V,\\Sigma,R,S)</span> of variables, terminals, rules of the form <span class=\"m\">A \\to w</span> with <span class=\"m\">A</span> a single variable, and a start variable. Its language is the set of terminal strings derivable from <span class=\"m\">S</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"derivations\" data-h=\"3\">Derivations</h3>\n<p>To generate a string, start with <span class=\"m\">S</span> and repeatedly pick a variable and replace it using one of its rules. Each replacement is a <strong>derivation step</strong>, written <span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow</span>; a sequence of zero or more steps is written <span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow^*</span>. When no variables remain, you have a string of terminals, and it is in the language.</p>\n<p>With the grammar <span class=\"m\">S \\to 0S1 \\mid \\varepsilon</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">S \\Rightarrow 0S1 \\Rightarrow 00S11 \\Rightarrow 000S111 \\Rightarrow 000111.</span>\nEach use of <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1</span> adds one 0 on the left and one 1 on the right, so the counts always stay equal, and the final step <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span> removes the variable. The language is exactly <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n : n\\ge0\\}</span>, and deriving <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> takes <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> steps. So a non-regular language has a two-rule grammar.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derivation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A sequence <span class=\"m\">S \\Rightarrow u_1 \\Rightarrow u_2 \\Rightarrow \\cdots \\Rightarrow w</span> where each step replaces one variable by the right side of one of its rules. The language of <span class=\"m\">G</span> is <span class=\"m\">L(G) = \\{w\\in\\Sigma^* : S\\Rightarrow^* w\\}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Context-free language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language generated by some context-free grammar.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"more-examples\" data-h=\"3\">More examples</h3>\n<p><strong>Balanced brackets.</strong> <span class=\"m\">S \\to (S)S \\mid \\varepsilon</span>. A balanced string is either empty, or an opening bracket, a balanced inside, its closing bracket, and then another balanced string. For example <span class=\"m\">S \\Rightarrow (S)S \\Rightarrow ()S \\Rightarrow ()(S)S \\Rightarrow ()()S \\Rightarrow ()()</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Palindromes</strong> over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S0 \\mid 1S1 \\mid 0 \\mid 1 \\mid \\varepsilon</span>. Each rule wraps the same symbol around both ends, building the string from the outside in. <span class=\"m\">S \\Rightarrow 1S1 \\Rightarrow 10S01\\Rightarrow 10101</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>Arithmetic expressions</strong>: <span class=\"m\">E \\to E + E \\mid E \\times E \\mid (E) \\mid a</span>, where <span class=\"m\">a</span> stands for a number. It generates <span class=\"m\">a + a \\times a</span>, <span class=\"m\">(a + a) \\times a</span>, and so on.</p>\n<p><strong>Equal numbers of <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span></strong>: <span class=\"m\">S\\to aSbS \\mid bSaS \\mid \\varepsilon</span>. Every string generated has equal counts, since each rule adds one <span class=\"m\">a</span> and one <span class=\"m\">b</span>, and with more care one can show that every such string can be generated.</p>\n<h3 id=\"designing-a-grammar\" data-h=\"3\">Designing a grammar</h3>\n<p>A good strategy is to ask: how can a string in the language be <strong>broken into smaller strings in the language</strong>? That recursive structure becomes the rules. For <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^{2n}\\}</span>, each 0 on the left is matched with two 1s on the right, so <span class=\"m\">S \\to 0S11 \\mid \\varepsilon</span>. For <span class=\"m\">\\{0^i1^j : i \\ne j\\}</span>, split into \"more 0s\" and \"more 1s\": <span class=\"m\">S\\to A\\mid B</span>, where <span class=\"m\">A</span> generates <span class=\"m\">0^i1^j</span> with <span class=\"m\">i&gt;j</span> (one forced extra 0 plus a balanced middle, <span class=\"m\">A \\to 0A \\mid 0C</span>, <span class=\"m\">C\\to 0C1\\mid\\varepsilon</span>) and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is similar with extra 1s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"regular-languages-are-context-free\" data-h=\"3\">Regular languages are context-free</h3>\n<p>Every regular language has a CFG. Given a DFA, make a variable <span class=\"m\">R_q</span> for each state <span class=\"m\">q</span>, add a rule <span class=\"m\">R_q \\to aR_{r}</span> for each transition <span class=\"m\">q\\xrightarrow{a} r</span>, and add <span class=\"m\">R_q\\to\\varepsilon</span> for each accepting state <span class=\"m\">q</span>. A derivation then simulates a run of the DFA, one symbol per step. Such grammars, with at most one variable at the right-hand end, are called <strong>right-linear</strong>. Since <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> is context-free but not regular, the context-free languages are a strictly larger class.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closure-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Closure properties</h3>\n<p>Context-free languages are closed under <strong>union</strong> (new start <span class=\"m\">S\\to S_1\\mid S_2</span>), <strong>concatenation</strong> (<span class=\"m\">S \\to S_1S_2</span>) and <strong>star</strong> (<span class=\"m\">S\\to SS_1\\mid\\varepsilon</span>). Unlike regular languages, they are <strong>not</strong> closed under intersection or complement; we will prove this after the context-free pumping lemma.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A string is in <span class=\"m\">L(G)</span> only if it contains <strong>no variables</strong>; <span class=\"m\">0S1</span> is a sentential form, not a member of the language. Forgetting the base rule (such as <span class=\"m\">S \\to \\varepsilon</span>) gives a grammar that never finishes. When checking a grammar, test both directions: everything it generates must be in the language, and everything in the language must be generable. A grammar like <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S1 \\mid 01</span> misses the empty string, which may or may not matter. Finally, \"context-free\" refers to the form of the rules, not to whether the language is simple.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A context-free grammar generates strings by repeatedly replacing a single variable with the right side of one of its rules, starting from the start variable, until only terminals remain. Grammars can express nesting and matching, such as <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> with <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> and balanced brackets with <span class=\"m\">S\\to(S)S\\mid\\varepsilon</span>. Every regular language is context-free, and context-free languages are closed under union, concatenation and star.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-pda\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;balanced ( )&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;(()())&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A preview of the next lessons: a machine with a stack checks the language of the grammar S → (S)S | ε.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step through <code>(()())</code> and watch the stack height after each symbol.\n2. Try <code>())(</code> and find the exact symbol where it fails.\n3. Derive <code>(()())</code> with the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to(S)S\\mid\\varepsilon</span> on paper, and compare the nesting of your derivation with the stack heights.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the stack height is the number of brackets still waiting to be matched, which is the nesting depth of the grammar's derivation at that point. It is never negative and returns to zero at the end for balanced strings. A finite automaton cannot track an unbounded height, which is why we need grammars (and stacks) for this language.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">G = (V,\\Sigma,R,S), \\quad A \\to w,\\ A\\in V,\\ w\\in(V\\cup\\Sigma)^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(G) = \\{w\\in\\Sigma^* : S\\Rightarrow^* w\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S\\to 0S1\\mid\\varepsilon:\\ \\ L = \\{0^n1^n\\},\\quad 0^n1^n \\text{ takes } n+1 \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{DFA} \\to \\text{CFG}: \\ R_q \\to aR_{\\delta(q,a)},\\ \\ R_q\\to\\varepsilon \\ (q\\in F)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Regular languages are context-free</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every regular language is generated by a right-linear context-free grammar, and <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> is context-free but not regular. So regular languages form a proper subclass of context-free languages.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Given a DFA <span class=\"m\">(Q,\\Sigma,\\delta,q_0,F)</span>, build the grammar described in the article with start variable <span class=\"m\">R_{q_0}</span>. By induction on <span class=\"m\">|w|</span>, <span class=\"m\">R_{q_0}\\Rightarrow^* wR_q</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\delta(q_0,w) = q</span>. A terminal string <span class=\"m\">w</span> is derived precisely by finishing with <span class=\"m\">R_q\\to\\varepsilon</span> for an accepting <span class=\"m\">q</span>, i.e. when the DFA accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>. The grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> generates the non-regular language <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span>: by induction each sentential form is <span class=\"m\">0^kS1^k</span> or <span class=\"m\">0^k1^k</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Closure of context-free languages</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L_1 = L(G_1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2 = L(G_2)</span> with start variables <span class=\"m\">S_1, S_2</span> (and disjoint variable sets), then <span class=\"m\">S\\to S_1\\mid S_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">S\\to S_1S_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS_1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> generate <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cup L_2</span>, <span class=\"m\">L_1L_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_1^*</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derive <span class=\"m\">0011</span> with <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> and count the steps</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow 0S1</span> (rule 1).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">0S1\\Rightarrow 00S11</span> (rule 1).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">00S11\\Rightarrow 0011</span> (rule 2).</li>\n<li>Three steps, matching <span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> with <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write a grammar for <span class=\"m\">\\{a^ib^jc^k : i = j\\}</span> (any number of <span class=\"m\">c</span>s)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Split the string into a matched part <span class=\"m\">a^ib^i</span> and a free part <span class=\"m\">c^k</span>.</li>\n<li>Matched part: <span class=\"m\">A\\to aAb\\mid\\varepsilon</span>.</li>\n<li>Free part: <span class=\"m\">C\\to cC\\mid\\varepsilon</span>.</li>\n<li>Combine: <span class=\"m\">S\\to AC</span>. Check <span class=\"m\">aabbc</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow AC \\Rightarrow aAbC\\Rightarrow aaAbbC\\Rightarrow aabbC \\Rightarrow aabbcC\\Rightarrow aabbc</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Convert the DFA for \"even number of 1s\" into a grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>States <span class=\"m\">A</span> (start, accepting) and <span class=\"m\">B</span>; transitions <span class=\"m\">A\\xrightarrow0A</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\xrightarrow1B</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\xrightarrow0B</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\xrightarrow1A</span>.</li>\n<li>Rules: <span class=\"m\">A\\to 0A\\mid 1B\\mid\\varepsilon</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\to0B\\mid1A</span>.</li>\n<li>Derive <span class=\"m\">101</span>? <span class=\"m\">A\\Rightarrow1B\\Rightarrow10B\\Rightarrow101A\\Rightarrow101</span>. It has two 1s: accepted, as expected.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Give a context-free grammar for <span class=\"m\">L = \\{0^n1^m : m \\ge n \\ge 0\\}</span> and use it to derive <span class=\"m\">0111</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Each 0 is matched with a 1, and extra 1s may be added at the end: <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S1\\mid T</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to 1T\\mid\\varepsilon</span>.\nDerivation of <span class=\"m\">0111</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow0S1\\Rightarrow0T1\\Rightarrow01T1\\Rightarrow011T1\\Rightarrow0111</span>.\n(Check correctness: every derived string is <span class=\"m\">0^n1^{n+k}</span> with <span class=\"m\">k\\ge0</span>, and every such string is derived by using <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1</span> <span class=\"m\">n</span> times and <span class=\"m\">T\\to1T</span> <span class=\"m\">k</span> times.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The syntax of almost every programming language is specified by a context-free grammar, usually written in BNF or EBNF notation. Parser generators such as Yacc, Bison and ANTLR read the grammar and produce a parser automatically. JSON, SQL and HTML's nested structure are all defined this way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Probabilistic context-free grammars attach a probability to each rule and were the standard model for statistical parsing in NLP. Today, grammar-constrained decoding forces a language model's output to be valid JSON or valid code by allowing only tokens that keep the output derivable from a grammar.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A recipe can contain sub-recipes (\"for the sauce, first make a roux\"), which can contain their own steps. That nested structure is context-free: a recipe is a list of steps, and a step can itself be a recipe.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a context-free grammar, the left side of every rule is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a single variable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a single terminal</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">any string</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>That is what \"context-free\" means: replace <span class=\"m\">A</span> regardless of its surroundings.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many derivation steps does <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> need to produce <span class=\"m\">0^41^4</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Four uses of <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S1</span> and one of <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which grammar generates balanced brackets?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">S\\to(S\\mid)S\\mid\\varepsilon</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">S\\to(S)S\\mid\\varepsilon</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">S\\to()S</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Opening bracket, balanced inside, matching close, then more balanced brackets.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is <span class=\"m\">0S1</span> a member of <span class=\"m\">L(G)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no, it still contains a variable</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Members of the language are terminal strings only.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S11\\mid\\varepsilon</span> generates <span class=\"m\">0^21^k</span> for which <span class=\"m\">k</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each 0 comes with two 1s.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Every regular language is context-free. Is every context-free language regular?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no, for example <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It has a CFG but fails the pumping lemma for regular languages.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "parse-trees-ambiguity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"structure-not-just-membership\" data-h=\"3\">Structure, not just membership</h3>\n<p>Knowing that <span class=\"m\">2 + 3\\times4</span> belongs to the language of arithmetic expressions is not enough for a calculator. It also needs to know the <strong>structure</strong>: is it <span class=\"m\">2 + (3\\times 4) = 14</span> or <span class=\"m\">(2+3)\\times4 = 20</span>? Grammars give structure through <strong>parse trees</strong>, and a grammar that allows two different structures for the same string is called <strong>ambiguous</strong>. Ambiguity is a real practical problem: it is the difference between a program doing what you meant and doing something else.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-newspaper-headline\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a newspaper headline</h3>\n<p>Consider the headline \"Scientists study whales from space\". Are the scientists in space, or are the whales? The words are the same, but the sentence can be grouped in two ways, and the groupings have different meanings. Programming languages cannot afford that kind of confusion, so language designers write grammars in which every program has exactly one grouping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"parse-trees\" data-h=\"3\">Parse trees</h3>\n<p>A <strong>parse tree</strong> for a derivation is a tree whose root is the start variable, whose internal nodes are variables, and whose leaves are terminals (or <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>). Whenever a rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to X_1X_2\\cdots X_k</span> is used, the node <span class=\"m\">A</span> gets children <span class=\"m\">X_1, \\dots, X_k</span> in order. Reading the leaves from left to right gives the derived string, called the <strong>yield</strong> of the tree.</p>\n<p>For the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> and the string <span class=\"m\">0011</span>, the tree is a spine: <span class=\"m\">S</span> has children <span class=\"m\">0, S, 1</span>, the middle <span class=\"m\">S</span> again has children <span class=\"m\">0, S, 1</span>, and the innermost <span class=\"m\">S</span> has the single child <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>. Notice how the tree makes the matching visible: each 0 and the 1 that matches it are children of the same node.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Parse tree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A tree whose root is the start variable, whose internal nodes are variables expanded by a rule (children = right side of the rule in order), and whose leaves read left to right give the derived string.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"many-derivations-one-tree\" data-h=\"3\">Many derivations, one tree</h3>\n<p>A single tree can come from several derivations, because the order in which you expand variables does not matter to the tree. For example, with <span class=\"m\">S\\to AB</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to b</span>, you could expand <span class=\"m\">A</span> first or <span class=\"m\">B</span> first, but the tree is the same. To pick one canonical derivation per tree, we use the <strong>leftmost derivation</strong>, which always replaces the leftmost variable. Trees and leftmost derivations are in one-to-one correspondence.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Leftmost derivation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A derivation in which every step replaces the leftmost variable of the current string.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"ambiguity\" data-h=\"3\">Ambiguity</h3>\n<p>A grammar is <strong>ambiguous</strong> if some string in its language has <strong>two or more different parse trees</strong> (equivalently, two different leftmost derivations). Consider\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\to E+E \\mid E\\times E\\mid a.</span>\nThe string <span class=\"m\">a + a\\times a</span> has two trees. In the first, the root uses <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+E</span>, and the right <span class=\"m\">E</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">a\\times a</span>: this means <span class=\"m\">a + (a\\times a)</span>. In the second, the root uses <span class=\"m\">E\\to E\\times E</span>, and the left <span class=\"m\">E</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">a + a</span>: this means <span class=\"m\">(a+a)\\times a</span>. Put in numbers <span class=\"m\">2</span>, <span class=\"m\">3</span>, <span class=\"m\">4</span>: the first tree evaluates to <span class=\"m\">14</span>, the second to <span class=\"m\">20</span>.</p>\n<p>Subtraction shows the same issue with only one operator. <span class=\"m\">8-4-2</span> has trees <span class=\"m\">(8-4)-2 = 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">8-(4-2) = 6</span>. Since everyone agrees the answer is <span class=\"m\">2</span> (subtraction groups to the left), the second tree is \"wrong\", and a grammar that allows it is a bad grammar for arithmetic.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Ambiguous grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A grammar in which some string has at least two different parse trees (equivalently, two different leftmost derivations).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-many-trees\" data-h=\"3\">How many trees?</h3>\n<p>With <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+E\\mid a</span>, a string with <span class=\"m\">n</span> plus signs has as many trees as there are ways to fully bracket <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> operands. For <span class=\"m\">n = 1</span> there is 1 tree, for <span class=\"m\">n = 2</span> there are 2, for <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span> there are 5, and for <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> there are 14. These are the <strong>Catalan numbers</strong> <span class=\"m\">C_n = \\frac{1}{n+1}\\binom{2n}{n}</span>. So ambiguity can multiply quickly: a 10-operator expression has <span class=\"m\">C_{10} = 16{,}796</span> trees.</p>\n<h3 id=\"removing-ambiguity\" data-h=\"3\">Removing ambiguity</h3>\n<p>We can usually rewrite the grammar to force the intended structure. The standard fix for arithmetic uses one variable per <strong>precedence level</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\to E+T\\mid T,\\qquad T\\to T\\times F\\mid F,\\qquad F\\to(E)\\mid a.</span>\nHere <span class=\"m\">E</span> is a sum of terms, <span class=\"m\">T</span> is a product of factors, and <span class=\"m\">F</span> is a number or a bracketed expression. Because a <span class=\"m\">+</span> can only be introduced at the <span class=\"m\">E</span> level, multiplication is always grouped first, giving <span class=\"m\">a + (a\\times a)</span>. Because <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+T</span> has the recursion on the <strong>left</strong>, <span class=\"m\">a + a + a</span> groups as <span class=\"m\">(a+a)+a</span>: left associativity. This grammar is unambiguous and generates the same strings as the ambiguous one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"inherent-ambiguity-and-the-dangling-else\" data-h=\"3\">Inherent ambiguity and the dangling else</h3>\n<p>Some languages are <strong>inherently ambiguous</strong>: every grammar for them is ambiguous. The classic example is <span class=\"m\">\\{a^ib^jc^k : i = j \\text{ or } j = k\\}</span>; strings like <span class=\"m\">a^nb^nc^n</span> fit both conditions, and it can be proved that any grammar must produce them in two ways. A famous practical case is the <strong>dangling else</strong>: in <code>if A then if B then X else Y</code>, does the <code>else</code> belong to the inner or the outer <code>if</code>? Most languages resolve it by a rule, \"an else belongs to the nearest unmatched if\", built into the grammar or the parser. There is no general algorithm to decide whether a given CFG is ambiguous; we will see later in the course why such questions can be undecidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Two different derivations do not make a grammar ambiguous; two different <strong>trees</strong> (or two different <em>leftmost</em> derivations) do. Ambiguity is a property of the <strong>grammar</strong>, not the language, except in the special case of inherently ambiguous languages. When removing ambiguity, check that the new grammar still generates the same language. And do not confuse ambiguity with the language being complicated: <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS\\mid a</span> generates the very simple language <span class=\"m\">a^+</span> but is ambiguous.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A parse tree records which rules were used and how the string is grouped; its leaves spell the string. Leftmost derivations correspond one-to-one with trees. A grammar is ambiguous if some string has two trees, and different trees can mean different things, as with <span class=\"m\">2 + 3\\times4</span> evaluating to 14 or 20. Precedence levels and left recursion give an unambiguous grammar for arithmetic, but some languages are inherently ambiguous, and ambiguity of a CFG cannot be decided in general.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-pda\" data-args='{&quot;view&quot;: &quot;tree&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The ambiguous grammar E → E∘E | number. Compare the two parse trees of the same expression and their values.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. For <code>2 + 3 × 4</code>, switch between Tree 1 and Tree 2 and note both values.\n2. Choose <code>8 − 4 − 2</code> and decide which tree matches the usual rule for subtraction.\n3. Choose <code>16 ÷ 4 ÷ 2</code> and compute both values before looking.\n4. Choose <code>2 × 3 + 4</code> and decide which tree the unambiguous grammar <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+T\\mid T</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to T\\times F\\mid F</span> would build.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the leaves are the same in both trees, only the grouping differs, and the grouping changes the value: 14 or 20, 2 or 6, 2 or 8, 10 or 14. The tree that groups subtraction and division to the left is the conventional one. For <code>2 × 3 + 4</code> the unambiguous grammar forces the product to be grouped first, giving 10.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{yield}(T) = \\text{leaves of } T \\text{ read left to right}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{ambiguous} \\iff \\exists w:\\ w \\text{ has} \\ge 2 \\text{ parse trees} \\iff \\ge 2 \\text{ leftmost derivations}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{trees of } a{+}a{+}\\cdots{+}a \\ (n \\text{ pluses}) = C_n = \\tfrac{1}{n+1}\\tbinom{2n}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\to E+T\\mid T,\\quad T\\to T\\times F\\mid F,\\quad F\\to(E)\\mid a</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Trees and leftmost derivations correspond</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a fixed grammar and terminal string <span class=\"m\">w</span>, there is a one-to-one correspondence between parse trees with yield <span class=\"m\">w</span> and leftmost derivations of <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Given a tree, produce a derivation by repeatedly expanding the leftmost unexpanded variable node using the rule recorded at that node; this is leftmost and determined by the tree. Given a leftmost derivation, build the tree by attaching children for each rule used, at the leftmost variable leaf; this is determined by the derivation. The two procedures undo each other, so the correspondence is a bijection.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Inherent ambiguity exists</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The language <span class=\"m\">\\{a^ib^jc^k : i=j \\text{ or } j=k\\}</span> is context-free, but every context-free grammar for it is ambiguous.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS\\mid a</span> is ambiguous</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Take the string <span class=\"m\">aaa</span>.</li>\n<li>Tree 1: <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS</span>, with the left <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS\\to aa</span> and the right <span class=\"m\">S\\to a</span>: grouping <span class=\"m\">(aa)a</span>.</li>\n<li>Tree 2: <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS</span>, with the left <span class=\"m\">S\\to a</span> and the right <span class=\"m\">S\\to SS\\to aa</span>: grouping <span class=\"m\">a(aa)</span>.</li>\n<li>Two different trees for one string, so the grammar is ambiguous (though <span class=\"m\">L = a^+</span> is simple).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Give the leftmost derivation of <span class=\"m\">a + a\\times a</span> in the unambiguous grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">E\\Rightarrow E + T</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow T + T\\Rightarrow F + T\\Rightarrow a + T</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow a + T\\times F \\Rightarrow a + F\\times F\\Rightarrow a + a\\times F\\Rightarrow a+a\\times a</span>.</li>\n<li>The <span class=\"m\">\\times</span> is created inside the right <span class=\"m\">T</span>, so the only tree means <span class=\"m\">a + (a\\times a)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count the parse trees of <span class=\"m\">a+a+a+a</span> in <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+E\\mid a</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>There are <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span> plus signs, so 4 operands.</li>\n<li>The root's plus can be the 1st, 2nd or 3rd. If it is the 1st, the right side <span class=\"m\">a+a+a</span> has <span class=\"m\">C_2 = 2</span> trees; 2nd: <span class=\"m\">1\\times1 = 1</span>; 3rd: <span class=\"m\">2</span>.</li>\n<li>Total <span class=\"m\">2 + 1 + 2 = 5 = C_3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Consider the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to aS\\mid aSbS\\mid\\varepsilon</span>. Show that it is ambiguous by giving two different leftmost derivations of <span class=\"m\">aab</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Derivation 1: <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow aS\\Rightarrow aaSbS\\Rightarrow aabS\\Rightarrow aab</span>. (The first <span class=\"m\">a</span> uses <span class=\"m\">S\\to aS</span>, the second <span class=\"m\">a</span> is matched with <span class=\"m\">b</span>.)\nDerivation 2: <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow aSbS\\Rightarrow aaSbS\\Rightarrow aabS\\Rightarrow aab</span>. (The first <span class=\"m\">a</span> is matched with <span class=\"m\">b</span>, and the inner <span class=\"m\">S\\to aS</span> produces the second <span class=\"m\">a</span>, followed by <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>.)\nLet us check the second carefully: <span class=\"m\">aSbS\\Rightarrow a(aS)bS = aaSbS</span>, then the leftmost <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span> gives <span class=\"m\">aabS</span>, then <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span> gives <span class=\"m\">aab</span>. The first steps differ (<span class=\"m\">S\\to aS</span> versus <span class=\"m\">S\\to aSbS</span>), so the leftmost derivations, and hence the trees, are different. The grammar is ambiguous. (This is the grammar of the dangling-else problem, with <span class=\"m\">a</span> = if and <span class=\"m\">b</span> = else.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compilers build a parse tree (or a compressed abstract syntax tree) for every program, and code generation walks it. Parser generators report \"shift/reduce conflicts\" when a grammar is ambiguous, and languages resolve cases like the dangling else and operator precedence with explicit rules.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In NLP, a sentence like \"I saw the man with the telescope\" has several parse trees. Statistical and neural parsers score the possible trees and pick the most probable one, and the parse structure is used as a feature in tasks such as relation extraction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Calculators that follow the order of operations implement the unambiguous grammar. Spreadsheets do too: typing <code>=2+3*4</code> gives 14, not 20, because the formula parser groups multiplication first.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Reading the leaves of a parse tree from left to right gives...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the derived string</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the list of rules used</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the leftmost derivation</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>This is the yield of the tree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">2 + 3\\times 4</span>, what does the tree grouping <span class=\"m\">(2+3)\\times4</span> evaluate to?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"20\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">5\\times4 = 20</span>; the conventional tree gives 14.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A grammar is ambiguous when some string has...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">two different derivations</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">two different parse trees</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a very long derivation</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Different derivation orders can give the same tree; only different trees count.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many parse trees does <span class=\"m\">a+a+a</span> have in <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+E\\mid a</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(a+a)+a</span> and <span class=\"m\">a+(a+a)</span>: <span class=\"m\">C_2 = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What value does <span class=\"m\">8-(4-2)</span> give?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The conventional left grouping <span class=\"m\">(8-4)-2</span> gives 2.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the unambiguous grammar <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+T\\mid T</span>, why does <span class=\"m\">a+a+a</span> group to the left?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the recursion <span class=\"m\">E\\to E+T</span> is on the left</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">because <span class=\"m\">+</span> has lower precedence than <span class=\"m\">\\times</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">because <span class=\"m\">F\\to(E)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Only the left child can contain another <span class=\"m\">+</span>, so earlier additions are grouped first.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "chomsky-normal-form": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-a-normal-form\" data-h=\"3\">Why a normal form?</h3>\n<p>Grammars can be written in endlessly many styles: long rules, rules that produce nothing, rules that just rename one variable as another. That freedom is pleasant for people but awkward for algorithms and proofs. A <strong>normal form</strong> is a standard shape that every grammar can be converted into without changing its language. The most important one is <strong>Chomsky normal form</strong> (CNF). Once a grammar is in CNF, we can answer the question \"is this string in the language?\" with a clean, guaranteed-polynomial algorithm called <strong>CYK</strong>, and we can prove theorems such as the pumping lemma for context-free languages.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-family-tree-with-two-children\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a family tree with two children</h3>\n<p>Imagine a strange family in which every parent has exactly two children, or else is a \"leaf\" person who has a single named role. Every family tree then has a very regular shape: it is a binary tree. If you know how many leaf people there are, you know exactly how many parents there are. CNF makes every parse tree look like that, and that regularity is what algorithms exploit.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A grammar is in <strong>Chomsky normal form</strong> if every rule has one of these forms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span>, where <span class=\"m\">B</span> and <span class=\"m\">C</span> are variables and neither is the start variable;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>, where <span class=\"m\">a</span> is a terminal;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>, allowed only for the start variable <span class=\"m\">S</span>, and only if <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is in the language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So no long rules, no mixtures of terminals and variables on a right side, no ε-rules except possibly one at the start, and no <strong>unit rules</strong> like <span class=\"m\">A\\to B</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Chomsky normal form</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A CFG whose rules all have the form <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span> (with <span class=\"m\">B</span>, <span class=\"m\">C</span> non-start variables), <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span> (a terminal), or <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span> for the start variable.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"counting-derivation-steps\" data-h=\"3\">Counting derivation steps</h3>\n<p>CNF gives an exact count. To derive a string of length <span class=\"m\">n\\ge1</span>, the rules <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span> build a binary tree skeleton with <span class=\"m\">n</span> leaves-to-be, which needs <span class=\"m\">n-1</span> uses, and then each of the <span class=\"m\">n</span> variables at the bottom is turned into a terminal with one use of <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>. In total, <strong>every derivation of a length-<span class=\"m\">n</span> string takes exactly <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> steps</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span> that is 7 steps. This means the number of possible derivations to check is finite, which is the first hint that membership is decidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"converting-a-grammar-to-cnf\" data-h=\"3\">Converting a grammar to CNF</h3>\n<p>Any CFG can be converted to CNF in four stages.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>New start variable.</strong> Add <span class=\"m\">S_0\\to S</span>, so the start variable never appears on a right side.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove ε-rules.</strong> For each variable <span class=\"m\">A</span> that can derive <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> (a <em>nullable</em> variable), delete <span class=\"m\">A\\to\\varepsilon</span> and, for every rule with <span class=\"m\">A</span> on the right, add a copy with that occurrence of <span class=\"m\">A</span> left out. For example, if <span class=\"m\">B</span> is nullable then <span class=\"m\">A\\to uBv</span> gets the extra rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to uv</span>. Keep <span class=\"m\">S_0\\to\\varepsilon</span> if the start was nullable.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove unit rules.</strong> Replace each <span class=\"m\">A\\to B</span> by <span class=\"m\">A\\to w</span> for every rule <span class=\"m\">B\\to w</span> (repeating as needed).</li>\n<li><strong>Shorten and separate.</strong> Replace each terminal <span class=\"m\">a</span> inside a longer right side with a new variable <span class=\"m\">U_a</span> and rule <span class=\"m\">U_a\\to a</span>. Then break each long rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to B_1B_2\\cdots B_k</span> into a chain <span class=\"m\">A\\to B_1C_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">C_1\\to B_2C_2</span>, ..., <span class=\"m\">C_{k-2}\\to B_{k-1}B_k</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here is a short example. Start with <span class=\"m\">S\\to 0S1\\mid 01</span>, which generates <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n : n\\ge1\\}</span>. There are no ε-rules and no unit rules. Introduce <span class=\"m\">A\\to0</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to1</span>, so the rules become <span class=\"m\">S\\to ASB\\mid AB</span>. Break <span class=\"m\">ASB</span> into <span class=\"m\">S\\to AT</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to SB</span>. The final CNF grammar is\n<span class=\"mb\">S\\to AT\\mid AB,\\qquad T\\to SB,\\qquad A\\to0,\\qquad B\\to1.</span>\n(Here <span class=\"m\">S</span> appears on a right side, inside <span class=\"m\">T \\to SB</span>; strictly, stage 1 would add a new start <span class=\"m\">S_0 \\to AT \\mid AB</span>. We skip it because <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is not in the language, so it changes nothing important for parsing.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-cyk-algorithm\" data-h=\"3\">The CYK algorithm</h3>\n<p>The <strong>Cocke-Younger-Kasami</strong> algorithm decides whether a string <span class=\"m\">w = w_1\\cdots w_n</span> is generated by a CNF grammar. It is a classic example of <strong>dynamic programming</strong>. For every substring <span class=\"m\">w_i\\cdots w_j</span>, it computes the set <span class=\"m\">T[i,j]</span> of variables that can derive that substring, starting with substrings of length 1 and building up.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Length 1: <span class=\"m\">T[i,i] = \\{A : A\\to w_i\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Length <span class=\"m\">\\ell &gt; 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">A\\in T[i,j]</span> if there is a rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span> and a split point <span class=\"m\">k</span> with <span class=\"m\">i\\le k&lt;j</span> such that <span class=\"m\">B\\in T[i,k]</span> and <span class=\"m\">C\\in T[k+1,j]</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The string is in the language iff the start variable is in <span class=\"m\">T[1,n]</span>.</p>\n<p>Let us run it on <span class=\"m\">w = 0011</span> with the grammar above. Length 1: positions 1 and 2 hold <span class=\"m\">\\{A\\}</span>, positions 3 and 4 hold <span class=\"m\">\\{B\\}</span>. Length 2: <span class=\"m\">T[1,2]</span> needs a rule with right side <span class=\"m\">AA</span>, which does not exist, so it is empty; <span class=\"m\">T[2,3]</span> has <span class=\"m\">AB</span>, so <span class=\"m\">S\\in T[2,3]</span>; <span class=\"m\">T[3,4]</span> would need <span class=\"m\">BB</span>, empty. Length 3: <span class=\"m\">T[1,3]</span> tries <span class=\"m\">A</span> followed by <span class=\"m\">T[2,3] = \\{S\\}</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">AS</span>, no rule; and <span class=\"m\">T[1,2]\\cdot T[3,3]</span>, empty. So empty. <span class=\"m\">T[2,4]</span> tries <span class=\"m\">T[2,3]\\cdot T[4,4]</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">SB</span>, so <span class=\"m\">T\\in T[2,4]</span>. Length 4: <span class=\"m\">T[1,4]</span> tries <span class=\"m\">T[1,1]\\cdot T[2,4]</span>, which is <span class=\"m\">AT</span>, so <span class=\"m\">S \\in T[1,4]</span>. The string is accepted.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-fast-is-it\" data-h=\"3\">How fast is it?</h3>\n<p>There are about <span class=\"m\">n^2/2</span> cells, and filling one cell tries up to <span class=\"m\">n-1</span> split points and every rule. So CYK runs in <span class=\"m\">O(n^3\\cdot|G|)</span> time, where <span class=\"m\">|G|</span> is the size of the grammar. For a 100-symbol string that is around a million basic steps times the grammar size: fine for a computer, and vastly better than trying every possible derivation. Practical compilers use faster linear-time parsers (LL and LR) for restricted grammars, but CYK works for <strong>every</strong> context-free grammar.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>When removing ε-rules, you must add copies for <strong>every subset</strong> of nullable occurrences: if <span class=\"m\">A\\to BCB</span> with <span class=\"m\">B</span> nullable, you add <span class=\"m\">A\\to CB</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span> and <span class=\"m\">A\\to C</span>. Do not forget that unit rules can chain (<span class=\"m\">A\\to B\\to C</span>). In CYK, the cell for a substring depends on <strong>all</strong> split points, not just one. And the <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> count applies only to CNF grammars and strings of length <span class=\"m\">n\\ge1</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Chomsky normal form restricts rules to <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>, and possibly <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>. Every CFG can be converted to CNF by adding a new start, removing ε-rules and unit rules, and splitting long rules. In CNF every length-<span class=\"m\">n</span> string has derivations of exactly <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> steps, and the CYK dynamic programming algorithm decides membership in <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span> time by filling a triangular table of variable sets.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"bigo\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> CYK fills about n²/2 table cells and tries up to n split points each, so its running time grows like n³.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">n</span> to 40 and compare the values shown for <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">n^3</span>.\n2. Increase <span class=\"m\">n</span> to 1000 and read off <span class=\"m\">n^3</span>, the rough number of steps CYK needs for a 1000-symbol input.\n3. Compare <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> with <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> at <span class=\"m\">n = 40</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> at <span class=\"m\">n = 40</span> is <span class=\"m\">64{,}000</span>, so CYK on a 40-symbol string is quick. At <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> it is <span class=\"m\">10^9</span>: slow but feasible, which is why compilers prefer linear-time parsers for restricted grammars. Meanwhile <span class=\"m\">2^{40}</span> is over a trillion; trying all parse trees blindly grows exponentially, and dynamic programming is what keeps CYK polynomial.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A\\to BC,\\quad A\\to a,\\quad S\\to\\varepsilon \\ (\\text{start only})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CNF derivation of } w,\\ |w| = n\\ge1: \\ \\text{exactly } 2n-1 \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">T[i,i] = \\{A : A\\to w_i\\},\\qquad T[i,j] = \\{A : A\\to BC,\\ B\\in T[i,k],\\ C\\in T[k+1,j],\\ i\\le k&lt;j\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CYK time} = O(n^3\\cdot|G|)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Every CFG has an equivalent CNF grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every context-free grammar <span class=\"m\">G</span> there is a grammar <span class=\"m\">G'</span> in Chomsky normal form with <span class=\"m\">L(G') = L(G)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Apply the four stages. Adding <span class=\"m\">S_0\\to S</span> does not change the language. Removing ε-rules while adding copies with nullable variables omitted preserves every derivation of a non-empty string (an occurrence that would have derived <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> is simply omitted), and we keep <span class=\"m\">S_0\\to\\varepsilon</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon\\in L(G)</span>. Replacing unit rules <span class=\"m\">A\\to B</span> by <span class=\"m\">A\\to w</span> for <span class=\"m\">B\\to w</span> shortcuts chains of renamings. Finally, introducing <span class=\"m\">U_a\\to a</span> and splitting long rules into chains of binary rules produces the same strings, since each chain can only be expanded in one way. Every stage preserves the language and the final rules have CNF form.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derivation length in CNF</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">G</span> is in CNF and <span class=\"m\">w\\in L(G)</span> with <span class=\"m\">|w| = n\\ge1</span>, every derivation of <span class=\"m\">w</span> has exactly <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> steps.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Each step either uses <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span>, which increases the number of symbols in the sentential form by 1, or <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>, which turns one variable into a terminal. Starting from 1 symbol and ending with <span class=\"m\">n</span> symbols needs <span class=\"m\">n-1</span> binary steps, and each of the <span class=\"m\">n</span> terminals is produced by exactly one <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span> step: <span class=\"m\">(n-1)+n = 2n-1</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Derive <span class=\"m\">0011</span> in the CNF grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to AT\\mid AB</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to SB</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to0</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow AT\\Rightarrow 0T</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow 0SB\\Rightarrow 0ABB</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Rightarrow 00BB\\Rightarrow 001B\\Rightarrow0011</span>.</li>\n<li>Count: 7 steps, which is <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot4-1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Remove ε-rules from <span class=\"m\">S\\to AB</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to aA\\mid\\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to b</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Nullable variables: only <span class=\"m\">A</span>.</li>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">S\\to AB</span>, add the copy without <span class=\"m\">A</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\to B</span>.</li>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">A\\to aA</span>, add <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span>; delete <span class=\"m\">A\\to\\varepsilon</span>.</li>\n<li>Result: <span class=\"m\">S\\to AB\\mid B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to aA\\mid a</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to b</span>, which still generates <span class=\"m\">a^*b</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Use CYK to show <span class=\"m\">001</span> is not generated by the same grammar</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Length 1: <span class=\"m\">\\{A\\}, \\{A\\}, \\{B\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Length 2: <span class=\"m\">T[1,2]</span> needs <span class=\"m\">AA</span>: empty. <span class=\"m\">T[2,3]</span> has <span class=\"m\">AB</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\{S\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Length 3: <span class=\"m\">T[1,3]</span>: <span class=\"m\">A\\cdot S</span> gives <span class=\"m\">AS</span>, no rule; <span class=\"m\">T[1,2]\\cdot B</span> is empty. So <span class=\"m\">T[1,3] = \\varnothing</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\notin T[1,3]</span>, so <span class=\"m\">001</span> is rejected, as it should be (two 0s, one 1).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Convert <span class=\"m\">S\\to aSb\\mid ab</span> into Chomsky normal form and state how many steps a CNF derivation of <span class=\"m\">aaabbb</span> takes.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Introduce <span class=\"m\">U\\to a</span> and <span class=\"m\">V\\to b</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\to USV\\mid UV</span>. Split <span class=\"m\">USV</span> into <span class=\"m\">S\\to UW</span>, <span class=\"m\">W\\to SV</span>.\nCNF grammar: <span class=\"m\">S\\to UW\\mid UV</span>, <span class=\"m\">W\\to SV</span>, <span class=\"m\">U\\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">V\\to b</span>. (There are no ε-rules or unit rules; if a strict new start variable is required, add <span class=\"m\">S_0\\to UW\\mid UV</span>.)\n<span class=\"m\">|aaabbb| = 6</span>, so any derivation takes <span class=\"m\">2\\cdot6-1 = 11</span> steps.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>CYK and its relatives (Earley parsing, GLR) are used when grammars are ambiguous or too general for LR parsers, for example in parsing natural language, in some IDE tools, and in RNA secondary structure prediction, where a CYK-like dynamic program finds the best folding of a molecule.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The inside algorithm for probabilistic CFGs is CYK with sums of probabilities instead of yes/no answers, and it is the basis of the inside-outside EM algorithm for learning grammar probabilities from text. Similar span-based dynamic programs power neural constituency parsers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Proofreading a sentence by asking \"can these two words form a phrase? can that phrase and the next one form a bigger phrase?\" and building upwards is exactly the CYK table, filled by hand.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which rule is allowed in Chomsky normal form?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A\\to aB</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A\\to B</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>CNF allows only <span class=\"m\">A\\to BC</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to a</span> and <span class=\"m\">S\\to\\varepsilon</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many steps does a CNF derivation of a string of length 5 take?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2n-1 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the running time of CYK on a string of length <span class=\"m\">n</span> (fixed grammar)?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>About <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> cells and up to <span class=\"m\">n</span> split points each.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In CYK, the string is accepted when...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the start variable is in the cell for the whole string</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every cell is non-empty</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the last row contains a terminal</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">T[1,n]</span> holds the variables deriving all of <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">n = 10</span>, how many cells <span class=\"m\">T[i,j]</span> with <span class=\"m\">i\\le j</span> does CYK fill?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"55\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">n(n+1)/2 = 55</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">When removing the ε-rule for nullable <span class=\"m\">B</span> from <span class=\"m\">A\\to BcB</span>, how many new rules are added?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Omit the first <span class=\"m\">B</span>, the second <span class=\"m\">B</span>, or both: <span class=\"m\">cB</span>, <span class=\"m\">Bc</span>, <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "pushdown-automata": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"adding-memory-carefully\" data-h=\"3\">Adding memory, carefully</h3>\n<p>A finite automaton fails on <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> because it cannot count without limit. What is the smallest amount of extra memory that fixes this? Not a full random-access memory, just a <strong>stack</strong>: a pile where you can only add to the top (<strong>push</strong>) and remove from the top (<strong>pop</strong>). A finite automaton with a stack is called a <strong>pushdown automaton</strong> (PDA), and it turns out to recognise exactly the context-free languages, matching the power of grammars the way finite automata matched regular expressions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-pile-of-plates\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a pile of plates</h3>\n<p>Picture checking a row of opening and closing brackets with a pile of plates. Each time you see an opening bracket, put a plate on the pile. Each time you see a closing bracket, take a plate off. If you ever need to remove a plate from an empty pile, there was a closing bracket with no partner. If plates remain at the end, some opening bracket never closed. The pile can grow as high as needed, which is the extra power a finite machine lacks, yet you only ever look at the top plate.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A PDA is a 6-tuple <span class=\"m\">(Q,\\Sigma,\\Gamma,\\delta,q_0,F)</span> where <span class=\"m\">Q</span> is a finite set of states, <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> the input alphabet, <span class=\"m\">\\Gamma</span> the <strong>stack alphabet</strong>, <span class=\"m\">q_0</span> the start state, <span class=\"m\">F</span> the accepting states, and the transition function\n<span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma_\\varepsilon\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon\\to\\mathcal P(Q\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon),</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma_\\varepsilon = \\Sigma\\cup\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\Gamma_\\varepsilon = \\Gamma\\cup\\{\\varepsilon\\}</span>. A transition written <span class=\"m\">a, X\\to Y</span> means: read input symbol <span class=\"m\">a</span> (or nothing if <span class=\"m\">a = \\varepsilon</span>), pop <span class=\"m\">X</span> from the top of the stack (or nothing), and push <span class=\"m\">Y</span> (or nothing). The machine is <strong>nondeterministic</strong>: there may be several choices, and it accepts if some sequence of choices consumes the whole input and ends in an accepting state.</p>\n<p>It is common to push a special bottom marker such as <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> at the start, so the machine can tell when its stack is empty. (The visual below draws it as a dollar sign.)</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pushdown automaton</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A nondeterministic finite automaton equipped with an unbounded stack. Each move reads an input symbol or ε, pops a stack symbol or ε, pushes a stack symbol or ε, and changes state.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Stack</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A last-in, first-out store: push adds a symbol on top, pop removes the top symbol, and only the top is visible.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-pda-for-mathph20x\" data-h=\"3\">A PDA for <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span></h3>\n<p>The machine has a \"pushing\" phase and a \"popping\" phase. It starts by pushing <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>. In the pushing state, each 0 is read and pushed. On the first 1 it switches to the popping state, and each 1 pops one 0. Reading a 0 after a 1 is impossible, so such strings die. At the end, if the top of the stack is <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>, every 0 was matched and the machine accepts.</p>\n<p>Trace <span class=\"m\">0011</span>: push <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>; read 0, push (stack <span class=\"m\">\\bot 0</span>); read 0, push (<span class=\"m\">\\bot 00</span>); read 1, pop (<span class=\"m\">\\bot 0</span>); read 1, pop (<span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>); end of input with <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> on top: accept. Trace <span class=\"m\">001</span>: after reading everything, the stack is <span class=\"m\">\\bot 0</span>, one 0 unmatched: reject. Trace <span class=\"m\">011</span>: after one pop the stack is <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>, and the next 1 has nothing to pop: the machine is stuck and rejects.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-nondeterminism-palindromes\" data-h=\"3\">Using nondeterminism: palindromes</h3>\n<p>For <strong>marked palindromes</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\{wcw^R\\}</span>, where a special centre symbol <span class=\"m\">c</span> is present, a PDA pushes every symbol until it reads <span class=\"m\">c</span>, then pops and compares each following symbol with the top. This works deterministically. For <strong>ordinary even palindromes</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\{ww^R\\}</span> there is no centre marker, so the PDA must <strong>guess</strong> where the middle is: at every step it may nondeterministically jump from pushing to popping. On input <span class=\"m\">0110</span>, the branch that guesses the middle after <span class=\"m\">01</span> pushes 0 and 1, then matches 1 and 0, and accepts. It can be proved that no deterministic PDA recognises <span class=\"m\">\\{ww^R\\}</span>, so unlike finite automata, <strong>nondeterministic PDAs are strictly more powerful than deterministic ones</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"equivalence-with-grammars\" data-h=\"3\">Equivalence with grammars</h3>\n<p>The central theorem: a language is context-free if and only if some PDA recognises it.</p>\n<p>From a grammar to a PDA the idea is to <strong>simulate a leftmost derivation on the stack</strong>. Push <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> and the start variable. Then loop: if the top is a variable <span class=\"m\">A</span>, pop it and push the right side of some rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to w</span> (chosen nondeterministically); if the top is a terminal, read the next input symbol and check that it matches, popping it; if the top is <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>, accept. For <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span> and input <span class=\"m\">01</span>: the stack goes <span class=\"m\">\\bot S</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\bot 1S0</span> (rule <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1</span>, with 0 on top), match 0 to give <span class=\"m\">\\bot 1S</span>, replace <span class=\"m\">S</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> to give <span class=\"m\">\\bot 1</span>, match 1 to give <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>, accept.</p>\n<p>The direction from PDA to grammar is more technical: one creates a variable <span class=\"m\">A_{pq}</span> that generates all strings taking the PDA from state <span class=\"m\">p</span> with some stack to state <span class=\"m\">q</span> with the same stack. We state it without the full proof.</p>\n<h3 id=\"deterministic-context-free-languages\" data-h=\"3\">Deterministic context-free languages</h3>\n<p>A <strong>deterministic PDA</strong> has at most one move available in every situation. The languages they recognise, the deterministic context-free languages, include most programming-language syntax, and they can be parsed in linear time; this is what LR parsers do. They are closed under complement, unlike general context-free languages.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A PDA can only see the <strong>top</strong> of the stack; it cannot count the stack or look inside it. Do not forget the ε-moves: a transition may read nothing, pop nothing, or push nothing. Acceptance requires consuming the <strong>entire input</strong>; reaching an accepting state halfway does not count. Remember the bottom marker if your design needs to test for an empty stack. And since PDAs are nondeterministic by default, a PDA accepts if <strong>any</strong> branch accepts, so a design with guesses is correct as long as the right guess leads to acceptance and no guess accepts a wrong string.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A pushdown automaton is a finite automaton plus a stack, with moves that read, pop and push. The stack provides unbounded but last-in-first-out memory, enough to match <span class=\"m\">0^n</span> with <span class=\"m\">1^n</span>, balance brackets, or check palindromes by guessing the middle. Nondeterministic PDAs recognise exactly the context-free languages; a grammar is turned into a PDA by simulating leftmost derivations on the stack. Deterministic PDAs are strictly weaker, yet they cover most programming languages and parse in linear time.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-pda\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;0\\u207f1\\u207f&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;000111&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The stack grows by one 0 per 0 read and shrinks by one per 1. Acceptance means the bottom marker (shown as a dollar sign) is on top at the end.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step through <code>000111</code> and write down the stack after each symbol.\n2. Try <code>00111</code> and <code>00011</code>; find the exact moment each one fails.\n3. Try <code>0101</code>.\n4. Switch the machine to \"w c wᴿ (marked palindromes)\" and run <code>abcba</code>, then <code>abcab</code>.\n5. Switch to \"equal # of a and b\" and run <code>abba</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the stack height after reading the 0s equals the number of 0s, which is the unbounded count a DFA cannot keep. <code>00111</code> gets stuck when a 1 finds only the bottom marker; <code>00011</code> ends with a 0 left on the stack; <code>0101</code> fails because a 0 arrives after the popping phase has begun. The palindrome machine pushes the first half and compares the second half in reverse order, which is the stack's last-in-first-out rule at work. The equal-count machine keeps only the surplus symbol on the stack.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma_\\varepsilon\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon\\to\\mathcal P(Q\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">a,\\ X\\to Y:\\ \\text{read } a,\\ \\text{pop } X,\\ \\text{push } Y\\quad(\\text{any may be } \\varepsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFG} \\to \\text{PDA}:\\ \\text{top } A \\Rightarrow \\text{replace by } w \\ (A\\to w);\\ \\ \\text{top } a \\Rightarrow \\text{match input}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{regular} \\subsetneq \\text{DCFL} \\subsetneq \\text{CFL}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">PDAs and CFGs are equivalent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language is context-free if and only if some (nondeterministic) pushdown automaton recognises it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(CFG to PDA) Build the PDA in the article. Claim: it accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> iff <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow^* w</span> by a leftmost derivation. At every moment, the input read so far followed by the stack contents (top first, above <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>) is a sentential form of a leftmost derivation. Replacing the top variable performs one derivation step, and matching a terminal moves it from the stack to the \"read\" part. The stack reaches <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> with all input read exactly when the sentential form has become <span class=\"m\">w</span>. (PDA to CFG) For each pair of states <span class=\"m\">p, q</span>, a variable <span class=\"m\">A_{pq}</span> generates the strings that take the PDA from <span class=\"m\">p</span> with empty stack to <span class=\"m\">q</span> with empty stack; rules <span class=\"m\">A_{pq}\\to aA_{rs}b</span> (a push matched by a later pop) and <span class=\"m\">A_{pq}\\to A_{pr}A_{rq}</span> (the stack empties in between) capture every computation. The details are a careful induction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Nondeterminism matters for PDAs</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The language <span class=\"m\">\\{ww^R : w\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\}</span> is recognised by a nondeterministic PDA but by no deterministic PDA.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Trace the PDA for <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> on <span class=\"m\">0011</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Push <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>. Stack (bottom to top): <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 0, push 0: <span class=\"m\">\\bot 0</span>. Read 0, push 0: <span class=\"m\">\\bot 00</span>.</li>\n<li>Read 1, pop 0: <span class=\"m\">\\bot 0</span>. Read 1, pop 0: <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>.</li>\n<li>Input finished and <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> is on top: accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simulate the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to(S)S\\mid\\varepsilon</span> on the stack for input <span class=\"m\">()</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Start: stack <span class=\"m\">\\bot S</span> (top on the right).</li>\n<li>Replace <span class=\"m\">S</span> by <span class=\"m\">(S)S</span>, pushing so that <span class=\"m\">(</span> is on top: <span class=\"m\">\\bot S)S(</span>.</li>\n<li>Match input <span class=\"m\">(</span>: stack <span class=\"m\">\\bot S)S</span>. Replace <span class=\"m\">S</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\bot S)</span>.</li>\n<li>Match input <span class=\"m\">)</span>: stack <span class=\"m\">\\bot S</span>. Replace <span class=\"m\">S</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>. Input finished: accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Design a PDA for <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^{2n}\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>In the pushing state, on each 0 push <strong>two</strong> symbols <span class=\"m\">X</span> (two moves, the second with input ε).</li>\n<li>On the first 1 switch to popping, and pop one <span class=\"m\">X</span> per 1.</li>\n<li>Accept when the input is finished and <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> is on top.</li>\n<li>Check <span class=\"m\">0011</span>: after <span class=\"m\">00</span> the stack holds 4 <span class=\"m\">X</span>s, but only 2 ones arrive: 2 <span class=\"m\">X</span>s remain, reject. Check <span class=\"m\">001111</span>: four pops, accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Describe a PDA for <span class=\"m\">L = \\{a^ib^jc^k : i = j + k\\}</span> and trace it on <span class=\"m\">aaabcc</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Push one <span class=\"m\">A</span> for each <span class=\"m\">a</span>. Then pop one <span class=\"m\">A</span> for each <span class=\"m\">b</span>, and continue popping one <span class=\"m\">A</span> for each <span class=\"m\">c</span> (allowing the <span class=\"m\">b</span> phase to be skipped, but never a <span class=\"m\">b</span> after a <span class=\"m\">c</span>). Accept when the input ends with <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> on top.\nTrace <span class=\"m\">aaabcc</span>: push three <span class=\"m\">A</span>s (<span class=\"m\">\\bot AAA</span>); read <span class=\"m\">b</span>, pop (<span class=\"m\">\\bot AA</span>); read <span class=\"m\">c</span>, pop (<span class=\"m\">\\bot A</span>); read <span class=\"m\">c</span>, pop (<span class=\"m\">\\bot</span>); end of input with <span class=\"m\">\\bot</span> on top: accept. Indeed <span class=\"m\">3 = 1 + 2</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every function call in a running program pushes a stack frame and every return pops it, so a program's control flow is a pushdown system. LR parsers in compilers are deterministic PDAs, and XML and JSON validators use a stack to check that every opened element is closed in the right order.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Stack-augmented neural networks (such as neural stack RNNs) and research on whether Transformers can recognise Dyck languages study precisely this boundary: which models can simulate a pushdown automaton, and which behave only like finite automata.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Back button in a web browser is a stack: each page you visit is pushed, and Back pops the most recent one. Undo in a text editor works the same way.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What extra memory does a PDA have compared with an NFA?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">an unbounded stack</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a second tape</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a finite counter</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The stack is unbounded, but only its top is visible.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">After the PDA for <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> reads <span class=\"m\">00011</span>, how many 0s remain on the stack?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Three pushes and two pops.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which language needs nondeterminism in a PDA?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{wcw^R\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{ww^R\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Without a centre marker the PDA must guess where the middle is.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">PDAs (nondeterministic) recognise exactly the...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">regular languages</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">context-free languages</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">decidable languages</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>This is the CFG-PDA equivalence theorem.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the PDA for <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^{2n}\\}</span> that pushes two symbols per 0, how many symbols (excluding the bottom marker) are on the stack after reading <span class=\"m\">000</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Two per 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">When converting a CFG to a PDA, what happens when a terminal is on top of the stack?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">it is matched against the next input symbol and popped</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is replaced by a rule</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the PDA accepts</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Variables are expanded, terminals are matched.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "cfl-pumping-lemma": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"where-stacks-run-out\" data-h=\"3\">Where stacks run out</h3>\n<p>A stack can match one count against another: as many 1s as 0s, as many closing brackets as opening ones. But what about matching <strong>three</strong> counts, as in <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>? A PDA could push the <span class=\"m\">a</span>s and pop them against the <span class=\"m\">b</span>s, but then the stack is empty and the information about <span class=\"m\">n</span> is gone before the <span class=\"m\">c</span>s arrive. Intuition says no PDA can do it. To prove it we need a context-free version of the pumping lemma, and its proof uses parse trees instead of state diagrams.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-set-of-nesting-dolls\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a set of nesting dolls</h3>\n<p>Imagine a Russian nesting doll where, somewhere inside, a doll of the same design appears again, holding a smaller copy of the same design. You could swap the inner copy for the outer one, making the doll bigger, or remove a layer, making it smaller, and you would still have a valid nesting doll. A parse tree that is tall enough must contain the same variable twice on one path, one inside the other, and the same \"swap\" works. Because the outer copy surrounds the inner one on both sides, pumping adds material in <strong>two places at once</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-key-observation-tall-trees-repeat-a-variable\" data-h=\"3\">The key observation: tall trees repeat a variable</h3>\n<p>Suppose a grammar has <span class=\"m\">|V|</span> variables and every right side has at most <span class=\"m\">b\\ge2</span> symbols. A parse tree of height <span class=\"m\">h</span> (the number of edges on the longest root-to-leaf path) has at most <span class=\"m\">b^h</span> leaves. So if a string is long, its parse tree must be tall: if the string has at least <span class=\"m\">b^{|V|+1}</span> symbols, some path from the root has at least <span class=\"m\">|V|+1</span> variables on it besides the leaf. With only <span class=\"m\">|V|</span> distinct variables, some variable <span class=\"m\">R</span> appears twice on that path, by the pigeonhole principle.</p>\n<p>Look at the two occurrences. The upper <span class=\"m\">R</span> generates some substring <span class=\"m\">vxy</span>, and the lower <span class=\"m\">R</span>, inside it, generates the middle part <span class=\"m\">x</span>. So the whole string splits as <span class=\"m\">s = uvxyz</span>. Now the swaps: replace the lower <span class=\"m\">R</span>'s subtree with a copy of the upper one, and you get <span class=\"m\">uv^2xy^2z</span>. Replace the upper subtree with the lower one, and you get <span class=\"m\">uxz = uv^0xy^0z</span>. Repeating gives <span class=\"m\">uv^ixy^iz</span> for every <span class=\"m\">i\\ge0</span>, all in the language.</p>\n<p>For example, in <span class=\"m\">S\\to0S1\\mid\\varepsilon</span>, the path from the root of the tree for <span class=\"m\">0011</span> passes through <span class=\"m\">S</span> several times. Taking two consecutive occurrences gives <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, and pumping produces <span class=\"m\">0^{k}1^{k}</span> for every <span class=\"m\">k\\ge 1</span>, all still in the language. The two pieces must grow <strong>together</strong>, which is exactly the matching that grammars are good at.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pumping lemma (context-free)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is context-free, there is a <span class=\"m\">p</span> such that every <span class=\"m\">s\\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|s|\\ge p</span> splits as <span class=\"m\">s = uvxyz</span> with <span class=\"m\">|vy| &gt; 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">|vxy|\\le p</span>, and <span class=\"m\">uv^ixy^iz\\in L</span> for all <span class=\"m\">i\\ge0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-statement\" data-h=\"3\">The statement</h3>\n<p><strong>Pumping lemma for CFLs.</strong> If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is context-free, there is a number <span class=\"m\">p</span> (the pumping length) such that every <span class=\"m\">s\\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|s|\\ge p</span> can be written as <span class=\"m\">s = uvxyz</span> where</p>\n<ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|vy| &gt; 0</span> (at least one of the two pumped pieces is non-empty),</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">|vxy|\\le p</span> (the pumped region is short),</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">uv^ixy^iz\\in L</span> for every <span class=\"m\">i\\ge0</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Compare with the regular version: there, one piece <span class=\"m\">y</span> was pumped and it had to be near the start (<span class=\"m\">|xy|\\le p</span>). Here, two pieces <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> are pumped together, and the window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> can be <strong>anywhere</strong> in the string, but it is short.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-it-mathph58x-is-not-context-free\" data-h=\"3\">Using it: <span class=\"m\">a^nb^nc^n</span> is not context-free</h3>\n<p>The proof is again a game. Assume <span class=\"m\">L = \\{a^nb^nc^n : n\\ge0\\}</span> is context-free with pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>. Choose <span class=\"m\">s = a^pb^pc^p</span>. The opponent chooses any split with <span class=\"m\">|vy| &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">|vxy|\\le p</span>.</p>\n<p>Because the window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> has length at most <span class=\"m\">p</span>, it cannot contain both an <span class=\"m\">a</span> and a <span class=\"m\">c</span>: there are <span class=\"m\">p</span> <span class=\"m\">b</span>s between the last <span class=\"m\">a</span> and the first <span class=\"m\">c</span>. So <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> together contain at most two of the three letters. Pump with <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span>. The pumped string gains at least one extra symbol, but at least one of the three letters does not increase. So the three counts are no longer all equal, and <span class=\"m\">uv^2xy^2z\\notin L</span>. (If <span class=\"m\">v</span> or <span class=\"m\">y</span> contained two different letters, the pumped string would even have them out of order, such as <span class=\"m\">abab</span>, which is also not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>.) Contradiction: <span class=\"m\">L</span> is not context-free.</p>\n<p>With numbers: if <span class=\"m\">p = 3</span>, then <span class=\"m\">s = aaabbbccc</span>. A legal window like <span class=\"m\">vxy = abb</span> with <span class=\"m\">v = a</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = b</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = b</span> pumps to <span class=\"m\">aaaab\\,bbb\\,ccc</span>, which has 4 <span class=\"m\">a</span>s, 4 <span class=\"m\">b</span>s and only 3 <span class=\"m\">c</span>s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"more-non-context-free-languages\" data-h=\"3\">More non-context-free languages</h3>\n<p>The same style works for <span class=\"m\">\\{ww : w\\in\\{0,1\\}^*\\}</span> (choose <span class=\"m\">s = 0^p1^p0^p1^p</span>; the short window can only disturb nearby blocks, which breaks the \"two equal halves\" structure), for <span class=\"m\">\\{a^{n^2}\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{a^{2^n}\\}</span> over one letter (pumping adds a bounded amount that cannot keep up with the gaps), and for <span class=\"m\">\\{a^ib^jc^k : i\\le j\\le k\\}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closure-properties-revisited\" data-h=\"3\">Closure properties, revisited</h3>\n<p>Now we can settle the closure questions left open earlier. Context-free languages are <strong>not closed under intersection</strong>: <span class=\"m\">L_1 = \\{a^nb^nc^m\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_2 = \\{a^mb^nc^n\\}</span> are both context-free (each matches only two counts), but\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1\\cap L_2 = \\{a^nb^nc^n\\},</span>\nwhich is not context-free. They are also <strong>not closed under complement</strong>: if they were, then since they are closed under union, De Morgan's law <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cap L_2 = \\overline{\\overline{L_1}\\cup\\overline{L_2}}</span> would make them closed under intersection.</p>\n<p>There is one useful positive result: the <strong>intersection of a context-free language with a regular language is context-free</strong>. Run a PDA and a DFA side by side, using pairs of states and the PDA's stack, just like the product construction. This lets us simplify problems: to show a language <span class=\"m\">L</span> is not context-free, intersect it with a well-chosen regular language and show the result is not context-free.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-chomsky-hierarchy-so-far\" data-h=\"3\">The Chomsky hierarchy so far</h3>\n<p>We now have a ladder of language classes: <strong>regular</strong> (finite automata, regular expressions) inside <strong>context-free</strong> (grammars, PDAs). Above them sit context-sensitive languages and, at the top, the languages recognised by <strong>Turing machines</strong>, which we meet in the next unit. <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span> is a natural example sitting above context-free: a simple program can check it easily, which tells us that real computers are more powerful than PDAs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Remember that <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> are pumped <strong>the same number of times</strong>. Do not assume <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> is at the start of the string; it can be anywhere, so you must consider every position. Make sure your argument handles the cases where <span class=\"m\">v</span> or <span class=\"m\">y</span> is empty (only one of them has to be non-empty). As before, the lemma only proves that a language is <strong>not</strong> context-free. And watch out for languages that look non-context-free but are not, such as <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^mc^{n+m}\\}</span>, which has the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to aSc\\mid T</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to bTc\\mid\\varepsilon</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Because a long string has a tall parse tree, and a tall tree repeats a variable on some path, every long string in a context-free language splits as <span class=\"m\">uvxyz</span> with a short window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> in which <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> can be pumped together. Choosing <span class=\"m\">s = a^pb^pc^p</span> shows that matching three counts is impossible for CFLs. As consequences, context-free languages are not closed under intersection or complement, though intersecting with a regular language keeps them context-free.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-pda\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;0\\u207f1\\u207f&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0011&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Pumping in a context-free language adds material in two places at once: one 0 on the left and one 1 on the right.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>0011</code>, then pump <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span> together: run <code>000111</code> and <code>00001111</code>.\n2. Now pump only one side: run <code>00011</code> and <code>00111</code>.\n3. Pump down (<span class=\"m\">i = 0</span>): run <code>01</code>, then clear the input box to run the empty string.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> pumping both pieces together keeps the string in the language every time, as the lemma promises for <span class=\"m\">u = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = \\varepsilon</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = 1</span> in <span class=\"m\">0011</span>. Pumping only one side breaks the match, which is why the lemma insists <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> are pumped the same number of times. A language with three linked counts, like <span class=\"m\">a^nb^nc^n</span>, cannot be pumped this way, because two pieces can repair at most two of the counts.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ CFL} \\Rightarrow \\exists p\\ \\forall s\\in L,\\ |s|\\ge p:\\ s = uvxyz,\\ |vy|&gt;0,\\ |vxy|\\le p,\\ \\forall i\\ge0:\\ uv^ixy^iz\\in L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{height } h \\Rightarrow \\le b^h \\text{ leaves};\\qquad p = b^{|V|+1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\{a^nb^nc^m\\}\\cap\\{a^mb^nc^n\\} = \\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFL}\\cap\\text{regular} = \\text{CFL}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pumping lemma for context-free languages</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every context-free language <span class=\"m\">L</span> has a pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span> with the property above.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Take a grammar for <span class=\"m\">L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|V|</span> variables and at most <span class=\"m\">b\\ge2</span> symbols on any right side, and let <span class=\"m\">p = b^{|V|+1}</span>. Let <span class=\"m\">s\\in L</span> with <span class=\"m\">|s|\\ge p</span>, and choose a parse tree for <span class=\"m\">s</span> with the fewest nodes. A tree of height <span class=\"m\">h</span> has at most <span class=\"m\">b^h</span> leaves, so its height is at least <span class=\"m\">|V|+1</span>, and its longest path has at least <span class=\"m\">|V|+1</span> variables. Among the lowest <span class=\"m\">|V|+1</span> variables on that path, some variable <span class=\"m\">R</span> repeats. Let the upper <span class=\"m\">R</span> generate <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> and the lower <span class=\"m\">R</span> generate <span class=\"m\">x</span>, so <span class=\"m\">s = uvxyz</span>. Replacing subtrees gives <span class=\"m\">uv^ixy^iz</span> for all <span class=\"m\">i\\ge 0</span>. If <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> were both empty, replacing the upper subtree by the lower would give a smaller tree for <span class=\"m\">s</span>, contradicting minimality, so <span class=\"m\">|vy|&gt;0</span>. The subtree of the upper <span class=\"m\">R</span> has height at most <span class=\"m\">|V|+1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">|vxy|\\le b^{|V|+1} = p</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">CFLs are not closed under intersection or complement</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>There are context-free <span class=\"m\">L_1, L_2</span> with <span class=\"m\">L_1\\cap L_2</span> not context-free, and there is a context-free language whose complement is not context-free.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prove <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span> is not context-free with <span class=\"m\">p = 4</span> in mind</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Choose <span class=\"m\">s = a^4b^4c^4</span>, length <span class=\"m\">12\\ge4</span>.</li>\n<li>Any window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> of length <span class=\"m\">\\le4</span> lies within at most two adjacent blocks.</li>\n<li>Case <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> inside the <span class=\"m\">a</span>s and <span class=\"m\">b</span>s: pumping <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span> raises the <span class=\"m\">a</span> or <span class=\"m\">b</span> count but leaves 4 <span class=\"m\">c</span>s.</li>\n<li>Case <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> inside the <span class=\"m\">b</span>s and <span class=\"m\">c</span>s: the <span class=\"m\">a</span> count stays at 4 while another rises.</li>\n<li>In every case the counts become unequal, so the pumped string is not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">\\{a^ib^jc^k : i,j,k \\text{ all equal}\\}</span> is obtained from a CFL and a regular language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"m\">E</span> be the language of strings over <span class=\"m\">\\{a,b,c\\}</span> with equally many <span class=\"m\">a</span>s, <span class=\"m\">b</span>s and <span class=\"m\">c</span>s.</li>\n<li>Intersect with the regular language <span class=\"m\">a^*b^*c^*</span>: <span class=\"m\">E\\cap a^*b^*c^* = \\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">E</span> were context-free, this intersection would be context-free (CFL ∩ regular).</li>\n<li>It is not, so <span class=\"m\">E</span> is not context-free either.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^mc^{n+m}\\}</span> IS context-free</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each <span class=\"m\">a</span> is matched with a <span class=\"m\">c</span> at the far end, and each <span class=\"m\">b</span> with a <span class=\"m\">c</span> just inside: <span class=\"m\">S\\to aSc\\mid T</span>, <span class=\"m\">T\\to bTc\\mid\\varepsilon</span>.</li>\n<li>Derive <span class=\"m\">abcc</span>: <span class=\"m\">S\\Rightarrow aSc\\Rightarrow aTc\\Rightarrow abTcc\\Rightarrow abcc</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">n = m = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">n + m = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>A grammar exists, so the pumping lemma would never produce a contradiction for this language.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Use the pumping lemma to show that <span class=\"m\">L = \\{a^n b^n c^k : k \\ge n\\}</span> is not context-free. (Hint: choose <span class=\"m\">s = a^pb^pc^p</span> and consider where the window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> lies; you may need <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span> in one case.)</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Assume <span class=\"m\">L</span> is CFL with pumping length <span class=\"m\">p</span>, and take <span class=\"m\">s = a^pb^pc^p \\in L</span>. The window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> (length <span class=\"m\">\\le p</span>) cannot contain both <span class=\"m\">a</span>s and <span class=\"m\">c</span>s.\nCase 1: <span class=\"m\">v</span> or <span class=\"m\">y</span> contains an <span class=\"m\">a</span> or a <span class=\"m\">b</span> (so the window touches the <span class=\"m\">a</span> and <span class=\"m\">b</span> blocks). If <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> together contain both letters, or only one of <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">b</span>, consider <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span>. If the number of <span class=\"m\">a</span>s and the number of <span class=\"m\">b</span>s change by different amounts, they become unequal: not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>. If both rise equally (one <span class=\"m\">a</span>-piece and one equal <span class=\"m\">b</span>-piece), then <span class=\"m\">n</span> rises above <span class=\"m\">p</span> while the <span class=\"m\">c</span>s stay at <span class=\"m\">p</span>, so <span class=\"m\">k &lt; n</span>: not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>.\nCase 2: <span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> contain only <span class=\"m\">c</span>s. Pump down, <span class=\"m\">i = 0</span>: the number of <span class=\"m\">c</span>s drops below <span class=\"m\">p</span> while <span class=\"m\">n = p</span>, so <span class=\"m\">k &lt; n</span>: not in <span class=\"m\">L</span>.\n(Out-of-order strings such as <span class=\"m\">aabab\\cdots</span> are never in <span class=\"m\">L</span>.) Every split fails, so <span class=\"m\">L</span> is not context-free.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Type checking is not context-free: \"every variable must be declared before it is used\" requires matching a name with an arbitrarily distant earlier occurrence, like <span class=\"m\">\\{ww\\}</span>. That is why compilers parse with a context-free grammar and then check declarations and types in a separate semantic-analysis pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Benchmarks for sequence models include <span class=\"m\">a^nb^nc^n</span> precisely because it is just beyond context-free. Studies show LSTMs can learn to count several quantities, while some attention-only models struggle to generalise to longer <span class=\"m\">n</span>, which links the Chomsky hierarchy to neural architecture design.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A dance routine in which every forward step must later be matched by a backward step, and also by a turn, in the same order and number, requires keeping track of three linked counts. A single stack of reminders is not enough, which is the everyday meaning of <span class=\"m\">a^nb^nc^n</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many pieces are pumped in the CFL pumping lemma?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">one</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">two, together</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">three</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">v</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> are both repeated <span class=\"m\">i</span> times.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">s = a^pb^pc^p</span>, why can the window <span class=\"m\">vxy</span> not contain both an <span class=\"m\">a</span> and a <span class=\"m\">c</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">there are <span class=\"m\">p</span> <span class=\"m\">b</span>s between them and <span class=\"m\">|vxy|\\le p</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">because <span class=\"m\">|vy|&gt;0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">because <span class=\"m\">u</span> must be non-empty</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The window would need length at least <span class=\"m\">p + 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">s = a^3b^3c^3</span>, <span class=\"m\">v = a</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = b</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = b</span> and <span class=\"m\">i = 2</span>, how many symbols does the pumped string have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"11\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Two extra symbols are added to the original 9.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^m\\}\\cap\\{a^mb^nc^n\\}</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^n\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the empty set</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The first condition matches <span class=\"m\">a</span> with <span class=\"m\">b</span>, the second <span class=\"m\">b</span> with <span class=\"m\">c</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A grammar has right sides of length at most 2 and 3 variables. What pumping length <span class=\"m\">p = b^{|V|+1}</span> does the proof give?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"16\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^{3+1} = 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The intersection of a context-free language and a regular language is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">always context-free</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">always regular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">possibly not context-free</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Run the PDA and DFA in parallel with the product construction.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "turing-machines": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"what-is-a-computer-really\" data-h=\"3\">What is a computer, really?</h3>\n<p>Finite automata have a fixed memory, and pushdown automata have a stack they can only use from the top. Neither can check <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>, yet a five-line program can. So what is the right mathematical model of a real, general-purpose computer? In 1936, before electronic computers existed, Alan Turing answered this by imagining a human clerk doing a calculation with pencil and paper, and stripping the activity down to its bare essentials. The result, the <strong>Turing machine</strong>, is still the standard definition of \"algorithm\" in computer science.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-clerk-with-a-paper-strip\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a clerk with a paper strip</h3>\n<p>Picture a clerk sitting in front of an endless strip of paper divided into squares. Each square holds one symbol or is blank. The clerk looks at one square at a time, and has a rule book that depends only on the clerk's current \"state of mind\" (one of finitely many) and the symbol in the current square. Each rule says: write this symbol in the square, move one square left or right, and switch to that state of mind. That is all. There is no limit on how much paper is used, and the clerk can go back and reread or erase anything. Turing argued that any mechanical calculation a person can do can be broken down into steps like these.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>A Turing machine (TM) is a 7-tuple <span class=\"m\">(Q,\\Sigma,\\Gamma,\\delta,q_0,q_{\\text{acc}},q_{\\text{rej}})</span> where</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"m\">Q</span> is a finite set of states;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span> is the input alphabet, not containing the blank symbol <span class=\"m\">\\sqcup</span>;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\Gamma</span> is the tape alphabet, with <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma\\subseteq\\Gamma</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sqcup\\in\\Gamma</span>;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma\\to Q\\times\\Gamma\\times\\{L,R\\}</span> is the transition function;</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q_0</span> is the start state, and <span class=\"m\">q_{\\text{acc}}\\ne q_{\\text{rej}}</span> are the accept and reject states, where the machine halts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The input is written on the leftmost squares of the tape, the rest is blank, and the head starts on the first input symbol. A transition <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q, a) = (r, b, R)</span> means: in state <span class=\"m\">q</span> reading <span class=\"m\">a</span>, write <span class=\"m\">b</span>, move right, and go to state <span class=\"m\">r</span>.</p>\n<p>Three differences from a DFA are crucial. The machine can <strong>write</strong> on the tape. The head can move <strong>both ways</strong>. And the tape is <strong>unbounded</strong>, so memory never runs out. The price is that the machine might <strong>never halt</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Turing machine</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A finite-state control with an infinite tape and a read-write head. Each step reads the symbol under the head, writes a symbol, moves left or right, and changes state. It halts on entering the accept or reject state.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"configurations-and-computation\" data-h=\"3\">Configurations and computation</h3>\n<p>A snapshot of the machine is a <strong>configuration</strong>: the tape contents, the head position and the state. It is common to write it as a string <span class=\"m\">u\\,q\\,v</span>, meaning the tape holds <span class=\"m\">uv</span>, the machine is in state <span class=\"m\">q</span>, and the head is on the first symbol of <span class=\"m\">v</span>. For example, <span class=\"m\">10\\,q_3\\,11</span> means tape <span class=\"m\">1011</span>, state <span class=\"m\">q_3</span>, head on the third square. A computation is a sequence of configurations, each following from the last by one transition. On a given input there are three possible outcomes: the machine <strong>accepts</strong>, <strong>rejects</strong>, or <strong>loops</strong> forever.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Configuration</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The complete state of a TM at one moment: tape contents, head position and current state, written <span class=\"m\">u\\,q\\,v</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"example-1-adding-one-in-binary\" data-h=\"3\">Example 1: adding one in binary</h3>\n<p>A TM can compute functions, not only answer yes or no. To add 1 to a binary number, the machine first moves right to the end of the input. When it sees a blank, it steps back left and enters a \"carry\" state. In carry, a 1 becomes 0 and the carry continues left; a 0 becomes 1 and the machine halts; a blank (the number was all 1s) becomes 1 and it halts. On input <span class=\"m\">1011</span> (eleven), it walks right over 4 symbols, steps back, changes the last two 1s to 0s, changes the 0 to 1, and halts with <span class=\"m\">1100</span> (twelve) after 8 steps.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-2-deciding-mathph30x\" data-h=\"3\">Example 2: deciding <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span></h3>\n<p>To decide <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> the machine repeatedly <strong>crosses off</strong> one 0 and one matching 1. It replaces the leftmost 0 with <span class=\"m\">X</span>, scans right past 0s and <span class=\"m\">Y</span>s to the first 1, replaces it with <span class=\"m\">Y</span>, scans back left to the <span class=\"m\">X</span>, and repeats. When no 0s remain, it checks that only <span class=\"m\">Y</span>s are left and accepts; any stray symbol leads to reject. On <span class=\"m\">0011</span> it makes two crossing rounds and then a final check. The same idea, with three letters crossed off per round, decides <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>, which no PDA can do.</p>\n<h3 id=\"recognisers-and-deciders\" data-h=\"3\">Recognisers and deciders</h3>\n<p>A TM <strong>recognises</strong> the language <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> of inputs it accepts. On inputs not in <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> it may reject <em>or loop</em>. A TM that halts on <strong>every</strong> input is a <strong>decider</strong>. This distinction is central to the rest of the course:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A language is <strong>Turing-recognisable</strong> (recursively enumerable) if some TM recognises it.</li>\n<li>A language is <strong>decidable</strong> (recursive) if some TM decides it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Every decidable language is recognisable. The converse fails: in a few lessons we will meet the halting problem, which is recognisable but not decidable. A simple recogniser that is not a decider: \"scan right; if you find a 1, accept\". On <span class=\"m\">0001</span> it accepts, but on <span class=\"m\">000</span> it walks right over the blanks forever, never rejecting.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decider</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A Turing machine that halts (accepts or rejects) on every input.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Turing-recognisable language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language <span class=\"m\">L</span> for which some TM accepts exactly the strings in <span class=\"m\">L</span>; on other inputs it may reject or run forever. Also called recursively enumerable.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decidable language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language <span class=\"m\">L</span> for which some TM halts on every input and accepts exactly the strings in <span class=\"m\">L</span>. Also called recursive.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-powerful-are-such-simple-steps\" data-h=\"3\">How powerful are such simple steps?</h3>\n<p>It seems unbelievable that this primitive device can do everything a laptop can. But each laptop operation, reading memory, adding numbers, comparing, jumping, can be simulated by a (long) sequence of tape moves. The busy beaver shows how much can happen in tiny machines: a 3-state TM started on a blank tape can write six 1s in 14 steps and halt, and the maximum running times of small machines grow faster than any computable function. Turing machines are slow, but speed is not the point. The point is <strong>what can be computed at all</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A TM's tape is infinite in one direction in the standard model; if the head is on the leftmost square and moves left, it stays put. Halting happens only in <span class=\"m\">q_{\\text{acc}}</span> or <span class=\"m\">q_{\\text{rej}}</span>; reaching the end of the input does not stop the machine. A recogniser is allowed to loop on inputs it rejects, so \"recognisable\" is weaker than \"decidable\". And do not confuse the tape alphabet with the input alphabet: the blank and helper symbols like <span class=\"m\">X</span>, <span class=\"m\">Y</span> are in <span class=\"m\">\\Gamma</span> but not in <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A Turing machine is a finite control plus an unbounded tape and a head that reads, writes and moves both ways. Each step is determined by the state and the symbol under the head. On each input it accepts, rejects or loops. Machines that always halt are deciders. Turing machines can compute functions such as binary increment and decide languages such as <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>, beyond the power of PDAs, and they are the standard model of what an algorithm can do.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;binary increment (+1)&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1011}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Step the machine and watch the head, the state and the tape. The step counter shows the running time.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>1011</code> step by step and check that it ends with <code>1100</code> after 8 steps.\n2. Try <code>111</code> and watch the carry run off the left end of the number.\n3. Switch to \"decide 0ⁿ1ⁿ\" and run <code>0011</code>, watching the <span class=\"m\">X</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y</span> marks appear.\n4. Run \"decide 0ⁿ1ⁿ\" on <code>0010</code> and find the step where it rejects.\n5. Switch to \"loops when there is no 1\" and run <code>000</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the increment machine first walks to the end and then works backwards, just like adding by hand. On <code>111</code> the carry reaches the blank to the left of the number, which becomes a new leading 1 (seven plus one is eight, <code>1000</code>); the visual's tape extends in both directions, a harmless variant explained in the next lesson. The <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span> decider crosses off one pair per round. The last machine never halts on <code>000</code>: it is a recogniser but not a decider, the distinction from the article.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">M = (Q,\\Sigma,\\Gamma,\\delta,q_0,q_{\\text{acc}},q_{\\text{rej}}),\\qquad \\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma\\to Q\\times\\Gamma\\times\\{L,R\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{configuration } u\\,q\\,v:\\ \\text{tape } uv,\\ \\text{state } q,\\ \\text{head on first symbol of } v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{outcomes: accept, reject, loop};\\qquad \\text{decidable}\\subseteq\\text{recognisable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{regular}\\subsetneq\\text{CFL}\\subsetneq\\text{decidable}\\subsetneq\\text{recognisable}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Every context-free language is decidable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For every CFL <span class=\"m\">L</span> there is a Turing machine that decides <span class=\"m\">L</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Convert a grammar for <span class=\"m\">L</span> to Chomsky normal form. A TM can run the CYK algorithm (or, more simply, try every derivation of exactly <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> steps, of which there are finitely many) on its tape, and halts with the correct answer on every input. So <span class=\"m\">L</span> is decidable. Since <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span> is decidable but not context-free, the inclusion is strict.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">A TM can decide <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The crossing-off machine that marks one <span class=\"m\">a</span>, one <span class=\"m\">b</span> and one <span class=\"m\">c</span> per round, and finally checks that nothing unmarked remains, halts on every input and accepts exactly <span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run the binary-increment TM on <span class=\"m\">0111</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Walk right over <span class=\"m\">0, 1, 1, 1</span> (4 steps); see a blank, move left into carry (step 5).</li>\n<li>Carry: <span class=\"m\">1\\to0</span>, move left (step 6); <span class=\"m\">1\\to0</span> (step 7); <span class=\"m\">1\\to0</span> (step 8).</li>\n<li>Read <span class=\"m\">0</span>: write <span class=\"m\">1</span> and halt (step 9).</li>\n<li>Tape: <span class=\"m\">1000</span>. Seven plus one is eight.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Write the configurations of a TM that replaces every 0 by 1, on input <span class=\"m\">00</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Rule: in <span class=\"m\">q</span> on 0, write 1 and move R; in <span class=\"m\">q</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\sqcup</span>, accept.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">q\\,00</span> (start).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">1\\,q\\,0</span> after step 1, then <span class=\"m\">11\\,q\\,\\sqcup</span> after step 2.</li>\n<li>Step 3 reads the blank and accepts: <span class=\"m\">11\\sqcup\\,q_{\\text{acc}}</span>. Three steps in total.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Describe a Turing machine that decides <span class=\"m\">L = \\{w\\in\\{0,1\\}^* : w \\text{ has equally many 0s and 1s}\\}</span>, and estimate how many steps it takes on an input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Repeat: scan from the left for an unmarked symbol. If there is none, accept. If the first unmarked symbol is a 0, mark it <span class=\"m\">X</span> and scan right for an unmarked 1; if none exists, reject; otherwise mark it <span class=\"m\">X</span> and return to the left end. Do the symmetric thing if the first unmarked symbol is a 1.\nEach round marks two symbols and scans the tape at most twice, so at most <span class=\"m\">n/2</span> rounds of <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> steps each: <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> steps. The machine always halts, so it is a decider.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Turing machine is the reference model behind every statement of the form \"no algorithm can do X\" and behind the formal definitions of running time and memory used in algorithm analysis. Programming languages are called <strong>Turing-complete</strong> when they can simulate any Turing machine; this includes C, Python, SQL with recursion, and even spreadsheet formulas with LAMBDA.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>\"Can a neural network compute any algorithm?\" is a Turing-machine question. Results show that recurrent networks with unbounded precision, and Transformers with enough chain-of-thought steps, are Turing-complete in principle, while fixed-depth models without extra steps are much more limited.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A knitting pattern or a player-piano roll is a long tape of instructions read one step at a time. A Turing machine is the same idea, with the extra power to write on the tape and to move back and forth.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which of these can a Turing machine do that a DFA cannot?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">write on its tape and move left</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">read one input symbol per step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">have finitely many states</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The read-write, two-way, unbounded tape is the key difference.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many steps does the binary-increment TM take on <span class=\"m\">1011</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>4 to walk right, 1 to step back, 3 in the carry state.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A TM that halts on every input is called...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a recogniser</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a decider</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an enumerator</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Recognisers may loop on inputs they do not accept.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What are the three possible outcomes of running a TM on an input?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">accept, reject, loop</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">accept, reject, error</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">halt, pause, loop</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A TM may run forever without halting.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The 3-state busy beaver in the visual halts after how many steps?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"14\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It writes six 1s on a blank tape and halts after 14 steps.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which language is decidable but not context-free?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A crossing-off TM decides it; the CFL pumping lemma rules out a PDA.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "tm-variants": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-fine-print-matter\" data-h=\"3\">Does the fine print matter?</h3>\n<p>Our definition of a Turing machine made many arbitrary choices: one tape, infinite in one direction, a head that must move left or right, deterministic rules. What if we allowed several tapes, or a head that can stay put, or nondeterministic guessing? A model of computation is only convincing if such details do not change what can be computed. This lesson shows that every reasonable variant computes <strong>exactly the same class</strong> of languages and functions. This robustness is the main evidence that Turing machines capture computation itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-kitchen-with-more-counters\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a kitchen with more counters</h3>\n<p>A cook with one long counter can prepare the same dishes as a cook with three counters; it just takes more walking back and forth to fetch things. Extra counters make cooking faster, but they never make a new dish possible. Turing machine variants are like that: they can change running time, sometimes a lot, but never the set of problems that can be solved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simulation-the-method-of-proof\" data-h=\"3\">Simulation: the method of proof</h3>\n<p>To show that variant B is no more powerful than the standard model A, we show that <strong>A can simulate B</strong>: given any B-machine, build an A-machine that accepts, rejects and loops on exactly the same inputs. The converse direction (B can simulate A) is usually obvious, since B is an extension of A. When both directions hold, the models are <strong>equivalent</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stay-put-and-two-way-infinite-tapes\" data-h=\"3\">Stay-put and two-way infinite tapes</h3>\n<p>Allow the head to stay put (<span class=\"m\">S</span>) as well as move. A standard machine simulates a stay-put move by moving right and then immediately left, using an extra intermediate state. So one step becomes two, and nothing new is computed.</p>\n<p>Allow the tape to be infinite in <strong>both</strong> directions. A one-way tape simulates it by folding the tape in half: square <span class=\"m\">i \\ge 0</span> and square <span class=\"m\">-i-1</span> share one cell of the one-way tape, using a tape alphabet of pairs, and the control remembers which half it is currently working on. Again the running time grows by only a constant factor.</p>\n<h3 id=\"multitape-machines\" data-h=\"3\">Multitape machines</h3>\n<p>A <span class=\"m\">k</span>-tape TM has <span class=\"m\">k</span> tapes, each with its own head; a transition reads all <span class=\"m\">k</span> symbols at once and writes, moves and changes state accordingly:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\to Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\times\\{L,R,S\\}^k.</span>\nMultiple tapes make programs much easier. To decide <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> with two tapes, copy the 0s to tape 2, then move back along tape 2 while reading the 1s on tape 1, one-for-one. That takes about <span class=\"m\">2n</span> steps instead of the <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> back-and-forth of the single-tape crossing-off machine.</p>\n<p>The simulation by one tape stores all <span class=\"m\">k</span> tapes on one tape separated by a marker <span class=\"m\">\\#</span>, with a dot over the symbol where each virtual head is. To simulate one step, the single-tape machine scans the whole tape to read the dotted symbols, then scans again to update them, shifting the tape contents to the right if a virtual tape needs more room. If the multitape machine runs for <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> steps, each virtual tape has length at most <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span>, so one simulated step costs <span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span> single-tape steps and the whole simulation costs <span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span>. A quadratic slowdown, but the same answers.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Multitape Turing machine</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A TM with <span class=\"m\">k</span> tapes and <span class=\"m\">k</span> independent heads, whose transitions read and write all <span class=\"m\">k</span> tapes in one step. It is equivalent to a single-tape TM with at most quadratic slowdown.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"nondeterministic-turing-machines\" data-h=\"3\">Nondeterministic Turing machines</h3>\n<p>A <strong>nondeterministic TM</strong> (NTM) may have several possible moves in a configuration: <span class=\"m\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma\\to\\mathcal P(Q\\times\\Gamma\\times\\{L,R\\})</span>. Its computation is a <strong>tree</strong> of branches, and it accepts if <strong>some</strong> branch reaches the accept state.</p>\n<p>A deterministic machine simulates an NTM by exploring the tree <strong>breadth-first</strong>: first all branches of length 1, then of length 2, and so on. If some branch accepts after <span class=\"m\">t</span> steps, the search reaches it. Depth-first search would be wrong, because it could follow an infinite branch forever and never try the accepting one. If each configuration has at most <span class=\"m\">b</span> choices, there are at most <span class=\"m\">b^t</span> branches of length <span class=\"m\">t</span>, so the simulation takes about <span class=\"m\">b^{t}</span> steps: an <strong>exponential</strong> slowdown. Whether this slowdown is unavoidable is, essentially, the P versus NP question we meet in the last unit.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Nondeterministic Turing machine</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A TM whose transition function gives a set of possible moves. It accepts an input if some branch of its computation tree accepts.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"enumerators\" data-h=\"3\">Enumerators</h3>\n<p>An <strong>enumerator</strong> is a TM with a printer: it starts on a blank tape and prints a (possibly infinite) list of strings. A language is Turing-recognisable if and only if some enumerator prints exactly its strings. Given a recogniser <span class=\"m\">M</span>, an enumerator runs <span class=\"m\">M</span> for <span class=\"m\">1, 2, 3, \\dots</span> steps on the first <span class=\"m\">1, 2, 3, \\dots</span> strings in shortlex order, printing those accepted. This <strong>dovetailing</strong> trick makes sure no single looping computation blocks the others.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Enumerator</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A Turing machine with an output printer that lists strings; the language it enumerates is the set of strings it eventually prints.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-universal-turing-machine\" data-h=\"3\">The universal Turing machine</h3>\n<p>A TM is a finite object, so it can be <strong>encoded</strong> as a string <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span>, listing its states and transitions. Turing showed that there is a single <strong>universal Turing machine</strong> <span class=\"m\">U</span> that, given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M, w\\rangle</span>, simulates <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>: it accepts if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts, rejects if <span class=\"m\">M</span> rejects, and loops if <span class=\"m\">M</span> loops. This is the idea of the stored-program computer, invented on paper a decade before one was built: one fixed machine runs any program it is given as data.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Universal Turing machine</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A Turing machine <span class=\"m\">U</span> that takes an encoding <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span> of a machine and an input and simulates <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Equivalent does not mean equally fast: multitape-to-single-tape costs a quadratic factor, and nondeterministic-to-deterministic costs an exponential factor (as far as anyone knows). When simulating an NTM, use breadth-first search, not depth-first. Models that are genuinely <strong>weaker</strong>, such as a TM whose head can never move left (which is no stronger than a DFA) or a TM that may only use the squares holding the input (a linear bounded automaton), are not counterexamples to robustness; they are restrictions, not reasonable variants. And the universal machine is a single, fixed machine; it is not a different machine for each program.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Turing machines with stay-put moves, two-way infinite tapes, several tapes, or nondeterministic choices all recognise exactly the same languages as the basic model. Proofs work by simulation: multitape machines are simulated with <span class=\"m\">O(t^2)</span> overhead, and nondeterministic machines by breadth-first search with exponential overhead. Enumerators characterise the recognisable languages, and the universal Turing machine shows that one machine can run every program given to it as data.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;unary addition&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;111+11&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A single-tape machine that adds numbers written in unary. Count its steps and compare with what a multitape machine would need.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>111+11</code> and check that the result is <code>11111</code> and the step counter reads 8.\n2. Try <code>1+1</code> and <code>1111+111</code>, recording the number of steps each time.\n3. Switch to \"decide palindromes\" and run <code>0110</code>, then <code>01110</code>, watching the head travel back and forth.\n4. Try a palindrome of length 8, such as <code>01111110</code>, and compare its step count with the length-4 case.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> unary addition takes about <span class=\"m\">n</span> steps for an input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, since one pass suffices. The palindrome machine, with only one tape, must run to the far end and back for every pair of symbols, so its step count grows roughly like <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>: doubling the length roughly quadruples the time. A two-tape machine could copy the input and compare it with its reverse in about <span class=\"m\">3n</span> steps, the speed-up (but not extra power) that multitape machines provide.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">k\\text{-tape}: \\ \\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\to Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\times\\{L,R,S\\}^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multitape time } t(n) \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{single-tape time } O(t(n)^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NTM time } t(n),\\ \\le b \\text{ choices} \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{deterministic time } 2^{O(t(n))}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">U(\\langle M,w\\rangle) = M(w)\\quad(\\text{accept / reject / loop})</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Multitape machines are equivalent to single-tape machines</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every <span class=\"m\">k</span>-tape TM that runs in time <span class=\"m\">t(n)\\ge n</span> has an equivalent single-tape TM running in time <span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Store the <span class=\"m\">k</span> tapes consecutively on one tape, separated by <span class=\"m\">\\#</span>, with dotted symbols marking head positions. To simulate one step: scan from the left end to the last <span class=\"m\">\\#</span> to learn the <span class=\"m\">k</span> symbols under the virtual heads (remembered in the finite control), then scan again, writing new symbols and moving dots. If a dot moves onto a <span class=\"m\">\\#</span>, shift the rest of the tape one cell right to make room. After <span class=\"m\">t</span> steps each virtual tape has at most <span class=\"m\">t + n</span> used cells, so one scan costs <span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span> steps and <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> simulated steps cost <span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Nondeterministic machines are equivalent to deterministic ones</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every nondeterministic TM has an equivalent deterministic TM. If the NTM is a decider, so is the deterministic machine.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Use three tapes: the input (never changed), a simulation tape, and an address tape that lists a sequence of choices <span class=\"m\">c_1c_2\\cdots c_t</span> with each <span class=\"m\">c_i \\le b</span>. For each address in order of length (breadth-first), copy the input to the simulation tape and follow the choices; accept if this branch accepts. If some branch accepts, its address is eventually tried. If every branch of the NTM halts, the tree is finite (by König's lemma), so the search can detect when all branches of the current length have halted and reject.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many steps does the one-tape simulation of a 2-tape machine take, roughly, if the 2-tape machine runs for 1,000 steps?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each simulated step scans a tape of length <span class=\"m\">O(t)</span>, about <span class=\"m\">1{,}000</span> cells (up to a constant).</li>\n<li>There are <span class=\"m\">1{,}000</span> simulated steps.</li>\n<li>Total: about <span class=\"m\">1{,}000\\times1{,}000 = 10^6</span> steps, up to a constant factor. Quadratic, not exponential.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">How many branches of length 10 must a breadth-first simulation explore if an NTM has 2 choices per step?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each step doubles the number of branches.</li>\n<li>After 10 steps: <span class=\"m\">2^{10} = 1{,}024</span> branches.</li>\n<li>Summing all lengths 1 to 10 gives <span class=\"m\">2 + 4 + \\cdots + 1024 = 2{,}046</span> branch prefixes.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A <strong>queue machine</strong> is like a PDA, but its memory is a queue (first in, first out) instead of a stack. Argue that queue machines are as powerful as Turing machines.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Store the TM's tape contents in the queue, with a special marker showing the head position. To simulate one TM step, rotate the queue: repeatedly remove the front symbol and add it to the back, until the marked symbol is reached; apply the transition (write the new symbol and move the marker left or right), then keep rotating to complete the cycle. Moving right is easy; moving left needs the machine to remember the previous symbol in its finite control during the rotation (or do a second full rotation). Since a queue machine can simulate any TM, and a TM can easily simulate a queue on its tape, the models are equivalent.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The universal Turing machine is the theoretical ancestor of interpreters, virtual machines (such as the JVM and Python's bytecode interpreter) and emulators. Equivalence of models also justifies analysing algorithms on the RAM model: anything a RAM computes in time <span class=\"m\">t</span>, a Turing machine computes in polynomial time in <span class=\"m\">t</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Neural Turing Machines and Differentiable Neural Computers add a differentiable read-write memory to a neural network, inspired directly by the TM's tape, so that the network can learn algorithms such as copying and sorting sequences.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Your phone can run an emulator of a 1980s game console, and that console's games run correctly inside it. One general-purpose machine simulating another, at some cost in speed, is the universal Turing machine idea in everyday life.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Adding more tapes to a Turing machine...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">lets it decide more languages</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">can make it faster but does not change what it can compute</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">makes it weaker</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every multitape TM can be simulated by a single-tape TM.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If a 3-tape TM runs in <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> steps, the single-tape simulation runs in about...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(2^{t(n)})</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each simulated step scans a tape of length <span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why must the deterministic simulation of an NTM use breadth-first search?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">depth-first search could follow an infinite branch forever</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">breadth-first search is faster in every case</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">NTMs have no infinite branches</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>BFS guarantees that an accepting branch of finite length is found.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An NTM has 3 choices per step. How many branches of length 4 are there?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"81\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3^4 = 81</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many steps does the unary addition TM take on <span class=\"m\">111+11</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"8\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Three 1s, the plus, two 1s, one step back, and one erase.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What does a universal Turing machine take as input?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">only a string <span class=\"m\">w</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">an encoding of a machine <span class=\"m\">M</span> together with an input <span class=\"m\">w</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a list of all Turing machines</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">U</span> simulates <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>: one fixed machine runs every program.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "church-turing-thesis": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-algorithm-mean\" data-h=\"3\">What does \"algorithm\" mean?</h3>\n<p>For centuries mathematicians used algorithms (long division, Euclid's method for the greatest common divisor) without defining the word. That was fine as long as they only wanted to show that something <strong>can</strong> be computed: you just present the method. But in 1900 David Hilbert asked, as the tenth of his famous problems, for \"a process according to which it can be determined in a finite number of operations\" whether a polynomial equation with integer coefficients has an integer solution. To prove that <strong>no</strong> such process exists, you need a precise definition of every possible process. The <strong>Church-Turing thesis</strong> supplies it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-judging-a-recipe-contest\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine judging a recipe contest</h3>\n<p>Suppose a contest asks: \"Is there any recipe at all that turns these ingredients into a soufflé?\" To answer yes, you show one recipe. To answer no, you must rule out every conceivable recipe, which is hopeless unless you first agree on what counts as a recipe: which kitchen actions are allowed. The Church-Turing thesis is that agreement for computation: the allowed actions are the steps of a Turing machine.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-statement\" data-h=\"3\">The statement</h3>\n<p><strong>Church-Turing thesis.</strong> A function is computable by an effective, step-by-step mechanical procedure (an algorithm) if and only if it is computable by a Turing machine.</p>\n<p>It is called a <strong>thesis</strong>, not a theorem, because one side, \"effective mechanical procedure\", is an informal idea. It cannot be proved mathematically, any more than one can prove that a definition of \"continuous\" captures our intuition of an unbroken curve. It can, however, be <strong>supported by evidence</strong>, and the evidence is overwhelming.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Church-Turing thesis</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The hypothesis that the intuitive notion of an algorithm coincides with Turing machine computability: anything that can be computed by a mechanical procedure can be computed by a Turing machine.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Algorithm</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A finite, precise list of instructions that can be followed mechanically, step by step, without insight or guessing. By the Church-Turing thesis, equivalently a Turing machine that halts on every input.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-evidence\" data-h=\"3\">The evidence</h3>\n<p><strong>Independent models agree.</strong> In the 1930s several people, working separately, proposed definitions of computability: Alonzo Church's lambda calculus (functions built from abstraction and application), Kurt Gödel and Stephen Kleene's general recursive functions (functions built from zero, successor, composition, recursion and minimisation), Emil Post's production systems, and Turing's machines. These look nothing alike, yet every one of them was proved to define <strong>exactly the same</strong> class of functions. Later models, such as register machines, cellular automata (including Conway's Game of Life), and every general-purpose programming language, also land in exactly this class.</p>\n<p><strong>Robustness.</strong> As the previous lesson showed, Turing machines can be extended with extra tapes, nondeterminism and other features without becoming more powerful.</p>\n<p><strong>Turing's analysis.</strong> Turing's own argument looked at what a human computer (a clerk) can do: look at a bounded region of paper, be in one of finitely many states of mind, and make small local changes. He argued that any such process can be simulated by his machines.</p>\n<p><strong>No counterexample.</strong> In nearly a century, nobody has found a procedure that everyone agrees is mechanical but that no Turing machine can perform.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-we-use-the-thesis\" data-h=\"3\">How we use the thesis</h3>\n<p>The thesis has a practical consequence: we may describe algorithms in ordinary language or pseudocode, and trust that a Turing machine could carry them out. From now on, a \"Turing machine\" description will usually be a high-level algorithm, such as \"convert the grammar to Chomsky normal form and run CYK\". Inputs that are not strings, such as graphs, grammars or other machines, are first <strong>encoded</strong> as strings; we write <span class=\"m\">\\langle G\\rangle</span> for the encoding of an object <span class=\"m\">G</span>. Any reasonable encoding works, since a TM can translate between them.</p>\n<p>The other consequence is the dramatic one. If we prove that no Turing machine solves a problem, then by the thesis <strong>no algorithm of any kind</strong> solves it, in any programming language, on any computer that will ever be built. Hilbert's tenth problem was settled this way in 1970: Yuri Matiyasevich, building on work of Martin Davis, Hilary Putnam and Julia Robinson, proved that no algorithm decides whether a polynomial equation has integer solutions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"computable-functions-grow-but-not-too-fast\" data-h=\"3\">Computable functions grow, but not too fast</h3>\n<p>To get a feel for the boundary, consider the <strong>busy beaver</strong> function. Among all <span class=\"m\">n</span>-state, 2-symbol Turing machines that eventually halt when started on a blank tape, let <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span> be the largest number of steps any of them takes. Known values are <span class=\"m\">S(1) = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">S(2) = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">S(3) = 21</span>, <span class=\"m\">S(4) = 107</span> and, proved by a large collaborative project in 2024, <span class=\"m\">S(5) = 47{,}176{,}870</span>. The function <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span> is perfectly well defined, yet it grows faster than every computable function, so by the thesis no algorithm can compute it. The 3-state machine in the visual below writes six 1s in 14 steps; it is not the longest-running 3-state machine, but six 1s is the most any 3-state machine can write.</p>\n<h3 id=\"physical-and-complexity-versions\" data-h=\"3\">Physical and complexity versions</h3>\n<p>The original thesis is about <strong>what</strong> can be computed. A stronger and more controversial claim, the <strong>extended (or strong) Church-Turing thesis</strong>, says that every reasonable model can be simulated by a Turing machine with only <strong>polynomial</strong> slowdown. This is what makes complexity classes like P model-independent. Quantum computers are the main challenge to the extended thesis: they are not believed to compute anything a Turing machine cannot, but they may compute some things, such as factoring large numbers with Shor's algorithm, exponentially faster.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The thesis is not a theorem and is not \"proved\" by the equivalence results; those results are evidence. It is about computability, not efficiency: the plain thesis says nothing about speed. It does not say that the human mind or the physical universe is a Turing machine; it says that <em>mechanical procedures</em> are captured by Turing machines. And \"computable\" here means computable in principle with unlimited time and memory, not feasible in practice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The Church-Turing thesis identifies the informal idea of an algorithm with the precise idea of a Turing machine. It cannot be proved, but it is supported by the equivalence of every proposed model of computation, by the robustness of Turing machines, and by Turing's analysis of human calculation. It lets us describe algorithms at a high level and, crucially, lets us prove that some problems have no algorithm at all. The extended thesis adds a claim about polynomial-time simulation, which quantum computing may challenge.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;3-state busy beaver&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A 3-state Turing machine started on a blank tape. It halts after 14 steps having written six 1s.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step through the machine and count the 1s on the tape after each step.\n2. Watch the transition table: which entries are used, and which entry finally halts the machine?\n3. Switch to \"loops when there is no 1\" with input <code>000</code> and let it run.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> a tiny machine with three states and two symbols already behaves in a complicated, hard-to-predict way, and it is far from obvious in advance that it halts. For larger <span class=\"m\">n</span>, deciding which <span class=\"m\">n</span>-state machines halt becomes impossible for any algorithm, which is why the busy beaver function is uncomputable. The looping machine is the other side of the story: to know it will run forever, you have to reason about it; just watching never gives you an answer.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{algorithm} \\ \\overset{\\text{thesis}}{\\Longleftrightarrow}\\ \\text{Turing machine that halts on every input}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{TM} \\equiv \\lambda\\text{-calculus} \\equiv \\mu\\text{-recursive functions} \\equiv \\text{RAM} \\equiv \\text{any general programming language}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S(1)=1,\\ S(2)=6,\\ S(3)=21,\\ S(4)=107,\\ S(5)=47{,}176{,}870</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{extended thesis: model time } t \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{TM time } t^{O(1)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Equivalence of classical models</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The functions computable by Turing machines, by the lambda calculus, and by general recursive functions are exactly the same.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The busy beaver function is not computable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>No Turing machine computes <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span>, and for every computable function <span class=\"m\">f</span> we have <span class=\"m\">S(n) &gt; f(n)</span> for all sufficiently large <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(Sketch of why <span class=\"m\">S</span> is uncomputable.) If some TM computed <span class=\"m\">S</span>, we could decide whether an <span class=\"m\">n</span>-state machine <span class=\"m\">M</span> halts on a blank tape: compute <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span>, run <span class=\"m\">M</span> for <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span> steps, and answer \"halts\" if it has halted by then and \"loops\" otherwise, since any halting <span class=\"m\">n</span>-state machine halts within <span class=\"m\">S(n)</span> steps. In the next lessons we prove that this halting question is undecidable, so <span class=\"m\">S</span> cannot be computable.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Describe a \"high-level\" TM for deciding whether a graph is connected</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Input: an encoding <span class=\"m\">\\langle G\\rangle</span> of a graph, such as a list of vertices and edges.</li>\n<li>Mark the first vertex.</li>\n<li>Repeat: mark every unmarked vertex joined by an edge to a marked vertex, until nothing new is marked.</li>\n<li>Accept if every vertex is marked, otherwise reject.</li>\n<li>Each stage is clearly mechanical, so by the Church-Turing thesis a TM can do it. (It also halts: at most <span class=\"m\">|V|</span> rounds.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">S(2)\\ge 6</span> by running a 2-state machine</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Machine: <span class=\"m\">A</span> on 0: write 1, R, go to <span class=\"m\">B</span>. <span class=\"m\">A</span> on 1: write 1, L, go to <span class=\"m\">B</span>. <span class=\"m\">B</span> on 0: write 1, L, go to <span class=\"m\">A</span>. <span class=\"m\">B</span> on 1: write 1, R, halt.</li>\n<li>Steps: <span class=\"m\">A</span> writes 1, R (1); <span class=\"m\">B</span> on 0 writes 1, L (2); <span class=\"m\">A</span> on 1 writes 1, L (3); <span class=\"m\">B</span> on 0 writes 1, L (4); <span class=\"m\">A</span> on 0 writes 1, R (5); <span class=\"m\">B</span> on 1 halts (6).</li>\n<li>It halts after 6 steps with four 1s on the tape, so <span class=\"m\">S(2)\\ge6</span> (in fact <span class=\"m\">S(2) = 6</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Explain why the Church-Turing thesis is needed to conclude that \"no computer program in any language can solve problem X\" from a proof that \"no Turing machine decides X\". Why can the thesis not be proved?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>The proof about Turing machines is a mathematical theorem about one formal model. Programs in Python, C or any future language are not Turing machines by definition. To transfer the result, we need that every such program can be simulated by a TM. For each existing language this can be proved, but for \"any algorithm, by any method\" we rely on the thesis that every mechanical procedure is TM-computable.\nIt cannot be proved because \"mechanical procedure\" is an informal concept; a proof needs both sides defined precisely, and any precise definition would just be another formal model whose equivalence to TMs is again a theorem, not the thesis itself.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every claim that a language is \"Turing-complete\" (C, Python, SQL with recursive queries, TeX, even CSS with user interaction and the Game of Life) means it can simulate a Turing machine, and by the thesis it can therefore express every algorithm. This is also why compilers can translate between languages: they all compute the same functions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The thesis frames what learning systems can and cannot do: a trained neural network, however large, is a computable function, so it cannot solve problems that are uncomputable, such as deciding in general whether a program halts. Results on Turing-completeness of Transformers with chain of thought are statements about which side of this boundary a model sits on.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A cooking recipe, a tax form, and the instructions for assembling furniture are all algorithms in the everyday sense: mechanical steps requiring no insight. The Church-Turing thesis claims that any such procedure, however complicated, could be carried out by a Turing machine.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is the Church-Turing thesis called a thesis and not a theorem?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">it relates a formal model to the informal notion of an algorithm</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it has been disproved</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it only holds for one-tape machines</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One side is not mathematically defined, so it cannot be proved.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which of these is NOT evidence for the thesis?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the lambda calculus and TMs compute the same functions</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">multitape TMs are no more powerful than single-tape TMs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">Turing machines are fast in practice</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The thesis is about what is computable, not about speed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">S(4)</span>, the maximum number of steps of a halting 4-state busy beaver?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"107\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Proved in the 1980s; <span class=\"m\">S(5) = 47{,}176{,}870</span> was proved in 2024.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The extended Church-Turing thesis claims that reasonable models simulate each other with...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">no slowdown</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">polynomial slowdown</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">exponential slowdown</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>This makes complexity classes such as P model-independent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many steps does the 2-state machine in the worked example take before halting?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It shows <span class=\"m\">S(2)\\ge 6</span>, and in fact <span class=\"m\">S(2) = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Hilbert's tenth problem asks for an algorithm to decide whether polynomial equations have integer solutions. What was the answer?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no such algorithm exists</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes, using Gaussian elimination</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is still open</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Matiyasevich (1970), building on Davis, Putnam and Robinson.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "decidability": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"questions-about-machines-as-inputs\" data-h=\"3\">Questions about machines as inputs</h3>\n<p>So far our machines have answered questions about strings. Now we turn it around and ask questions about <strong>machines themselves</strong>: does this DFA accept this string? Does this grammar generate anything at all? Do these two DFAs accept the same language? Because machines can be encoded as strings, each question becomes a language, and we can ask whether that language is <strong>decidable</strong>: is there an algorithm that always halts with the correct yes-or-no answer? This lesson collects the problems that <strong>are</strong> decidable, and ends with a counting argument showing that undecidable problems must exist.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-quality-control-office\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a quality-control office</h3>\n<p>Picture an office that receives blueprints of vending machines and must answer questions about each one: \"Will this design ever give out a drink?\" or \"Does this new design behave exactly like the old one?\" The office cannot build each machine and test it forever. It needs a method that looks at the blueprint and always finishes with an answer. For finite automata, such methods exist. For Turing machines, as we will see next, they mostly do not.</p>\n<h3 id=\"problems-as-languages\" data-h=\"3\">Problems as languages</h3>\n<p>Each problem is a set of encoded instances whose answer is yes. For example\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{DFA}} = \\{\\langle B, w\\rangle : B \\text{ is a DFA that accepts } w\\}.</span>\nDeciding <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span> means building one TM that, given any pair <span class=\"m\">\\langle B,w\\rangle</span>, halts and says whether <span class=\"m\">B</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>. Inputs that are not valid encodings are simply rejected.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decision problem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A question with a yes-or-no answer about an input object, identified with the language of encodings of yes-instances.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"acceptance-problems\" data-h=\"3\">Acceptance problems</h3>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span> is decidable.</strong> Simulate <span class=\"m\">B</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>: keep track of the current state, follow one transition per symbol, and at the end check whether the state is accepting. This takes <span class=\"m\">|w|</span> steps of simulation and always halts. With the DFA for \"divisible by 3\" and <span class=\"m\">w = 110</span>, the states visited are <span class=\"m\">0, 1, 0, 0</span>; the final state is accepting, so <span class=\"m\">\\langle B, 110\\rangle \\in A_{\\text{DFA}}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{NFA}}</span> and <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{REX}}</span> are decidable</strong> too: convert the NFA to a DFA with the subset construction, or the regular expression to an NFA with Thompson's construction and then to a DFA, and use the previous decider. Reducing a new problem to one already solved is a pattern we will use constantly.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{CFG}}</span> is decidable</strong>: convert the grammar to Chomsky normal form and run CYK, or list every derivation with exactly <span class=\"m\">2n-1</span> steps. Either way the procedure halts. This proves again that every context-free language is decidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"emptiness-problems\" data-h=\"3\">Emptiness problems</h3>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}} = \\{\\langle B\\rangle : L(B) = \\varnothing\\}</span> is decidable.</strong> A DFA accepts some string exactly when some accepting state can be reached from the start. So mark the start state, repeatedly mark every state that has an arrow from a marked state, and when nothing changes, accept <span class=\"m\">\\langle B\\rangle</span> if no accepting state is marked. This is graph search and takes at most <span class=\"m\">|Q|</span> rounds.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{CFG}}</span> is decidable.</strong> Mark every terminal. Repeatedly mark a variable <span class=\"m\">A</span> if some rule <span class=\"m\">A\\to X_1\\cdots X_k</span> has every <span class=\"m\">X_i</span> marked (so <span class=\"m\">A</span> can generate a terminal string). When nothing changes, the language is empty exactly when the start variable is unmarked.</p>\n<h3 id=\"equivalence-of-dfas\" data-h=\"3\">Equivalence of DFAs</h3>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{DFA}} = \\{\\langle B, C\\rangle : L(B) = L(C)\\}</span> is decidable.</strong> Two languages are equal exactly when their <strong>symmetric difference</strong> is empty:\n<span class=\"mb\">L(B)\\,\\triangle\\,L(C) = \\big(L(B)\\cap\\overline{L(C)}\\big)\\cup\\big(\\overline{L(B)}\\cap L(C)\\big) = \\varnothing.</span>\nRegular languages are closed under complement, intersection and union, so we can build a DFA for the symmetric difference with the product construction and run the <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}}</span> decider. Another method: minimise both DFAs and check whether the results are identical up to renaming.</p>\n<p>By contrast, <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{CFG}}</span>, the question whether two grammars generate the same language, is <strong>undecidable</strong>. Context-free languages are not closed under complement or intersection, so the trick above fails, and in fact no algorithm exists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"recognisable-and-co-recognisable\" data-h=\"3\">Recognisable and co-recognisable</h3>\n<p>A language is <strong>co-recognisable</strong> if its complement is recognisable. A key theorem: <strong>a language is decidable if and only if it is both recognisable and co-recognisable.</strong> Given a recogniser for <span class=\"m\">L</span> and one for <span class=\"m\">\\overline L</span>, run them in parallel, one step each in turn. Every input is in exactly one of the two languages, so one of the machines eventually accepts, and we answer accordingly. This gives a convenient strategy: if <span class=\"m\">L</span> is recognisable but not decidable, its complement cannot be recognisable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"most-languages-are-not-recognisable\" data-h=\"3\">Most languages are not recognisable</h3>\n<p>Here is a surprising counting fact. Every Turing machine has a finite description, so the set of all TMs is <strong>countable</strong>: we can list them <span class=\"m\">M_1, M_2, M_3, \\dots</span> in shortlex order of their encodings. But the set of all languages over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> is <strong>uncountable</strong>. Suppose we could list all languages <span class=\"m\">L_1, L_2, \\dots</span>, and list all strings <span class=\"m\">s_1, s_2, \\dots</span>. Define the diagonal language <span class=\"m\">D = \\{s_i : s_i\\notin L_i\\}</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">D</span> differs from each <span class=\"m\">L_i</span> on the string <span class=\"m\">s_i</span>, so <span class=\"m\">D</span> is not on the list, a contradiction. Since there are only countably many recognisers and uncountably many languages, <strong>some languages are not recognised by any Turing machine</strong>, indeed almost all of them. The next lesson finds a specific, natural one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>\"Decidable\" requires halting on <strong>every</strong> input, including the no-instances; a procedure that runs forever when the answer is no is only a recogniser. Simulating a machine is a decider only when the simulation is guaranteed to stop, which is true for DFAs (<span class=\"m\">|w|</span> steps) but not for TMs. Do not assume a problem that is decidable for DFAs stays decidable for CFGs: acceptance and emptiness survive, but equivalence does not. And the counting argument shows that undecidable languages exist but does not name one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Questions about automata and grammars become languages of encodings. Acceptance, emptiness and equivalence are decidable for DFAs, acceptance and emptiness are decidable for CFGs, and equivalence for CFGs is undecidable. A language is decidable exactly when it and its complement are both recognisable. Because Turing machines are countable but languages are uncountable, most languages cannot be recognised by any machine.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"dfa\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;divisible by 3&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Deciding A_DFA is just simulation: follow one arrow per symbol and check the final state.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Type <code>110</code> and check the path <span class=\"m\">0\\to1\\to0\\to0</span> from the article.\n2. Type a few long strings and notice the simulation always finishes after one step per symbol.\n3. Without typing anything, find which states are reachable from the start; is any accepting state reachable?</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the simulation halts after exactly <span class=\"m\">|w|</span> steps, so the procedure is a decider for <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span>. Step 3 is the <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}}</span> algorithm: the accepting state is reachable (it is the start state itself), so the language is non-empty. For a Turing machine, neither trick works, because a simulation may never end.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{DFA}},\\ A_{\\text{NFA}},\\ A_{\\text{REX}},\\ E_{\\text{DFA}},\\ EQ_{\\text{DFA}},\\ A_{\\text{CFG}},\\ E_{\\text{CFG}}\\ \\text{are decidable};\\quad EQ_{\\text{CFG}} \\text{ is undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(B) = L(C) \\iff \\big(L(B)\\cap\\overline{L(C)}\\big)\\cup\\big(\\overline{L(B)}\\cap L(C)\\big) = \\varnothing</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ decidable} \\iff L \\text{ and } \\overline L \\text{ are recognisable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|\\{\\text{TMs}\\}| = \\aleph_0 &lt; 2^{\\aleph_0} = |\\mathcal P(\\Sigma^*)|</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decidable problems about regular languages and CFLs</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{NFA}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{REX}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{DFA}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{CFG}}</span> and <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{CFG}}</span> are decidable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decidable iff recognisable and co-recognisable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">L</span> is decidable if and only if both <span class=\"m\">L</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\overline L</span> are Turing-recognisable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">L</span> is decided by <span class=\"m\">M</span>, then <span class=\"m\">M</span> recognises <span class=\"m\">L</span>, and <span class=\"m\">M</span> with accept and reject swapped recognises (indeed decides) <span class=\"m\">\\overline L</span>. Conversely, let <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> recognise <span class=\"m\">L</span> and <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> recognise <span class=\"m\">\\overline L</span>. On input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, alternate steps of <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">w</span> lies in <span class=\"m\">L</span> or in <span class=\"m\">\\overline L</span>, one of them eventually accepts. If <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> accepts, accept; if <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> accepts, reject. This machine always halts and decides <span class=\"m\">L</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Some languages are not Turing-recognisable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>There are languages over <span class=\"m\">\\{0,1\\}</span> recognised by no Turing machine.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of TMs is countable, since each has a finite encoding and the finite strings over a finite alphabet can be listed. The set of all languages is uncountable by diagonalisation: given any list <span class=\"m\">L_1, L_2,\\dots</span> of languages and the list <span class=\"m\">s_1, s_2, \\dots</span> of all strings, <span class=\"m\">D = \\{s_i : s_i\\notin L_i\\}</span> differs from every <span class=\"m\">L_i</span>. A countable set of machines cannot recognise uncountably many languages.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decide whether the grammar <span class=\"m\">S\\to AB</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\to aA</span>, <span class=\"m\">B\\to b</span> generates anything</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Mark the terminals <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">b</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">B\\to b</span>: all marked, so mark <span class=\"m\">B</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">A\\to aA</span> needs <span class=\"m\">A</span> itself; <span class=\"m\">A</span> has no other rule, so <span class=\"m\">A</span> is never marked.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">S\\to AB</span> needs <span class=\"m\">A</span>: <span class=\"m\">S</span> is never marked. Answer: <span class=\"m\">L = \\varnothing</span>, the grammar is in <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{CFG}}</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decide <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{DFA}}</span> for \"ends in 1\" and \"odd binary numbers\"</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>A binary string represents an odd number exactly when its last bit is 1.</li>\n<li>So the two DFAs describe the same language, and the symmetric difference is empty.</li>\n<li>Mechanically: build the product DFA accepting where exactly one machine accepts; no such product state is reachable, so the <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}}</span> test answers \"empty\", and we accept.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Show that <span class=\"m\">\\text{INF}_{\\text{DFA}} = \\{\\langle B\\rangle : L(B) \\text{ is infinite}\\}</span> is decidable.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Let <span class=\"m\">B</span> have <span class=\"m\">k</span> states. Claim: <span class=\"m\">L(B)</span> is infinite iff <span class=\"m\">B</span> accepts some string <span class=\"m\">w</span> with <span class=\"m\">k\\le|w|&lt;2k</span>.\nIf such <span class=\"m\">w</span> exists, the pumping lemma (with <span class=\"m\">p = k</span>) pumps it to infinitely many accepted strings. Conversely, if <span class=\"m\">L(B)</span> is infinite it contains a string of length <span class=\"m\">\\ge k</span>; take the shortest one of length <span class=\"m\">\\ge k</span>. If its length were <span class=\"m\">\\ge 2k</span>, pumping down (removing a loop of length between 1 and <span class=\"m\">k</span> within the first <span class=\"m\">k</span> symbols) would give a shorter accepted string of length <span class=\"m\">\\ge k</span>, a contradiction.\nDecider: test all strings of length <span class=\"m\">k</span> to <span class=\"m\">2k-1</span> with the <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span> decider; finitely many, so it halts. (Alternatively: check for a cycle on some path from the start to an accepting state.)</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Decidable questions about automata power real tools: regex engines check whether two patterns are equivalent, firewalls check whether a rule set can ever match, and model checkers check that \"the system's behaviour intersected with the bad behaviours\" is empty. The undecidability of grammar equivalence explains why tools cannot always tell you whether two grammars define the same language.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Verification of neural networks asks decidable questions about finite objects (\"is there an input in this box that changes the label?\"), which are decidable but often NP-hard. Knowing which questions are decidable, and at what cost, shapes which robustness guarantees can be offered.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A spell checker that checks one word against a finite dictionary always finishes: a decidable problem. Asking \"will this computer program ever crash for any input?\" is a question about machines of unlimited power, and, as the next lessons show, no general checker can always answer it.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span> decidable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">simulating a DFA on <span class=\"m\">w</span> always stops after <span class=\"m\">|w|</span> steps</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">DFAs have no accepting states</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every language is decidable</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Simulation halts, so the decider always answers.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which problem is undecidable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{DFA}}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{CFG}}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{CFG}}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Equivalence is decidable for DFAs but not for grammars.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{DFA}}</span> reduces to which decidable problem?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">emptiness of the symmetric difference</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">acceptance of the empty string</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the halting problem</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">L(B) = L(C)</span> iff <span class=\"m\">L(B)\\triangle L(C) = \\varnothing</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A language is decidable if and only if it and its complement are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">finite</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">recognisable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">context-free</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Run the two recognisers in parallel; one must accept.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The DFA for \"divisible by 3\" has 3 states. How many states does the product DFA for comparing it with a 2-state DFA have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3\\times2 = 6</span> pairs of states.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The set of Turing machines is countable and the set of languages is uncountable. What follows?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">some languages are not recognised by any TM</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every language is decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">there are more TMs than languages</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A countable set of recognisers cannot cover uncountably many languages.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "halting-problem": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-ultimate-debugging-tool\" data-h=\"3\">The ultimate debugging tool</h3>\n<p>Every programmer has wished for a tool that looks at a program and its input and says: \"this will finish\" or \"this will run forever\". It would catch infinite loops before they happen, verify that servers respond, and settle mathematical conjectures automatically. Alan Turing proved in 1936 that such a tool is <strong>impossible</strong>. Not merely hard to build, not too slow on today's hardware: impossible for any algorithm, ever. This result, the undecidability of the halting problem, is the foundation for nearly every impossibility result in computer science.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-perfect-fortune-teller\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a perfect fortune teller</h3>\n<p>Imagine a fortune teller who claims to predict perfectly whether any person will do whatever she predicts. Now a contrarian walks in, determined to do the opposite of whatever she says he will do. Whatever she predicts about him, he does the other thing, so she is wrong about him. Her claim to be perfect must be false. The proof of the halting problem builds exactly such a contrarian program, using the supposed halting checker against itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-problems\" data-h=\"3\">The problems</h3>\n<p>Two closely related languages:\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ is a TM that accepts } w\\},</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ is a TM that halts on } w\\}.</span>\nBoth are <strong>recognisable</strong>: the universal machine <span class=\"m\">U</span> simulates <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> and accepts if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts (or, for HALT, if <span class=\"m\">M</span> halts). If <span class=\"m\">M</span> runs forever, so does <span class=\"m\">U</span>, which is allowed for a recogniser. The question is whether we can do better and always halt with an answer.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Acceptance problem for TMs</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ accepts } w\\}</span>. It is recognisable (by the universal TM) but undecidable.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Halting problem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ halts on input } w\\}</span>. It is recognisable but undecidable.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-simulation-is-not-enough\" data-h=\"3\">Why simulation is not enough</h3>\n<p>The obvious approach is to run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> and wait. If it halts, we know. But if it has not halted after a million steps, it might halt after a million and one, or never. There is no point at which waiting longer tells us \"never\". A cleverer approach would analyse the program's code instead of running it, and for many specific programs this works. Turing's theorem says that no single method can work for <strong>all</strong> programs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-diagonal-proof\" data-h=\"3\">The diagonal proof</h3>\n<p>Suppose, for contradiction, that some TM <span class=\"m\">H</span> decides <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>: on input <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, <span class=\"m\">H</span> always halts, accepting if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> and rejecting otherwise.</p>\n<p>Build a new machine <span class=\"m\">D</span> that uses <span class=\"m\">H</span> as a subroutine. On input <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span>, the encoding of a machine:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Run <span class=\"m\">H</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,\\langle M\\rangle\\rangle</span>, asking \"does <span class=\"m\">M</span> accept its own description?\"</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">H</span> accepts, <strong>reject</strong>. If <span class=\"m\">H</span> rejects, <strong>accept</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"m\">D</span> is the contrarian: it does the opposite of what <span class=\"m\">M</span> does on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M \\rangle</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">H</span> always halts, so does <span class=\"m\">D</span>. Now feed <span class=\"m\">D</span> its own description, <span class=\"m\">\\langle D\\rangle</span>. By construction,\n<span class=\"mb\">D \\text{ accepts } \\langle D\\rangle \\iff H \\text{ rejects } \\langle D,\\langle D\\rangle\\rangle \\iff D \\text{ does not accept } \\langle D\\rangle.</span>\nThat is a contradiction. So no such <span class=\"m\">H</span> exists, and <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-is-called-diagonal\" data-h=\"3\">Why it is called diagonal</h3>\n<p>List all Turing machines <span class=\"m\">M_1, M_2, M_3, \\dots</span> down the side of a table and their descriptions <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_1\\rangle, \\langle M_2\\rangle,\\dots</span> across the top. In row <span class=\"m\">i</span>, column <span class=\"m\">j</span>, write \"accept\" if <span class=\"m\">M_i</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_j\\rangle</span>. The machine <span class=\"m\">D</span> is built to disagree with the <strong>diagonal</strong>: on input <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_i\\rangle</span> it does the opposite of entry <span class=\"m\">(i, i)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">D</span> differs from every row, yet <span class=\"m\">D</span> is a Turing machine and must be some row. This is Cantor's diagonal argument, the same idea used to show the real numbers are uncountable, applied to programs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"from-acceptance-to-halting\" data-h=\"3\">From acceptance to halting</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable too. Suppose <span class=\"m\">R</span> decided it. Then we could decide <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>: on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, run <span class=\"m\">R</span>; if <span class=\"m\">M</span> does not halt on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, reject. If it does halt, it is now safe to simulate <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> to the end, and answer as <span class=\"m\">M</span> does. Since <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable, <span class=\"m\">R</span> cannot exist. This \"if you could solve B, you could solve A\" argument is called a <strong>reduction</strong>, and the next lesson develops it fully.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-language-that-is-not-even-recognisable\" data-h=\"3\">A language that is not even recognisable</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is recognisable but not decidable. By the theorem from the previous lesson (decidable iff recognisable and co-recognisable), its complement <span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span> <strong>cannot be recognisable</strong>: otherwise <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> would be decidable. So <span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span>, \"pairs where <span class=\"m\">M</span> does not accept <span class=\"m\">w</span>\", is a concrete, natural example of a language no Turing machine can even recognise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-it-means-in-practice\" data-h=\"3\">What it means in practice</h3>\n<p>The theorem does not say that we can never tell whether a specific program halts; for most simple programs we can. It says that <strong>every</strong> method fails on some program. Real tools respond in three ways: they answer only for restricted kinds of programs (for example, loops with a fixed bound), they allow the answer \"don't know\", or they settle for time limits. Many famous open problems could be settled by a halting checker: a program that searches for a counterexample to Goldbach's conjecture halts if and only if the conjecture is false.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The proof does not show that <span class=\"m\">D</span> \"crashes\"; it shows the assumed decider <span class=\"m\">H</span> cannot exist. Running <span class=\"m\">D</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle D\\rangle</span> is legitimate because programs can take program descriptions as input; compilers do this every day. \"Undecidable\" is not the same as \"unrecognisable\": <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is recognisable. And the result concerns a single algorithm for all inputs, not individual instances; each specific pair <span class=\"m\">\\langle M, w\\rangle</span> either halts or does not, and for that one instance a trivial \"always yes\" or \"always no\" machine is correct.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The halting problem and the acceptance problem for Turing machines are recognisable, by simulation, but undecidable. The proof assumes a decider <span class=\"m\">H</span>, builds a contrarian machine <span class=\"m\">D</span> that does the opposite of what each machine does on its own description, and derives a contradiction by running <span class=\"m\">D</span> on itself, which is a diagonal argument. Consequently <span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span> is not recognisable, and no general method can predict whether every program halts.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;loops when there is no 1&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0001&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A recogniser for \"contains a 1\". On inputs without a 1 it runs forever, and watching it never tells you so.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>0001</code> and watch it accept after a few steps.\n2. Run <code>000</code> and keep pressing step or let it run. When would you be sure it will never halt?\n3. Run <code>00000001</code> and compare: at step 5 the two runs look exactly alike.\n4. Switch to \"decide 0ⁿ1ⁿ\" and run any input: does it ever fail to halt?</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> at every moment, the run on <code>000</code> looks like a run that might still find a 1 later, just as the run on <code>00000001</code> did. Watching a computation can confirm halting but never non-halting, which is why simulation only gives a recogniser. For this tiny machine, reading its code tells us it loops forever on <code>000</code>; the halting theorem says no single algorithm can do such reasoning correctly for every machine.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ accepts } w\\},\\quad \\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ halts on } w\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D(\\langle M\\rangle) = \\begin{cases}\\text{reject} &amp; \\text{if } M \\text{ accepts } \\langle M\\rangle\\\\ \\text{accept} &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D \\text{ accepts } \\langle D\\rangle \\iff D \\text{ does not accept } \\langle D\\rangle</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}} \\text{ recognisable, undecidable};\\quad \\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}} \\text{ not recognisable}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\"><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>No Turing machine decides <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">H</span> decides <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>. Define <span class=\"m\">D</span>: on input <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span>, run <span class=\"m\">H</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,\\langle M\\rangle\\rangle</span> and output the opposite answer. <span class=\"m\">D</span> halts on all inputs. Then <span class=\"m\">D</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">\\langle D\\rangle</span> iff <span class=\"m\">H</span> rejects <span class=\"m\">\\langle D,\\langle D\\rangle\\rangle</span> iff <span class=\"m\">D</span> does not accept <span class=\"m\">\\langle D\\rangle</span>, a contradiction.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">The halting problem is undecidable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose <span class=\"m\">R</span> decides <span class=\"m\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}}</span>. Decide <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> as follows: on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, run <span class=\"m\">R</span>. If it rejects, <span class=\"m\">M</span> loops on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, so reject. If it accepts, simulate <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, which is guaranteed to halt, and accept iff <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts. This decides <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>, contradicting the previous theorem.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">A non-recognisable language</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span> is not Turing-recognisable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is recognisable. If <span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span> were also recognisable, <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> would be decidable by the decidable-iff-recognisable-and-co-recognisable theorem. It is not, so <span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span> is not recognisable.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Fill in the diagonal for three machines and build <span class=\"m\">D</span>'s answers</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Suppose <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> accepts every input, <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> rejects every input, and <span class=\"m\">M_3</span> accepts exactly the descriptions of even length, with <span class=\"m\">|\\langle M_3\\rangle|</span> even.</li>\n<li>Diagonal entries: <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_1\\rangle</span>: accept. <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_2\\rangle</span>: reject. <span class=\"m\">M_3</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_3\\rangle</span>: accept.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D</span> flips each: reject <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_1\\rangle</span>, accept <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_2\\rangle</span>, reject <span class=\"m\">\\langle M_3\\rangle</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">D</span> differs from <span class=\"m\">M_1</span>, <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> and <span class=\"m\">M_3</span> on their own descriptions, and the same holds for every machine in the list.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Turn Goldbach's conjecture into a halting question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Write a program <span class=\"m\">G</span> that tests <span class=\"m\">n = 4, 6, 8, \\dots</span> in turn, checking whether <span class=\"m\">n</span> is a sum of two primes (a finite check for each <span class=\"m\">n</span>).</li>\n<li>If some <span class=\"m\">n</span> fails, <span class=\"m\">G</span> halts and prints it.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">G</span> halts iff Goldbach's conjecture is false.</li>\n<li>A halting decider would settle the conjecture, which illustrates how strong such a decider would be and why its impossibility is believable.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A student claims: \"I can decide the halting problem. Run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> for <span class=\"m\">10^9</span> steps. If it has halted, answer yes; otherwise answer no.\" Give a precise argument that this method is wrong, using a concrete machine.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Let <span class=\"m\">M</span> be a machine that counts from 1 to <span class=\"m\">10^9 + 1</span> (incrementing a counter on its tape) and then halts. It halts on every input, but takes more than <span class=\"m\">10^9</span> steps, so the student's method answers \"no\", which is wrong. Any fixed time bound <span class=\"m\">T</span> fails in the same way for a machine that deliberately runs for <span class=\"m\">T + 1</span> steps. More generally, by the halting theorem, every algorithm that always halts gives a wrong answer on some input, so no modification of this method can be fully correct.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The halting problem explains why compilers cannot detect all infinite loops, why antivirus software cannot perfectly detect all malicious behaviour, and why static analysers (type checkers, linters, termination checkers in proof assistants like Coq and Lean) must be conservative: they reject some correct programs, or answer \"unknown\", in order to always halt.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>There is no general algorithm that can look at an arbitrary trained model with tool use or code execution and guarantee it will never perform some behaviour, because such a guarantee would imply deciding properties of arbitrary programs. Practical AI safety work therefore relies on restricted settings, testing and probabilistic guarantees rather than complete verification.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Your computer's \"this program is not responding\" dialog cannot know whether the program is stuck forever or just slow. It waits for a while and then asks you, because no software can decide in general whether the program will ever respond.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which statement about <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> is true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">it is recognisable but not decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is not recognisable</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The universal TM recognises it; the diagonal argument shows it is undecidable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the diagonal proof, what does <span class=\"m\">D</span> do on input <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it simulates <span class=\"m\">M</span> on the empty string</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">it does the opposite of what <span class=\"m\">H</span> says <span class=\"m\">M</span> does on <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it always loops</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">D</span> disagrees with every machine on that machine's own description.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which language is not Turing-recognisable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>If it were, <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> would be decidable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A machine runs for exactly 500 steps on input <span class=\"m\">w</span> and then halts. If a \"checker\" waits only 400 steps, how many more steps would it have needed to see the halt?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">500 - 400 = 100</span>. Any fixed time limit can be beaten by a slower machine.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many steps does the recogniser \"loops when there is no 1\" take to accept <span class=\"m\">0001</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It reads three 0s (3 steps) and accepts on the 1 (the fourth step).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why does simulating <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> not decide the halting problem?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">if <span class=\"m\">M</span> never halts, the simulation never halts either</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">simulation is too slow on modern computers</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the universal TM cannot read encodings</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Simulation confirms halting but can never confirm looping.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "reductions": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"solving-problems-by-translating-them\" data-h=\"3\">Solving problems by translating them</h3>\n<p>Once we know one problem is undecidable, we rarely need another diagonal argument. Instead we <strong>reduce</strong>: we show that if some new problem <span class=\"m\">B</span> could be solved, then the known undecidable problem <span class=\"m\">A</span> could be solved too. Since <span class=\"m\">A</span> cannot be solved, neither can <span class=\"m\">B</span>. Reductions are the main tool of computability theory, and in the final unit the same idea, with a time limit added, becomes the foundation of NP-completeness.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-converting-units\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine converting units</h3>\n<p>Suppose you can only measure temperatures in Celsius, and someone asks whether a liquid is above 212 degrees Fahrenheit. You translate the question: 212 F is 100 C, so you ask your Celsius thermometer whether it is above 100 C. Every Fahrenheit question becomes a Celsius question with the same answer. If Fahrenheit questions were impossible to answer, Celsius ones would have to be impossible too, since one translates into the other. That translation is a reduction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"mapping-reductions\" data-h=\"3\">Mapping reductions</h3>\n<p>A function <span class=\"m\">f:\\Sigma^*\\to\\Sigma^*</span> is <strong>computable</strong> if some TM, on every input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, halts with <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> on its tape. Language <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <strong>mapping reducible</strong> to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, written <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span>, if there is a computable <span class=\"m\">f</span> such that for every <span class=\"m\">w</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">w\\in A \\iff f(w)\\in B.</span>\nBoth directions matter: yes-instances must map to yes-instances, and no-instances to no-instances.</p>\n<p>A tiny example with numbers: let EVEN and ODD be the binary encodings of even and odd numbers. The function <span class=\"m\">f(n) = n+1</span> is computable (it is the binary-increment machine), and <span class=\"m\">n</span> is even iff <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> is odd, so EVEN <span class=\"m\">\\le_m</span> ODD. For instance <span class=\"m\">f(6) = 7</span>: 6 is even and 7 is odd; <span class=\"m\">f(3) = 4</span>: 3 is not even and 4 is not odd.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Computable function</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">f</span> for which some Turing machine, on every input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, halts with <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> on its tape.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mapping reduction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A computable function <span class=\"m\">f</span> with <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span> for all <span class=\"m\">w</span>. We write <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span>: \"A is no harder than B\".</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"what-reductions-tell-us\" data-h=\"3\">What reductions tell us</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is decidable, then <span class=\"m\">A</span> is decidable: on input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, compute <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> and run <span class=\"m\">B</span>'s decider. Turned around, this is how we prove undecidability:</p>\n<p><strong>If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">A</span> is undecidable, then <span class=\"m\">B</span> is undecidable.</strong></p>\n<p>The direction is the most common source of errors. To show a new problem <span class=\"m\">B</span> is hard, reduce a <strong>known hard problem to <span class=\"m\">B</span></strong>, not the other way round. The same holds for recognisability: if <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is recognisable, so is <span class=\"m\">A</span>; so if <span class=\"m\">A</span> is not recognisable, neither is <span class=\"m\">B</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-1-mathph47x\" data-h=\"3\">Example 1: <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m \\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}}</span></h3>\n<p>Given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, construct a machine <span class=\"m\">M'</span>: \"on input <span class=\"m\">x</span>, run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">x</span>; if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts, accept; if <span class=\"m\">M</span> rejects, loop forever\". Let <span class=\"m\">f(\\langle M,w\\rangle) = \\langle M',w\\rangle</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">M'</span> halts on <span class=\"m\">w</span> exactly when <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>. Constructing <span class=\"m\">M'</span> is just editing the code of <span class=\"m\">M</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f</span> is computable. Hence HALT is undecidable, confirming the last lesson's result in reduction form.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-2-emptiness-mathph63x\" data-h=\"3\">Example 2: emptiness, <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span></h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M) = \\varnothing\\}</span>. Given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, build <span class=\"m\">M_w</span>: \"on input <span class=\"m\">x</span>, ignore <span class=\"m\">x</span>, run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, and accept if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts\". Then <span class=\"m\">L(M_w)</span> is everything if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>, and empty otherwise. So\n<span class=\"mb\">\\langle M,w\\rangle\\in A_{\\text{TM}} \\iff \\langle M_w\\rangle\\notin E_{\\text{TM}}.</span>\nThis maps <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> to the <strong>complement</strong> of <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\overline{E_{\\text{TM}}}</span> is undecidable, and since decidable languages are closed under complement, <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable too. Notice the trick: the reduction never runs <span class=\"m\">M</span>; it only builds a new machine description, which always takes finite time.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-3-equivalence-mathph80x\" data-h=\"3\">Example 3: equivalence, <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span></h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M_1,M_2\\rangle : L(M_1) = L(M_2)\\}</span>. Reduce from <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span>: map <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\langle M, M_\\varnothing\\rangle</span>, where <span class=\"m\">M_\\varnothing</span> rejects everything. Then <span class=\"m\">L(M) = L(M_\\varnothing)</span> iff <span class=\"m\">L(M) = \\varnothing</span>. So <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span> and <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable. In fact <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span> is neither recognisable nor co-recognisable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"beyond-turing-machines\" data-h=\"3\">Beyond Turing machines</h3>\n<p>Reductions reach problems that look nothing like machines. In the <strong>Post correspondence problem</strong>, you are given dominoes with a string on top and a string on the bottom, such as <span class=\"m\">\\left[\\frac{b}{ca}\\right], \\left[\\frac{a}{ab}\\right], \\left[\\frac{ca}{a}\\right], \\left[\\frac{abc}{c}\\right]</span>, and you ask whether some sequence of them (with repeats allowed) spells the same string on top and bottom. Here the sequence <span class=\"m\">\\left[\\frac{a}{ab}\\right]\\left[\\frac{b}{ca}\\right]\\left[\\frac{ca}{a}\\right]\\left[\\frac{a}{ab}\\right]\\left[\\frac{abc}{c}\\right]</span> spells <span class=\"m\">abcaaabc</span> on both rows. Deciding this in general is undecidable, by a reduction that encodes a TM's computation history as dominoes. From PCP, further reductions show that ambiguity of context-free grammars and equivalence of CFGs are undecidable, and Hilbert's tenth problem was handled the same way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The direction: <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> with <span class=\"m\">A</span> undecidable shows <span class=\"m\">B</span> undecidable; reducing <span class=\"m\">B</span> to a hard problem shows nothing. The reduction function must be <strong>computable and total</strong>: it must halt on every input, so it may construct machines but must not run them in a way that might loop. The equivalence must hold in <strong>both</strong> directions. And when your reduction maps <span class=\"m\">A</span> to the complement of <span class=\"m\">B</span> (as with <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span>), say so: for decidability that is fine, but for recognisability it changes the conclusion.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A mapping reduction <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> is a computable translation <span class=\"m\">f</span> with <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>. Decidability and recognisability flow downward along reductions, so undecidability flows upward: reducing a known undecidable problem, such as <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>, to a new problem proves the new problem undecidable. With this method, halting, emptiness and equivalence of Turing machines, the Post correspondence problem, and problems about grammars are all undecidable.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;binary increment (+1)&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 110}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The reduction from EVEN to ODD is the computable function f(n) = n + 1, computed by this machine.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>110</code> (six) and check that the output is <code>111</code> (seven), an odd number.\n2. Run <code>11</code> (three), not even, and check that the output <code>100</code> (four) is not odd.\n3. Try several inputs and confirm that the machine halts on every one.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the output's last bit is 1 exactly when the input's last bit was 0, so \"input even\" and \"output odd\" always agree: that is the condition <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>. The machine halts on every input, which makes <span class=\"m\">f</span> a <strong>computable</strong> function, the other requirement of a mapping reduction. The undecidability reductions in this lesson are the same thing with a fancier <span class=\"m\">f</span>: one that edits machine descriptions.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B \\iff \\exists \\text{ computable } f\\ \\forall w:\\ w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B,\\ B \\text{ decidable} \\Rightarrow A \\text{ decidable};\\qquad A\\le_m B,\\ A\\text{ undecidable}\\Rightarrow B\\text{ undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B \\iff \\overline A\\le_m\\overline B;\\qquad A\\le_m B,\\ B\\le_m C\\Rightarrow A\\le_m C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}},\\quad A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\overline{E_{\\text{TM}}},\\quad E_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reductions transfer decidability</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is decidable, then <span class=\"m\">A</span> is decidable. If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is recognisable, then <span class=\"m\">A</span> is recognisable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f</span> be the reduction and <span class=\"m\">M_B</span> decide (or recognise) <span class=\"m\">B</span>. On input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, compute <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span>, which halts because <span class=\"m\">f</span> is computable, then run <span class=\"m\">M_B</span> on <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> and give its answer. Since <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>, this machine accepts exactly <span class=\"m\">A</span>, and it halts whenever <span class=\"m\">M_B</span> does.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Undecidability of emptiness and equivalence</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span> and <span class=\"m\">EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span> are undecidable.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Check the reduction EVEN <span class=\"m\">\\le_m</span> ODD on three inputs</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(n) = n + 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n = 10</span>: even; <span class=\"m\">f = 11</span>, odd. Both \"yes\".</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n = 7</span>: not even; <span class=\"m\">f = 8</span>, not odd. Both \"no\".</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">n = 0</span>: even; <span class=\"m\">f = 1</span>, odd. Both \"yes\". The equivalence holds in both directions.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Prove that <span class=\"m\">\\textit{ACCEPT}_\\varepsilon = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : M \\text{ accepts } \\varepsilon\\}</span> is undecidable</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Reduce from <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>. Given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, build <span class=\"m\">M_w</span>: \"on any input, erase it, write <span class=\"m\">w</span>, run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>\".</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">M_w</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span> iff <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>.</li>\n<li>Building <span class=\"m\">M_w</span> from <span class=\"m\">\\langle M, w\\rangle</span> is computable (it only writes code).</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\textit{ACCEPT}_\\varepsilon</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\textit{ACCEPT}_\\varepsilon</span> is undecidable.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verify the PCP solution from the article</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Top strings in order: <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">b</span>, <span class=\"m\">ca</span>, <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">abc</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">abcaaabc</span>.</li>\n<li>Bottom strings: <span class=\"m\">ab</span>, <span class=\"m\">ca</span>, <span class=\"m\">a</span>, <span class=\"m\">ab</span>, <span class=\"m\">c</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">abcaaabc</span>.</li>\n<li>Both rows spell the same 8-symbol string, so this instance is a yes-instance.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Show that <span class=\"m\">\\textit{REGULAR}_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M) \\text{ is regular}\\}</span> is undecidable by reduction from <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, build <span class=\"m\">R</span>: \"on input <span class=\"m\">x</span>: if <span class=\"m\">x</span> has the form <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span>, accept. Otherwise run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> and accept if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts.\"\nIf <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>, <span class=\"m\">R</span> accepts every string, so <span class=\"m\">L(R) = \\Sigma^*</span>, which is regular. If <span class=\"m\">M</span> does not accept <span class=\"m\">w</span>, <span class=\"m\">R</span> accepts exactly <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span>, which is not regular.\nSo <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle\\in A_{\\text{TM}}\\iff\\langle R\\rangle\\in\\textit{REGULAR}_{\\text{TM}}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f(\\langle M,w\\rangle) = \\langle R\\rangle</span> is computable because it only writes a machine description. Hence <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\textit{REGULAR}_{\\text{TM}}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\textit{REGULAR}_{\\text{TM}}</span> is undecidable.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Reductions are how computer scientists classify problems. Proving that a static-analysis question (does this variable ever become null? is this code reachable?) is undecidable is done by reducing the halting problem to it, which tells tool builders that they must approximate. The same technique, with polynomial-time functions, underlies NP-hardness proofs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Many ML problems are solved by reduction: multi-class classification reduced to several binary problems (one-vs-rest), ranking reduced to pairwise classification, and inference in one graphical model reduced to another. \"Learning reductions\" is an area that studies how errors transfer through such translations.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If you can parallel park a car, you can park a small van: the van problem reduces to the car problem with a few adjustments. Conversely, if an expert shows that parking the van in some spot is impossible, and every car instance can be translated into that van instance, then the car instance is impossible too. Reductions carry both good news and bad news.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To prove a new problem <span class=\"m\">B</span> is undecidable using a known undecidable problem <span class=\"m\">A</span>, you show...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">B\\le_m A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> have the same size</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Hardness flows from <span class=\"m\">A</span> up to <span class=\"m\">B</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In a mapping reduction <span class=\"m\">f</span> from <span class=\"m\">A</span> to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, which must hold?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\Rightarrow f(w)\\in B</span> only</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f</span> is computable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">f</span> must be one-to-one</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Yes-instances map to yes-instances and no-instances to no-instances, by a halting computation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using the reduction <span class=\"m\">f(n) = n+1</span> from EVEN to ODD, what is <span class=\"m\">f(12)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"13\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>12 is even and 13 is odd, as required.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> reduces to which language via the machine <span class=\"m\">M_w</span> that ignores its input?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the complement of <span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">E_{\\text{TM}}</span> directly</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{DFA}}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> iff <span class=\"m\">L(M_w)</span> is non-empty.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many dominoes are used in the PCP solution in the article?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{a}{ab}, \\frac{b}{ca}, \\frac{ca}{a}, \\frac{a}{ab}, \\frac{abc}{c}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_m B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B</span> is decidable, then <span class=\"m\">A</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">undecidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">not recognisable</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Compute <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> and run <span class=\"m\">B</span>'s decider.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "rice-theorem": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"one-theorem-instead-of-a-hundred-reductions\" data-h=\"3\">One theorem instead of a hundred reductions</h3>\n<p>In the last lesson we proved, one at a time, that emptiness, equivalence and regularity of Turing machine languages are undecidable. Each proof built a machine that behaved one way if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepted <span class=\"m\">w</span> and another way otherwise. The pattern is so regular that it can be turned into a single theorem, proved by Henry Rice in 1953. <strong>Rice's theorem</strong> says that <em>every</em> interesting question about the behaviour of programs, in the sense of what they accept, is undecidable. It is the reason no tool can perfectly answer \"does this program ever output a password?\" or \"does this function compute the same thing as that one?\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-judging-books-by-their-content\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine judging books by their content</h3>\n<p>Suppose a library hires you to sort books automatically. Some rules depend only on the cover: \"does the book have more than 300 pages?\" You can always check that quickly. Others depend on the story: \"does the hero survive at the end?\" Two books printed in different fonts but with the same story must get the same answer. Rice's theorem says that for programs, every \"story\" question (about what the program does) is undecidable, unless the answer is the same for every program. \"Cover\" questions about the code itself are a different matter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"semantic-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Semantic properties</h3>\n<p>A <strong>property</strong> of Turing machine languages is just a set <span class=\"m\">P</span> of languages; a machine <span class=\"m\">M</span> \"has the property\" if <span class=\"m\">L(M)\\in P</span>. The property is about the <strong>language</strong>, not the machine, so two machines with the same language either both have it or both lack it. Such properties are called <strong>semantic</strong>. Examples: \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is empty\", \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is regular\", \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> contains the string 101\", \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is finite\", \"<span class=\"m\">L(M) = \\Sigma^*</span>\".</p>\n<p>A property is <strong>trivial</strong> if it holds for every recognisable language or for none of them. Trivial properties are decidable for a silly reason: the machine that always says yes (or always says no) decides them.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Semantic property</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A set <span class=\"m\">P</span> of Turing-recognisable languages. A TM <span class=\"m\">M</span> has property <span class=\"m\">P</span> when <span class=\"m\">L(M)\\in P</span>, so machines with the same language agree on it.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Non-trivial property</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A property that some recognisable language has and some recognisable language lacks.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-statement\" data-h=\"3\">The statement</h3>\n<p><strong>Rice's theorem.</strong> Let <span class=\"m\">P</span> be a non-trivial property of Turing-recognisable languages. Then\n<span class=\"mb\">L_P = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M)\\in P\\}</span>\nis undecidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-proof-idea\" data-h=\"3\">The proof idea</h3>\n<p>We reduce <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> to <span class=\"m\">L_P</span>, generalising the reductions from the last lesson. We may assume the empty language does <strong>not</strong> have <span class=\"m\">P</span> (otherwise use the complement property <span class=\"m\">\\overline P</span>, which is also non-trivial; if <span class=\"m\">L_{\\overline P}</span> is undecidable, so is <span class=\"m\">L_P</span>). Because <span class=\"m\">P</span> is non-trivial, some recognisable language <span class=\"m\">L_0</span> <strong>does</strong> have <span class=\"m\">P</span>; let <span class=\"m\">T</span> be a machine recognising it.</p>\n<p>Given <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span>, build a machine <span class=\"m\">S</span>: \"on input <span class=\"m\">x</span>, first run <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>. If that accepts, run <span class=\"m\">T</span> on <span class=\"m\">x</span> and accept if <span class=\"m\">T</span> accepts\". Two cases:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>, then <span class=\"m\">S</span> behaves exactly like <span class=\"m\">T</span>, so <span class=\"m\">L(S) = L_0</span>, which has <span class=\"m\">P</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">M</span> does not accept <span class=\"m\">w</span>, then <span class=\"m\">S</span> accepts nothing, so <span class=\"m\">L(S) = \\varnothing</span>, which lacks <span class=\"m\">P</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle\\in A_{\\text{TM}}\\iff\\langle S\\rangle\\in L_P</span>. Building <span class=\"m\">S</span> is computable (we only write a description; we never run <span class=\"m\">M</span>). Therefore <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m L_P</span>, and <span class=\"m\">L_P</span> is undecidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-rice-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Using Rice's theorem</h3>\n<p>To apply the theorem to a set of machines, check two things:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Is it semantic?</strong> Whenever <span class=\"m\">L(M_1) = L(M_2)</span>, do <span class=\"m\">M_1</span> and <span class=\"m\">M_2</span> get the same answer?</li>\n<li><strong>Is it non-trivial?</strong> Give one recognisable language with the property and one without.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If both hold, the problem is undecidable, with no further work. For \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> contains 101\": it is semantic, since it depends only on <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span>, and non-trivial, since <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> contains 101 and <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> does not. So it is undecidable. For \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is finite\": semantic; <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> is finite and <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> is not; undecidable.</p>\n<h3 id=\"when-rice-does-not-apply\" data-h=\"3\">When Rice does not apply</h3>\n<p>Questions about the <strong>machine</strong> rather than its language can be decidable. \"Does <span class=\"m\">M</span> have at least 5 states?\" is not semantic: two machines with the same language can have 3 and 50 states. It is decidable by reading the code. \"Does <span class=\"m\">M</span> run for more than 10 steps on input <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>?\" is also not semantic, and it is decidable: simulate <span class=\"m\">M</span> for at most 11 steps. Be careful, though: Rice's theorem <strong>simply does not tell us</strong> anything about non-semantic properties, and each one must be analysed separately. Some are decidable and some are not. For example, \"does <span class=\"m\">M</span> ever enter state <span class=\"m\">q_7</span> on input <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>?\" is not semantic, but it is undecidable by a direct reduction from the halting problem.</p>\n<p>Rice's theorem is also about <strong>Turing machines</strong>. For DFAs and CFGs, many semantic questions are decidable: emptiness, finiteness and membership. The theorem bites because Turing machines are so powerful.</p>\n<h3 id=\"decidable-versus-recognisable\" data-h=\"3\">Decidable versus recognisable</h3>\n<p>Rice's theorem says nothing about recognisability. Some semantic properties are recognisable: \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is non-empty\" can be recognised by running <span class=\"m\">M</span> on all strings in parallel (dovetailing) and accepting when any of them accepts. Others, like \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is empty\" or \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is infinite\", are not even recognisable. Rice's theorem only tells us that none of them is <strong>decidable</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Check that the property is about the language, not the code. \"<span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">\\langle M\\rangle</span>\" is not a property of <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> alone in the required sense, so apply the theorem carefully. Check non-triviality with <strong>recognisable</strong> languages: \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is not recognisable\" is trivial, because every <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is recognisable. Do not use Rice's theorem to conclude that a question about DFAs, grammars or bounded computations is undecidable. And remember that undecidable does not mean that no instance can be answered; it means no single algorithm answers all instances correctly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Rice's theorem states that every non-trivial semantic property of Turing machines, any property of the recognised language that some but not all recognisable languages have, is undecidable. The proof reduces <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}</span> by building a machine whose language is either <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> or a fixed language with the property, depending on whether <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>. Properties of the code itself, and properties of weaker models such as DFAs, fall outside the theorem and may be decidable.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;decide palindromes&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0110&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The transition table is the machine's code; the set of strings it accepts is its language. Rice's theorem is about the second, not the first.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Count the rows of the transition table: that is a property of the code, decidable just by looking.\n2. Run <code>0110</code>, <code>010</code> and <code>0111</code> and note which are accepted.\n3. Ask: \"does this machine's language contain 0110?\" and \"is this machine's language infinite?\".</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the number of states is a syntactic property; a different palindrome machine could have more or fewer states and the same language. Questions in step 3 are semantic. For this particular machine you can answer them by understanding it, but Rice's theorem says that no single algorithm can answer such questions for every Turing machine. The visual shows exactly the dividing line: reading the table is easy, predicting the language in general is impossible.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">P \\text{ semantic}: \\ L(M_1) = L(M_2)\\Rightarrow\\big(\\langle M_1\\rangle\\in L_P\\iff\\langle M_2\\rangle\\in L_P\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P \\text{ non-trivial}: \\ \\exists\\, L_1\\in P,\\ L_2\\notin P \\ \\ (L_1, L_2 \\text{ recognisable})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Rice}: \\ P \\text{ semantic and non-trivial}\\Rightarrow L_P = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M)\\in P\\} \\text{ undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{reduction: } L(S) = \\begin{cases}L_0\\in P &amp; M \\text{ accepts } w\\\\ \\varnothing\\notin P &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Rice's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">P</span> is a non-trivial property of Turing-recognisable languages, then <span class=\"m\">\\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M)\\in P\\}</span> is undecidable.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Assume <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing\\notin P</span> (otherwise replace <span class=\"m\">P</span> by its complement; a set is decidable iff its complement is). Pick a recognisable <span class=\"m\">L_0\\in P</span> with recogniser <span class=\"m\">T</span>. Map <span class=\"m\">\\langle M,w\\rangle</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\langle S\\rangle</span>, where <span class=\"m\">S</span> on input <span class=\"m\">x</span> runs <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> and, if <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts, runs <span class=\"m\">T</span> on <span class=\"m\">x</span> and accepts if <span class=\"m\">T</span> does. If <span class=\"m\">M</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span> then <span class=\"m\">L(S) = L_0\\in P</span>; otherwise <span class=\"m\">L(S) = \\varnothing\\notin P</span>. The map is computable, so <span class=\"m\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m L_P</span> and <span class=\"m\">L_P</span> is undecidable.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> contains at least 3 strings\" decidable?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Semantic? It depends only on <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span>: yes.</li>\n<li>Non-trivial? <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> has at least 3 strings; <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> has none. Yes.</li>\n<li>By Rice's theorem it is undecidable.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is \"<span class=\"m\">M</span> halts within 100 steps on input 0\" decidable?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Semantic? No: two machines with the same language can take different numbers of steps.</li>\n<li>So Rice's theorem does not apply.</li>\n<li>Decide it directly: simulate <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">0</span> for at most 100 steps and check whether it has halted. This always finishes, so the problem is decidable.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Is \"<span class=\"m\">L(M) = L(M)^R</span>\" (the language is closed under reversal) decidable?</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Semantic: it depends only on <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span>.</li>\n<li>Non-trivial: <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> equals its reversal; <span class=\"m\">\\{01\\}</span> does not (<span class=\"m\">\\{01\\}^R = \\{10\\}</span>).</li>\n<li>Undecidable by Rice's theorem.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For each set, say whether Rice's theorem applies, and whether the set is decidable: (a) <span class=\"m\">\\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M) = \\{0,1\\}^*\\}</span>; (b) <span class=\"m\">\\{\\langle M\\rangle : M \\text{ has an even number of states}\\}</span>; (c) <span class=\"m\">\\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M) \\text{ is recognisable}\\}</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Semantic, and non-trivial (<span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span> has it, <span class=\"m\">\\varnothing</span> does not). Rice applies: undecidable.\n(b) Not semantic (state count is about the code). Rice does not apply. Decidable: count the states in the encoding.\n(c) Semantic but trivial: every <span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> is recognisable, so every machine has the property. The set is all valid encodings, which is decidable.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Rice's theorem is why program analysis tools must approximate. A tool that answers \"does this program ever divide by zero?\" or \"is this function pure?\" for all programs is impossible, so real analysers are sound but incomplete (they may raise false alarms) or complete but unsound (they may miss bugs), and they operate on abstractions of programs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Questions like \"does this neural network implement the same function as that one for all inputs?\" are decidable only because networks of fixed size over bounded inputs are finite objects. Once models can run arbitrary code or loop, semantic questions about their behaviour fall under Rice's theorem, so auditing relies on testing and interpretability rather than complete decision procedures.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>App stores cannot build a perfect automatic checker that rejects exactly the apps that will ever leak your contacts. Leaking is a property of behaviour, so by Rice's theorem any automatic reviewer either misses some leaks or wrongly flags some innocent apps, which is why manual review and runtime permissions exist.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Rice's theorem applies to properties of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the language a TM recognises</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of states of a TM</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the running time of a TM</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is about semantic properties only.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is \"<span class=\"m\">L(M)</span> contains the string 101\" decidable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">yes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">no, by Rice's theorem</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Semantic and non-trivial.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Is \"<span class=\"m\">M</span> has exactly 4 states\" decidable?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">yes, by reading the encoding</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">no, by Rice's theorem</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is a syntactic property, outside Rice's theorem.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A property held by every recognisable language is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">undecidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">trivial, hence decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">not semantic</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The machine that always says yes decides it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A decider for \"<span class=\"m\">M</span> halts within <span class=\"m\">k</span> steps on <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon</span>\" simulates at most how many steps when <span class=\"m\">k = 50</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"50\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Simulate up to 50 steps and check; it always finishes.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the proof of Rice's theorem, if <span class=\"m\">M</span> does not accept <span class=\"m\">w</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">L(S)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the empty language</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">L_0</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma^*</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">S</span> never gets past running <span class=\"m\">M</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>, so it accepts nothing.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "time-complexity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"from-can-it-be-done-to-how-fast\" data-h=\"3\">From \"can it be done?\" to \"how fast?\"</h3>\n<p>Computability theory sorted problems into those an algorithm can solve and those it cannot. But a decidable problem whose algorithm needs longer than the age of the universe is not much use. <strong>Complexity theory</strong> asks how much of a resource, usually time, a problem needs. This lesson sets up the measuring stick: the running time of a Turing machine as a function of the length of its input, compared using big-O notation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-sorting-exam-papers\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine sorting exam papers</h3>\n<p>A teacher sorting 30 exam papers by surname might compare every pair, about <span class=\"m\">30\\times29/2 = 435</span> comparisons. A cleverer teacher splits the pile, sorts each half and merges them, needing roughly <span class=\"m\">30\\log_2 30\\approx 147</span> comparisons. With 30 papers both are fine. With a million papers the first method needs about <span class=\"m\">5\\times10^{11}</span> comparisons and the second about <span class=\"m\">2\\times10^{7}</span>. What matters is not the exact count for one input but <strong>how the count grows</strong> as inputs get bigger. That growth rate is what time complexity measures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"running-time\" data-h=\"3\">Running time</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">M</span> be a deterministic TM that halts on every input. Its <strong>running time</strong> is the function <span class=\"m\">t:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N</span> where <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> is the <strong>maximum</strong> number of steps <span class=\"m\">M</span> takes on any input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>. This is <strong>worst-case</strong> analysis: the time is guaranteed for every input of that length. We say <span class=\"m\">M</span> runs in time <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> or is a <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span>-time machine.</p>\n<p>Measuring by input <strong>length</strong> is important. For a number <span class=\"m\">N</span> written in binary, the length is about <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 N</span> digits, not <span class=\"m\">N</span>. An algorithm that takes <span class=\"m\">N</span> steps on the number <span class=\"m\">N</span> is therefore <strong>exponential</strong> in the input length, since <span class=\"m\">N\\approx2^n</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Running time</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a decider <span class=\"m\">M</span>, the function <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span> giving the maximum number of steps <span class=\"m\">M</span> uses on any input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"big-o-notation\" data-h=\"3\">Big-O notation</h3>\n<p>Exact step counts depend on details of the machine, so we compare growth rates. We write <span class=\"m\">f(n) = O(g(n))</span> if there are constants <span class=\"m\">c&gt;0</span> and <span class=\"m\">n_0</span> such that <span class=\"m\">f(n)\\le c\\,g(n)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">n\\ge n_0</span>: eventually, <span class=\"m\">f</span> is at most a constant multiple of <span class=\"m\">g</span>. For example <span class=\"m\">5n^2+3n+7 = O(n^2)</span>, since for <span class=\"m\">n\\ge1</span> it is at most <span class=\"m\">15n^2</span>. We write <span class=\"m\">f(n) = o(g(n))</span> if <span class=\"m\">f(n)/g(n)\\to0</span>: <span class=\"m\">f</span> grows strictly slower, as in <span class=\"m\">n\\log n = o(n^2)</span>.</p>\n<p>In big-O, only the <strong>fastest-growing term</strong> matters, and constant factors disappear. Logarithm bases do not matter either, since <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 n = \\log_2 10\\cdot\\log_{10}n</span>. The common rates, from slow to fast, are\n<span class=\"mb\">1,\\ \\log n,\\ \\sqrt n,\\ n,\\ n\\log n,\\ n^2,\\ n^3,\\ 2^n,\\ n!.</span>\nPolynomials <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> are often called <strong>feasible</strong>, while exponential functions <span class=\"m\">2^{n^k}</span> quickly become hopeless: <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> at <span class=\"m\">n = 100</span> is a million, while <span class=\"m\">2^{100}</span> is about <span class=\"m\">1.27\\times10^{30}</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Big-O notation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">f(n) = O(g(n))</span> if there exist <span class=\"m\">c&gt;0</span> and <span class=\"m\">n_0</span> with <span class=\"m\">f(n)\\le c\\cdot g(n)</span> for all <span class=\"m\">n\\ge n_0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Time complexity class</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(t(n))</span> is the set of languages decided by some (multitape, deterministic) Turing machine in time <span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"analysing-a-machine-mathph55x\" data-h=\"3\">Analysing a machine: <span class=\"m\">0^n1^n</span></h3>\n<p>Consider the single-tape crossing-off decider for <span class=\"m\">\\{0^k1^k\\}</span> from the Turing machine lesson, on an input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>. First it scans the input to check it has the form <span class=\"m\">0^*1^*</span>: <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> steps. Then each round crosses off one 0 and one 1, scanning across the tape and back, which is <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> steps, and there are at most <span class=\"m\">n/2</span> rounds. Total: <span class=\"m\">O(n) + (n/2)\\cdot O(n) = O(n^2)</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\{0^k1^k\\}\\in\\text{TIME}(n^2)</span>.</p>\n<p>Can we do better? A cleverer single-tape machine crosses off <strong>every other</strong> 0 and every other 1 in each pass, checking that the parities of the remaining counts agree. Each pass halves the counts, so there are <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> passes of <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> steps each: <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span>. With <strong>two tapes</strong> it is even simpler: copy the 0s to the second tape, then match them against the 1s in one pass: <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span>. It is a theorem that no single-tape machine can do better than <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> for this language, so here the model makes a real difference.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-models-change-time\" data-h=\"3\">How models change time</h3>\n<p>Different models change running times, but only by limited amounts. A <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span>-time multitape TM can be simulated by a single-tape TM in time <span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span>, a <strong>polynomial</strong> change. A <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span>-time nondeterministic TM can be simulated deterministically in time <span class=\"m\">2^{O(t(n))}</span>, an <strong>exponential</strong> change, as far as anyone knows. That is why the complexity classes in the next lessons are defined to be insensitive to polynomial changes, and why nondeterminism gets its own classes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"more-time-means-more-problems\" data-h=\"3\">More time means more problems</h3>\n<p>The <strong>time hierarchy theorem</strong> says that, for well-behaved time bounds, more time really lets you solve more problems: for instance <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(n)\\subsetneq\\text{TIME}(n^2)\\subsetneq\\text{TIME}(n^3)\\subsetneq\\cdots</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(2^n)\\subsetneq\\text{TIME}(2^{2n})</span>. The proof is a time-limited version of the diagonal argument from the halting problem: a machine with more time simulates every faster machine on its own description and does the opposite.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Measure time in terms of the <strong>length</strong> of the input, not its numeric value; trial division up to <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt N</span> looks fast but is exponential in the number of digits. Big-O is an <strong>upper bound</strong>: saying an algorithm is <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span> is true even if it is actually <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span>, so state the tightest bound you can. Worst-case running time is a maximum over <strong>all</strong> inputs of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, not the time on a typical input. And do not ignore the model: one-tape versus multitape can change <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> into <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> or <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span>, though never polynomial into exponential.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The running time of a decider is the worst-case number of steps as a function of input length, compared with big-O notation, which ignores constant factors and lower-order terms. <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(t(n))</span> collects the languages decidable in time <span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span>. The single-tape decider for <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> takes <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span>, a smarter one <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span>, and a two-tape machine <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span>. Reasonable deterministic models differ by at most a polynomial factor, nondeterminism may cost an exponential factor, and the time hierarchy theorem shows that extra time really increases power.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"bigo\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each curve is a running time. Grow n and watch polynomials stay manageable while exponential and factorial curves explode.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Set <span class=\"m\">n = 40</span> and compare <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> (<span class=\"m\">1{,}600</span>) with <span class=\"m\">2^n</span>.\n2. Switch to the log y-axis and see how the polynomial curves become nearly straight lines, while <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> and <span class=\"m\">n!</span> keep bending upward.\n3. Push <span class=\"m\">n</span> to 1000 and read off <span class=\"m\">n\\log n</span> and <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>: the crossing-off decider for <span class=\"m\">0^k1^k</span> against the halving decider.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> at <span class=\"m\">n = 40</span>, <span class=\"m\">2^{40}\\approx1.1\\times10^{12}</span>, while <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> is only <span class=\"m\">64{,}000</span>. On the log axis, every polynomial is eventually dominated by every exponential. At <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span> the <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> machine needs about <span class=\"m\">10^4</span> steps where the <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> machine needs <span class=\"m\">10^6</span>: both polynomial, but a factor of 100 apart, which is why constant-free big-O still matters for comparing algorithms.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">t_M(n) = \\max_{|w| = n}\\ \\#\\text{steps of } M \\text{ on } w</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f = O(g)\\iff \\exists c,n_0\\ \\forall n\\ge n_0:\\ f(n)\\le c\\,g(n);\\qquad f = o(g)\\iff f(n)/g(n)\\to0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{TIME}(t(n)) = \\{L : L \\text{ decided in time } O(t(n))\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multitape } t \\Rightarrow \\text{single-tape } O(t^2);\\qquad \\text{NTM } t\\Rightarrow\\text{DTM } 2^{O(t)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Time hierarchy theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">t_1(n)\\log t_1(n) = o(t_2(n))</span> and <span class=\"m\">t_2</span> is time-constructible, then <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(t_1(n))\\subsetneq\\text{TIME}(t_2(n))</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">A lower bound for one tape</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every single-tape TM deciding <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> needs <span class=\"m\">\\Omega(n\\log n)</span> time, and <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> is achievable; with two tapes <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> is achievable.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">3n^3 + 20n^2 + 5 = O(n^3)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">n\\ge1</span>: <span class=\"m\">20n^2\\le20n^3</span> and <span class=\"m\">5\\le5n^3</span>.</li>\n<li>So <span class=\"m\">3n^3+20n^2+5\\le(3+20+5)n^3 = 28n^3</span>.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">c = 28</span> and <span class=\"m\">n_0 = 1</span> the definition holds.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Analyse trial division for primality in terms of input length</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Input: <span class=\"m\">N</span> in binary, length <span class=\"m\">n\\approx\\log_2 N</span>.</li>\n<li>Trial division tests divisors up to <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt N</span>, about <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt N = 2^{n/2}</span> divisions.</li>\n<li>So the time is exponential in <span class=\"m\">n</span>: for a 2048-bit number, about <span class=\"m\">2^{1024}</span> divisions.</li>\n<li>Moral: \"linear in <span class=\"m\">N</span>\" or \"square root of <span class=\"m\">N</span>\" can still be exponential in the input length.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count the passes of the halving decider on <span class=\"m\">0^{16}1^{16}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Each pass removes half of the remaining 0s and half of the 1s.</li>\n<li>Counts go <span class=\"m\">16\\to8\\to4\\to2\\to1\\to0</span>: 5 passes.</li>\n<li>In general <span class=\"m\">\\lfloor\\log_2 k\\rfloor+1</span> passes, each <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span>, for <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> total.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An algorithm on graphs with <span class=\"m\">v</span> vertices takes <span class=\"m\">4v^3 + 2^{10}v^2</span> steps. (a) Give its big-O running time in terms of <span class=\"m\">v</span>. (b) For which <span class=\"m\">v</span> is the second term larger than the first? (c) If the input is an adjacency matrix of length <span class=\"m\">n = v^2</span>, give the running time in terms of <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">O(v^3)</span>, the dominant term.\n(b) <span class=\"m\">2^{10}v^2 &gt; 4v^3 \\iff 1024 &gt; 4v\\iff v &lt; 256</span>. So for <span class=\"m\">v &lt; 256</span> the \"lower-order\" term dominates, a reminder that big-O describes large inputs.\n(c) <span class=\"m\">v = \\sqrt n</span>, so <span class=\"m\">v^3 = n^{3/2}</span>: the time is <span class=\"m\">O(n^{1.5})</span>, which is still polynomial in the input length.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Time complexity is how engineers choose algorithms: a database query planner chooses between an <span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span> sort-merge join and an <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> nested loop, and a hashing scheme with <span class=\"m\">O(1)</span> expected lookups replaces an <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> search tree only when the constant factors favour it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Training costs in ML are complexity statements: self-attention takes <span class=\"m\">O(n^2d)</span> time for sequence length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, which motivated linear-time attention variants; <span class=\"m\">k</span>-nearest-neighbours prediction costs <span class=\"m\">O(nd)</span> per query without an index; and exact Gaussian process regression costs <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>, which is why sparse approximations exist.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A delivery company that plans routes by checking every possible order of 15 stops faces <span class=\"m\">15!\\approx1.3\\times10^{12}</span> orders, which is too many even for a fast computer. Growth rate, not computer speed, decides what is practical.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Running time is measured as a function of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the length of the input</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the numeric value of the input</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of states of the machine</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>For numbers in binary, the length is about <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 N</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">7n^2 + 100n + 3</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">not <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The dominant term is <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>; it is also <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span>, a weaker bound.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> when <span class=\"m\">n = 1000</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1000000\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">10^6</span> steps.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many passes does the halving decider make on <span class=\"m\">0^81^8</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">8\\to4\\to2\\to1\\to0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A two-tape TM can decide <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> in time...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">O(n)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n\\log n)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Copy the 0s to the second tape and match them with the 1s in one pass.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Simulating a nondeterministic <span class=\"m\">t(n)</span>-time machine deterministically is known to take at most...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(t(n))</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">O(t(n)^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">2^{O(t(n))}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Breadth-first search over the computation tree.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "class-p": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"what-counts-as-efficient\" data-h=\"3\">What counts as efficient?</h3>\n<p>Running times like <span class=\"m\">n</span>, <span class=\"m\">n^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">n^3</span> are manageable; running times like <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> are not. Complexity theory draws its first big line exactly there. The class <strong>P</strong> contains every problem that can be solved in time bounded by some polynomial in the input length. It is the standard formal meaning of \"efficiently solvable\", sometimes called the <strong>Cobham-Edmonds thesis</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-checking-a-road-map\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine checking a road map</h3>\n<p>Suppose you have a map of 500 towns and the roads between them, and you want to know whether you can drive from Ashford to Zennor. You do not need to try every possible route, of which there are astronomically many. Instead you spread outward: mark Ashford, then every town one road away, then every town one road from those, and so on. Each road is looked at a bounded number of times, so the work is roughly proportional to the size of the map. That spreading search is a polynomial-time algorithm, and it puts this problem in P.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{P} = \\bigcup_{k\\ge1}\\text{TIME}(n^k).</span>\nA language is in P if some deterministic Turing machine decides it in time <span class=\"m\">O(n^k)</span> for some constant <span class=\"m\">k</span>.</p>\n<p>Why polynomials? Three reasons. <strong>Robustness</strong>: every reasonable deterministic model (single-tape TMs, multitape TMs, RAM machines, real programming languages) simulates the others with polynomial overhead, and a polynomial of a polynomial is a polynomial, so P is the same class in all of them. <strong>Closure</strong>: running a polynomial-time subroutine a polynomial number of times stays polynomial. <strong>Practice</strong>: problems that are in P usually have algorithms with small exponents, and the gap between polynomial and exponential is enormous in practice.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">The class P</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of languages decidable by a deterministic Turing machine in time <span class=\"m\">O(n^k)</span> for some constant <span class=\"m\">k</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Polynomial-time algorithm</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An algorithm whose worst-case running time is <span class=\"m\">O(n^k)</span> for some constant <span class=\"m\">k</span>, where <span class=\"m\">n</span> is the input length.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"encoding-matters-a-little\" data-h=\"3\">Encoding matters, a little</h3>\n<p>Inputs must be encoded reasonably. Graphs can be given as adjacency matrices or edge lists, which differ in length polynomially, so either is fine. Numbers must be written in <strong>binary</strong> (or any base at least 2), not <strong>unary</strong>: writing <span class=\"m\">N</span> as <span class=\"m\">N</span> ones inflates the input exponentially and makes slow algorithms look fast.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-1-path\" data-h=\"3\">Example 1: PATH</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\text{PATH} = \\{\\langle G,s,t\\rangle : G \\text{ is a directed graph with a path from } s \\text{ to } t\\}</span>. Breadth-first search marks <span class=\"m\">s</span>, then repeatedly marks every vertex reachable by one edge from a marked vertex, until nothing changes, and accepts if <span class=\"m\">t</span> is marked. With <span class=\"m\">v</span> vertices there are at most <span class=\"m\">v</span> rounds, each scanning all edges, so the time is polynomial; a careful implementation takes <span class=\"m\">O(v + e)</span> steps. Brute force, listing all possible paths, could take exponential time. So PATH <span class=\"m\">\\in</span> P.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-2-relprime-and-euclid\" data-h=\"3\">Example 2: RELPRIME and Euclid</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\text{RELPRIME} = \\{\\langle x,y\\rangle : \\gcd(x,y) = 1\\}</span>. Trying all divisors up to <span class=\"m\">\\min(x,y)</span> is exponential in the number of digits. Euclid's algorithm repeatedly replaces <span class=\"m\">(x,y)</span> by <span class=\"m\">(y, x\\bmod y)</span> until <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>. Every two steps the larger number at least halves, so the number of steps is at most about <span class=\"m\">2\\log_2 x</span>, which is linear in the number of digits. For example <span class=\"m\">\\gcd(84, 35)</span>: <span class=\"m\">(84,35)\\to(35,14)\\to(14,7)\\to(7,0)</span>, giving 7, so 84 and 35 are not relatively prime. Each step is a division of <span class=\"m\">n</span>-digit numbers, so the total time is polynomial.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-3-every-context-free-language\" data-h=\"3\">Example 3: every context-free language</h3>\n<p>The CYK algorithm decides membership for a fixed CNF grammar in <span class=\"m\">O(n^3)</span> time. So every context-free language is in P.</p>\n<h3 id=\"example-4-primality\" data-h=\"3\">Example 4: primality</h3>\n<p>Is a given number prime? For centuries the best known methods were exponential in the number of digits. In 2002, Manindra Agrawal, Neeraj Kayal and Nitin Saxena proved that PRIMES is in P with the AKS algorithm. In practice, randomised tests such as Miller-Rabin are used because they are faster, but the theoretical question was settled.</p>\n<h3 id=\"closure-properties\" data-h=\"3\">Closure properties</h3>\n<p>P is closed under union, intersection, complement, concatenation and star. For complement, flip the answer of the decider. For concatenation, try all <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> split points and run both deciders on each piece: polynomial. For star, a dynamic programming table over all substrings does the job in polynomial time, just like CYK.</p>\n<h3 id=\"beyond-p\" data-h=\"3\">Beyond P</h3>\n<p>Not everything is in P. By the time hierarchy theorem, there are decidable problems that need exponential time. And for many important problems, such as finding the shortest tour through cities or satisfying a Boolean formula, nobody knows a polynomial algorithm, but nobody can prove one does not exist. That is the subject of the next lessons.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A running time like <span class=\"m\">O(n^{100})</span> is in P but useless in practice; P is a theoretical idealisation, and in practice exponents are usually small. \"Polynomial in the value of a number\" is not polynomial time; algorithms like that are called <strong>pseudo-polynomial</strong>. Average-case speed does not count: the bound must hold for the worst input. And \"we don't know a polynomial algorithm\" is not the same as \"the problem is not in P\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>P is the class of problems decidable in polynomial time by a deterministic machine. It is robust across reasonable models and encodings, closed under the usual operations, and captures the practical idea of efficient computation. PATH (by breadth-first search), RELPRIME (by Euclid's algorithm), every context-free language (by CYK) and primality (by AKS) are all in P. Whether many other natural problems are in P is one of the great open questions.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;, &quot;alg&quot;: &quot;bfs&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Breadth-first search decides PATH in polynomial time: each step marks the vertices one edge further from the start.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step through the search and record which vertices are marked after each round.\n2. Count how many rounds it takes until nothing new is marked.\n3. Pick a target vertex and note the round in which it is first marked.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the number of rounds is at most the number of vertices, and each round looks at each edge at most once, so the total work is polynomial, matching the PATH analysis in the article. The order of discovery is by distance from the start: BFS never needs to consider whole paths, which is why it avoids the exponential number of routes a brute-force search would try.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{P} = \\bigcup_{k\\ge1}\\text{TIME}(n^k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{PATH: BFS in } O(v+e);\\qquad \\gcd(x,y) = \\gcd(y, x\\bmod y) \\text{ in } O(\\log x) \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFL}\\subseteq\\text{P}\\ (\\text{CYK } O(n^3));\\qquad \\text{PRIMES}\\in\\text{P}\\ (\\text{AKS, 2002})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{poly}(\\text{poly}(n)) = \\text{poly}(n)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">PATH is in P</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>PATH is decidable in polynomial time by breadth-first search.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">RELPRIME is in P</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Euclid's algorithm decides RELPRIME in time polynomial in the number of digits of <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">x\\ge y</span>. After one step the pair is <span class=\"m\">(y, x\\bmod y)</span>. If <span class=\"m\">y\\le x/2</span> then the new first number <span class=\"m\">y</span> is at most <span class=\"m\">x/2</span>. If <span class=\"m\">y &gt; x/2</span> then <span class=\"m\">x\\bmod y = x - y &lt; x/2</span>, so after the second step the first number is less than <span class=\"m\">x/2</span>. Either way the first number at least halves every two steps, so there are at most <span class=\"m\">2\\log_2 x + 2</span> steps, linear in the number of digits. Each step is a division, computable in polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Closure of P</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A, B\\in</span> P then <span class=\"m\">A\\cup B</span>, <span class=\"m\">A\\cap B</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\overline A</span>, <span class=\"m\">AB</span> and <span class=\"m\">A^*</span> are in P.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Run Euclid's algorithm on <span class=\"m\">(144, 89)</span> and count the steps</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">(144,89)\\to(89,55)\\to(55,34)\\to(34,21)\\to(21,13)\\to(13,8)</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\to(8,5)\\to(5,3)\\to(3,2)\\to(2,1)\\to(1,0)</span>.</li>\n<li>10 steps, gcd 1, so the pair is relatively prime. Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the worst case, and even they need only about 1.44 times the number of bits.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that P is closed under concatenation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"m\">M_A</span>, <span class=\"m\">M_B</span> decide <span class=\"m\">A</span>, <span class=\"m\">B</span> in times <span class=\"m\">O(n^a)</span>, <span class=\"m\">O(n^b)</span>.</li>\n<li>On input <span class=\"m\">w</span> of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, for each of the <span class=\"m\">n+1</span> split points <span class=\"m\">w = xy</span>, run <span class=\"m\">M_A</span> on <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">M_B</span> on <span class=\"m\">y</span>.</li>\n<li>Accept if both accept for some split.</li>\n<li>Time: <span class=\"m\">(n+1)\\cdot O(n^a + n^b) = O(n^{\\max(a,b)+1})</span>, polynomial.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The problem SUBSET-SUM-UNARY gives a list of positive integers <span class=\"m\">a_1,\\dots,a_k</span> and a target <span class=\"m\">T</span>, all written in <strong>unary</strong>, and asks whether some subset sums to exactly <span class=\"m\">T</span>. Show that it is in P, and explain why this does not show that the usual (binary) SUBSET-SUM is in P.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Use dynamic programming: maintain the set <span class=\"m\">R\\subseteq\\{0,1,\\dots,T\\}</span> of reachable sums, starting with <span class=\"m\">R = \\{0\\}</span>, and for each <span class=\"m\">a_i</span> replace <span class=\"m\">R</span> by <span class=\"m\">R\\cup\\{r + a_i : r\\in R, r + a_i\\le T\\}</span>. Accept iff <span class=\"m\">T\\in R</span>. This takes <span class=\"m\">O(kT)</span> steps. In unary, the input length <span class=\"m\">n</span> is at least <span class=\"m\">T + \\sum a_i\\ge T</span> and at least <span class=\"m\">k</span>, so <span class=\"m\">O(kT) = O(n^2)</span>: polynomial.\nIn binary, <span class=\"m\">T</span> can be about <span class=\"m\">2^n</span>, so <span class=\"m\">O(kT)</span> is exponential in the input length. The algorithm is only pseudo-polynomial, and binary SUBSET-SUM is in fact NP-complete.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Most of an algorithms course is a tour of P: sorting, shortest paths (Dijkstra), minimum spanning trees, maximum flow, matching, linear programming (proved polynomial by the ellipsoid and interior-point methods) and parsing. Knowing a problem is in P is the first step to engineering a fast solution.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Many core ML computations are in P: least-squares regression (solving the normal equations in <span class=\"m\">O(nd^2 + d^3)</span>), training linear SVMs as convex quadratic programs, and inference in tree-structured graphical models by message passing. Training a general neural network to optimality, by contrast, is NP-hard, which is why we rely on heuristics like gradient descent.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Satellite navigation computes the fastest route across a country with millions of road segments in a fraction of a second. Shortest paths are in P, and good polynomial algorithms plus preprocessing make them practical at enormous scale.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">P is defined as the union of <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(n^k)</span> over all...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">constants <span class=\"m\">k\\ge1</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">k\\le 3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">functions <span class=\"m\">k(n)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Each language in P has some fixed polynomial bound.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Euclid's algorithm on <span class=\"m\">(84, 35)</span> returns which gcd?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(84,35)\\to(35,14)\\to(14,7)\\to(7,0)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why must numbers be encoded in binary rather than unary when defining P?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">unary inflates input length exponentially, making slow algorithms look polynomial</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">unary cannot represent zero</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">Turing machines cannot read unary</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A number <span class=\"m\">N</span> takes <span class=\"m\">N</span> symbols in unary but about <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 N</span> in binary.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which problem is known to be in P?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">PRIMES (is <span class=\"m\">N</span> prime?)</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">factoring <span class=\"m\">N</span> into primes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the halting problem</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>AKS (2002). No polynomial factoring algorithm is known.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Trying all split points to show closure under concatenation: how many split points does a string of length 9 have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">n + 1</span> ways to split a string of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, including empty pieces.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A problem has an algorithm running in time <span class=\"m\">O(2^n)</span> but no known polynomial algorithm. What can we conclude?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is not in P</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">nothing yet; it might still be in P</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">it is undecidable</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Absence of a known algorithm is not a proof of impossibility.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "class-np": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"finding-versus-checking\" data-h=\"3\">Finding versus checking</h3>\n<p>Some problems are hard to solve but easy to check. A Sudoku puzzle can take an hour to solve, but if a friend hands you a completed grid, you can check it in a minute: every row, column and box contains each digit once. A huge number may be hard to factor, but if someone tells you that <span class=\"m\">91 = 7\\times13</span>, one multiplication confirms it. The class <strong>NP</strong> captures exactly this kind of problem: those whose \"yes\" answers come with a short proof, a <strong>certificate</strong>, that can be checked quickly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-seating-plan\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a seating plan</h3>\n<p>Imagine planning a wedding with 200 guests and a list of pairs who must not sit at the same table. Finding a seating plan that works may take days of trial and error, because the number of possible plans is astronomical. But if the wedding planner hands you a finished plan, you can verify it by going through the list of forbidden pairs once. The plan itself is the certificate, and checking it is fast. Whether finding can always be made as fast as checking is the P versus NP question.</p>\n<h3 id=\"verifiers-and-certificates\" data-h=\"3\">Verifiers and certificates</h3>\n<p>A <strong>verifier</strong> for a language <span class=\"m\">A</span> is an algorithm <span class=\"m\">V</span> that takes two inputs, <span class=\"m\">w</span> and a string <span class=\"m\">c</span> called a <strong>certificate</strong> (or witness), such that\n<span class=\"mb\">w\\in A \\iff \\text{there is some } c \\text{ with } V \\text{ accepting } \\langle w,c\\rangle.</span>\nA <strong>polynomial-time verifier</strong> runs in time polynomial in <span class=\"m\">|w|</span>. Since it can only read polynomially many symbols, the certificate it uses must be polynomially short as well.</p>\n<p><strong>NP</strong> is the class of languages that have polynomial-time verifiers.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verifier</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An algorithm <span class=\"m\">V</span> such that <span class=\"m\">w\\in A</span> exactly when some certificate <span class=\"m\">c</span> makes <span class=\"m\">V</span> accept <span class=\"m\">\\langle w,c\\rangle</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Certificate</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A string that proves membership of <span class=\"m\">w</span> in <span class=\"m\">A</span> when checked by a verifier, such as a satisfying assignment or a path.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">The class NP</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The set of languages that have a polynomial-time verifier; equivalently, the languages decided by a nondeterministic Turing machine in polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"examples\" data-h=\"3\">Examples</h3>\n<p><strong>SAT</strong>: given a Boolean formula, is there an assignment of true and false to its variables that makes it true? Certificate: the assignment. Check: evaluate the formula, which takes time linear in its length. The formula <span class=\"m\">(p\\lor q)\\land(\\lnot p\\lor r)\\land(\\lnot q\\lor\\lnot r)</span> has the certificate <span class=\"m\">p</span> = false, <span class=\"m\">q</span> = true, <span class=\"m\">r</span> = false: the clauses evaluate to true, true, true.</p>\n<p><strong>HAMPATH</strong>: does a directed graph have a path from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> visiting every vertex exactly once? Certificate: the ordered list of vertices. Check: every vertex appears exactly once, the list starts at <span class=\"m\">s</span>, ends at <span class=\"m\">t</span>, and each consecutive pair is an edge.</p>\n<p><strong>CLIQUE</strong>: does a graph contain <span class=\"m\">k</span> vertices that are all joined to each other? Certificate: the <span class=\"m\">k</span> vertices. Check: all <span class=\"m\">\\binom k2</span> pairs are edges.</p>\n<p><strong>SUBSET-SUM</strong>: given numbers <span class=\"m\">S</span> and a target <span class=\"m\">t</span>, does some subset of <span class=\"m\">S</span> sum to <span class=\"m\">t</span>? For <span class=\"m\">S = \\{4, 11, 16, 21, 27\\}</span> and <span class=\"m\">t = 25</span>, the certificate <span class=\"m\">\\{4, 21\\}</span> is checked by one addition. Without a certificate, a brute-force search would try up to <span class=\"m\">2^5 = 32</span> subsets, and <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> in general.</p>\n<p><strong>COMPOSITES</strong>: is <span class=\"m\">N</span> composite? Certificate: a factor <span class=\"m\">d</span> with <span class=\"m\">1&lt;d&lt;N</span>. Check: divide. For <span class=\"m\">N = 91</span>, <span class=\"m\">d = 7</span> works.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-nondeterministic-view\" data-h=\"3\">The nondeterministic view</h3>\n<p>NP stands for <strong>nondeterministic polynomial time</strong>. An equivalent definition: <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP iff some nondeterministic TM decides <span class=\"m\">A</span> in polynomial time. The two views match. Given a verifier, a nondeterministic machine <strong>guesses</strong> the certificate one symbol at a time (branching on every possible symbol) and then runs the verifier; some branch accepts exactly when a certificate exists. Conversely, given a nondeterministic machine, the certificate is the <strong>sequence of choices</strong> made on an accepting branch, and the verifier simply replays them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"p-is-inside-np\" data-h=\"3\">P is inside NP</h3>\n<p>Every language in P is in NP: the verifier ignores the certificate and solves the problem directly. So <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}</span>. The other direction, whether every efficiently checkable problem is efficiently solvable, is the <strong>P versus NP</strong> question, one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems with a one-million-dollar prize. Most researchers believe <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\ne\\text{NP}</span>, but there is no proof.</p>\n<p>We also know <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span>: a deterministic machine can try every certificate of length up to <span class=\"m\">p(n)</span>, of which there are <span class=\"m\">2^{O(p(n))}</span>, and run the verifier on each. So NP problems are always solvable, just possibly slowly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"conp\" data-h=\"3\">coNP</h3>\n<p>The class <strong>coNP</strong> contains the complements of NP languages: problems whose <strong>no</strong> answers have short certificates. TAUTOLOGY (is a formula true under every assignment?) is in coNP, since a falsifying assignment proves a formula is not a tautology. Nobody knows whether NP equals coNP. If <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>, then both would equal P, since P is closed under complement.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>NP does <strong>not</strong> mean \"not polynomial\"; it means nondeterministic polynomial, and it includes all of P. A certificate only has to exist for yes-instances; for no-instances, no certificate works, but we do not need a proof of \"no\" (that would be coNP). The verifier must run in time polynomial in the length of <span class=\"m\">w</span>, not of <span class=\"m\">c</span>; long certificates are not allowed. And being in NP is not the same as being hard: sorting is in NP, because it is in P.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>NP is the class of problems whose yes-instances have certificates that can be checked in polynomial time, or equivalently the problems solved in polynomial time by a nondeterministic Turing machine that guesses a certificate. SAT, HAMPATH, CLIQUE, SUBSET-SUM and COMPOSITES are in NP. <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span>, and whether <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span> is the most famous open question in computer science. coNP is the class of complements, with short certificates for no-answers.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"truth-table\" data-args='{&quot;expr&quot;: &quot;(p_\\u2228_q)_\\u2227_(\\u00acp_\\u2228_r)_\\u2227_(\\u00acq_\\u2228_\\u00acr)&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Solving SAT by brute force means filling every row. Checking a certificate means evaluating one row.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Find the rows where the formula is true and count them.\n2. Check the article's certificate, <span class=\"m\">p</span> false, <span class=\"m\">q</span> true, <span class=\"m\">r</span> false, by reading that single row.\n3. Add a fourth variable to the expression (for example append <code>_∧_(s_∨_¬s)</code>) and see how many rows the table needs now.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> exactly 2 of the 8 rows are true: <span class=\"m\">(p,q,r) = (0,1,0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(1,0,1)</span>. Verifying a given row takes one quick evaluation, but finding a true row by brute force means scanning up to <span class=\"m\">2^n</span> rows, and each extra variable doubles the table. That gap between checking one row and searching all rows is the gap between NP verification and the best known solving methods.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A\\in\\text{NP}\\iff\\exists\\ \\text{poly-time } V,\\ \\text{polynomial } p:\\ w\\in A\\iff\\exists c,\\ |c|\\le p(|w|),\\ V(\\langle w,c\\rangle) = \\text{accept}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NP} = \\bigcup_k\\text{NTIME}(n^k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME};\\qquad \\text{coNP} = \\{\\overline A : A\\in\\text{NP}\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{brute force over certificates}: \\ 2^{p(n)}\\cdot\\text{poly}(n)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verifiers and nondeterministic machines agree</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A language has a polynomial-time verifier if and only if it is decided by some nondeterministic polynomial-time Turing machine.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Given verifier <span class=\"m\">V</span> running in time <span class=\"m\">n^k</span>, build an NTM that on input <span class=\"m\">w</span> nondeterministically writes a string <span class=\"m\">c</span> of length at most <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> and then runs <span class=\"m\">V</span> on <span class=\"m\">\\langle w,c\\rangle</span>, accepting if <span class=\"m\">V</span> does. It accepts iff some certificate exists, in polynomial time. Conversely, given an NTM <span class=\"m\">N</span> running in time <span class=\"m\">n^k</span>, let the certificate be the list of nondeterministic choices on an accepting branch; the verifier simulates <span class=\"m\">N</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> following those choices and accepts iff that branch accepts. This takes polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">P is contained in NP</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verify a CLIQUE certificate</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Graph edges: <span class=\"m\">12, 13, 14, 23, 24, 34, 45</span>. Claim: <span class=\"m\">\\{1,2,3,4\\}</span> is a clique of size 4.</li>\n<li>Pairs to check: <span class=\"m\">\\binom42 = 6</span>: <span class=\"m\">12, 13, 14, 23, 24, 34</span>.</li>\n<li>All six are edges, so the certificate is valid. The check took 6 lookups, polynomial in the graph size.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verify a HAMPATH certificate</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Graph edges: <span class=\"m\">s\\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">a\\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">b\\to t</span>, <span class=\"m\">s\\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">a\\to t</span>. Certificate: <span class=\"m\">s, a, b, t</span>.</li>\n<li>Each of the 4 vertices appears exactly once, it starts at <span class=\"m\">s</span> and ends at <span class=\"m\">t</span>.</li>\n<li>Consecutive pairs <span class=\"m\">s\\to a</span>, <span class=\"m\">a\\to b</span>, <span class=\"m\">b\\to t</span> are all edges. Valid.</li>\n<li>A wrong certificate such as <span class=\"m\">s, b, a, t</span> fails, because <span class=\"m\">b\\to a</span> is not an edge.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Show that <span class=\"m\">\\textit{DOUBLE-SAT} = \\{\\langle\\varphi\\rangle : \\varphi \\text{ has at least two different satisfying assignments}\\}</span> is in NP. Then give a certificate showing that <span class=\"m\">(p\\lor q)\\land(\\lnot p\\lor\\lnot q)</span> is in DOUBLE-SAT.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Certificate: two assignments <span class=\"m\">a_1\\ne a_2</span>. Verifier: check that they differ in some variable and that each makes <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> true. This is two formula evaluations and one comparison, polynomial in <span class=\"m\">|\\varphi|</span>, so DOUBLE-SAT <span class=\"m\">\\in</span> NP.\nFor <span class=\"m\">(p\\lor q)\\land(\\lnot p\\lor\\lnot q)</span> (exactly one of <span class=\"m\">p,q</span> true): <span class=\"m\">a_1 = (p{=}1, q{=}0)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(1)\\land(1) = 1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">a_2 = (p{=}0,q{=}1)</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(1)\\land(1) = 1</span>. They differ, so the formula is in DOUBLE-SAT.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>NP is the natural home of search problems in software: scheduling, register allocation, package dependency resolution (installing a set of versions that satisfies all constraints), and test generation. Their solutions are easy to check, which is why tools can confidently report \"here is a valid solution\" even when finding one took exponential worst-case time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Many learning problems are NP problems in disguise: finding the smallest decision tree consistent with data, the sparsest linear model with a given error (best subset selection), or the optimal clustering for <span class=\"m\">k</span>-means. A proposed solution is easy to score, but finding the best one is hard, which motivates greedy methods and convex relaxations such as the lasso.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A teacher checking homework answers works in \"verify\" mode, while the student solving it works in \"search\" mode. Crossword and Sudoku puzzles are fun precisely because checking is easy and solving is hard.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">NP stands for...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">not polynomial</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">nondeterministic polynomial time</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">numerically polynomial</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It includes all of P.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For SUBSET-SUM with <span class=\"m\">S = \\{4,11,16,21,27\\}</span>, how many subsets would brute force consider?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"32\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2^5 = 32</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A certificate for SAT is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">an assignment that makes the formula true</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a proof that no assignment works</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of variables</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The verifier evaluates the formula under the given assignment.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which inclusion is known to hold?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{P}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{EXPTIME}\\subseteq\\text{NP}</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A polynomial-time decider is a verifier that ignores the certificate.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the smallest prime factor of 91, a certificate that 91 is composite?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">91 = 7\\times13</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">TAUTOLOGY (true under every assignment) is naturally in which class?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">NP</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">coNP</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">neither</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A single falsifying assignment certifies that a formula is not a tautology.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "np-completeness": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-hardest-problems-in-np\" data-h=\"3\">The hardest problems in NP</h3>\n<p>In the early 1970s, Stephen Cook and Leonid Levin independently made a startling discovery. Among the problems in NP there are some that are <strong>as hard as all the others combined</strong>: if any one of them has a polynomial-time algorithm, then every problem in NP does, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>. These problems are called <strong>NP-complete</strong>. Soon afterwards Richard Karp showed that 21 famous problems from graph theory, scheduling and logic were all NP-complete. Today thousands are known. NP-completeness is the standard way to show that a problem is \"probably intractable\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-universal-adapter\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a universal adapter</h3>\n<p>Imagine a travel adapter that converts any plug in the world into one standard shape. If you own one charger for that standard shape, you can charge every device. An NP-complete problem is the standard shape: every NP problem can be quickly converted into it, so a fast solver for it would be a fast solver for everything in NP.</p>\n<h3 id=\"polynomial-time-reductions\" data-h=\"3\">Polynomial-time reductions</h3>\n<p>A language <span class=\"m\">A</span> is <strong>polynomial-time reducible</strong> to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, written <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span>, if there is a function <span class=\"m\">f</span> computable in polynomial time with\n<span class=\"mb\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B \\quad\\text{for all } w.</span>\nThis is the mapping reduction from computability with a time limit. If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\in</span> P, then <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> P: compute <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> in polynomial time (so <span class=\"m\">|f(w)|</span> is polynomial in <span class=\"m\">|w|</span>) and run <span class=\"m\">B</span>'s polynomial algorithm on it. Polynomials compose to polynomials. Reductions are also <strong>transitive</strong>: if <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\le_p C</span> then <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p C</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Polynomial-time reduction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A polynomial-time computable <span class=\"m\">f</span> with <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>. We write <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"np-hard-and-np-complete\" data-h=\"3\">NP-hard and NP-complete</h3>\n<p>A language <span class=\"m\">B</span> is <strong>NP-hard</strong> if every <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP satisfies <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span>. It is <strong>NP-complete</strong> if, in addition, <span class=\"m\">B\\in</span> NP. So NP-complete problems are the hardest problems inside NP; NP-hard problems are at least that hard but may lie outside NP (the halting problem is NP-hard but not in NP, as it is undecidable).</p>\n<p>The key consequence: <strong>if any NP-complete problem is in P, then <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span></strong>. And if <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\ne\\text{NP}</span>, then no NP-complete problem has a polynomial algorithm.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">NP-hard</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A problem to which every problem in NP reduces in polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">NP-complete</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A problem that is in NP and NP-hard.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-to-prove-a-problem-is-np-complete\" data-h=\"3\">How to prove a problem is NP-complete</h3>\n<p>Proving NP-hardness from scratch means handling every NP problem at once, which is what the Cook-Levin theorem (next lesson) does for SAT. After that, there is a two-step recipe:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Show <span class=\"m\">B\\in</span> NP by describing a certificate and a polynomial-time verifier.</li>\n<li>Choose a <strong>known</strong> NP-complete problem <span class=\"m\">A</span> and show <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since every NP problem reduces to <span class=\"m\">A</span>, and <span class=\"m\">A</span> reduces to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, transitivity makes every NP problem reduce to <span class=\"m\">B</span>. Mind the direction: reduce the known hard problem <strong>to</strong> the new problem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-first-family-of-reductions\" data-h=\"3\">A first family of reductions</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">G</span> have vertex set <span class=\"m\">V</span> with <span class=\"m\">n</span> vertices. An <strong>independent set</strong> is a set of vertices with no edges between them. A <strong>vertex cover</strong> is a set of vertices touching every edge. A <strong>clique</strong> is a set of vertices all joined to each other.</p>\n<p><strong>INDEPENDENT-SET and VERTEX-COVER.</strong> A set <span class=\"m\">S</span> is independent exactly when <span class=\"m\">V\\setminus S</span> is a vertex cover. Indeed, <span class=\"m\">S</span> has no internal edge iff every edge has at least one endpoint outside <span class=\"m\">S</span>. So <span class=\"m\">G</span> has an independent set of size <span class=\"m\">k</span> iff it has a vertex cover of size <span class=\"m\">n - k</span>. The reduction <span class=\"m\">f(\\langle G,k\\rangle) = \\langle G,n-k\\rangle</span> runs in polynomial time. For a graph with 10 vertices, an independent set of size 4 corresponds to a vertex cover of size 6.</p>\n<p><strong>CLIQUE and INDEPENDENT-SET.</strong> The <strong>complement graph</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span> has an edge exactly where <span class=\"m\">G</span> does not. A clique in <span class=\"m\">G</span> is an independent set in <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f(\\langle G,k\\rangle) = \\langle\\overline G,k\\rangle</span> reduces CLIQUE to INDEPENDENT-SET. Building <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span> takes <span class=\"m\">O(n^2)</span> time.</p>\n<p>Once CLIQUE is shown NP-complete (in the reductions lesson, from 3SAT), these one-line reductions make INDEPENDENT-SET and VERTEX-COVER NP-complete as well.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-np-completeness-means-in-practice\" data-h=\"3\">What NP-completeness means in practice</h3>\n<p>Showing a problem is NP-complete is not a reason to give up; it is a reason to change strategy. Options include exact algorithms that are exponential but fast on typical inputs (modern SAT solvers handle millions of variables in industrial instances), approximation algorithms with guaranteed quality (a vertex cover at most twice the optimum, in polynomial time), restricting to special cases that are in P (2-SAT, or independent sets in trees), and heuristics. Also, if <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\ne\\text{NP}</span>, Ladner's theorem guarantees problems in NP that are neither in P nor NP-complete; factoring and graph isomorphism are suspected examples.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The direction of the reduction is the most common error: to prove <span class=\"m\">B</span> is hard, reduce a known hard problem to <span class=\"m\">B</span>, never <span class=\"m\">B</span> to a known hard problem. The reduction must preserve both yes and no answers and run in polynomial time. NP-complete requires membership in NP; NP-hard does not. \"NP-complete\" is a statement about worst cases, so many instances may still be easy. And NP-completeness does not prove that no polynomial algorithm exists; it shows that finding one would prove <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A polynomial-time reduction <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span> translates instances of <span class=\"m\">A</span> into instances of <span class=\"m\">B</span> with the same answer. NP-hard problems are those to which all of NP reduces; NP-complete problems are NP-hard problems in NP. A polynomial algorithm for any NP-complete problem would prove <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>. To prove a problem NP-complete, show it is in NP and reduce a known NP-complete problem to it. Independent set, vertex cover and clique are linked by simple reductions using complements.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"graph\" data-args='{&quot;graph&quot;: &quot;sample&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Use this graph to practise the three related problems: independent sets, vertex covers and cliques.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Find a set of vertices with no edges between any two of them (an independent set). Make it as large as you can.\n2. Take all the other vertices: check that every edge touches at least one of them (a vertex cover).\n3. Find the largest group of vertices that are all joined to each other (a clique).\n4. Count: does (size of your independent set) + (size of your vertex cover) equal the number of vertices?</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the complement of an independent set is always a vertex cover, so the two sizes add up to <span class=\"m\">n</span>, which is the reduction <span class=\"m\">\\langle G,k\\rangle\\mapsto\\langle G,n-k\\rangle</span>. Checking each answer is quick, edge by edge, but convincing yourself that your independent set is the <strong>largest</strong> possible is much harder; for big graphs no polynomial method is known, because the problem is NP-complete.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">A\\le_p B\\iff\\exists f \\text{ poly-time}:\\ w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_p B,\\ B\\in\\text{P}\\Rightarrow A\\in\\text{P};\\qquad A\\le_p B\\le_p C\\Rightarrow A\\le_p C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">B \\text{ NP-complete} \\iff B\\in\\text{NP}\\ \\land\\ \\forall A\\in\\text{NP}: A\\le_p B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S \\text{ independent}\\iff V\\setminus S \\text{ vertex cover};\\qquad \\text{clique in } G\\iff\\text{independent set in }\\overline G</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">NP-complete problems decide P versus NP</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If some NP-complete problem <span class=\"m\">B</span> is in P, then <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP. Since <span class=\"m\">B</span> is NP-hard, <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span> by some <span class=\"m\">f</span> running in time <span class=\"m\">n^a</span>, so <span class=\"m\">|f(w)|\\le|w|^a</span> (plus a constant). <span class=\"m\">B</span> is decided in time <span class=\"m\">m^b</span>. On input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, compute <span class=\"m\">f(w)</span> and decide <span class=\"m\">B</span>: total time <span class=\"m\">O(n^a + n^{ab})</span>, polynomial. So <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> P, and as <span class=\"m\">A</span> was arbitrary, <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{P}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Transitivity</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p B</span> and <span class=\"m\">B\\le_p C</span>, then <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p C</span>. Hence if <span class=\"m\">A</span> is NP-complete, <span class=\"m\">C\\in</span> NP and <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p C</span>, then <span class=\"m\">C</span> is NP-complete.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Compose the reductions: <span class=\"m\">g(f(w))</span>. <span class=\"m\">f</span> runs in time <span class=\"m\">n^a</span>, producing output of length at most <span class=\"m\">n^a</span>; <span class=\"m\">g</span> then runs in time <span class=\"m\">(n^a)^b = n^{ab}</span>. The composition is polynomial and <span class=\"m\">w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B\\iff g(f(w))\\in C</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reduce INDEPENDENT-SET to VERTEX-COVER on a path of 5 vertices</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Path <span class=\"m\">1-2-3-4-5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">n = 5</span>. Question: is there an independent set of size 3?</li>\n<li>Reduction output: is there a vertex cover of size <span class=\"m\">5 - 3 = 2</span>?</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\{2,4\\}</span> touches every edge <span class=\"m\">12, 23, 34, 45</span>: yes, a cover of size 2.</li>\n<li>Its complement <span class=\"m\">\\{1,3,5\\}</span> is independent of size 3. Both answers are \"yes\", as the reduction promises.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reduce CLIQUE to INDEPENDENT-SET with the complement graph</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">G</span>: vertices <span class=\"m\">1,2,3,4</span>, edges <span class=\"m\">12, 13, 23, 34</span>. Is there a clique of size 3? Yes: <span class=\"m\">\\{1,2,3\\}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span> has the missing edges: <span class=\"m\">14, 24</span> (the complete graph has 6 edges, <span class=\"m\">G</span> has 4, so <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span> has 2).</li>\n<li>In <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\{1,2,3\\}</span> has no internal edges: an independent set of size 3. The answers agree.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Suppose you have proved <span class=\"m\">\\text{VERTEX-COVER}\\le_p X</span> for a new problem <span class=\"m\">X</span>. (a) What else must you show to conclude <span class=\"m\">X</span> is NP-complete? (b) A classmate instead proves <span class=\"m\">X\\le_p\\text{VERTEX-COVER}</span>. What does that show? (c) If someone finds an <span class=\"m\">O(n^4)</span> algorithm for <span class=\"m\">X</span>, what follows?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) That <span class=\"m\">X\\in</span> NP: give a certificate and a polynomial-time verifier. Together with the reduction from the NP-complete VERTEX-COVER, <span class=\"m\">X</span> is NP-complete.\n(b) Only that <span class=\"m\">X</span> is no harder than VERTEX-COVER; in particular <span class=\"m\">X\\in</span> NP (if the reduction is polynomial). It says nothing about <span class=\"m\">X</span> being hard.\n(c) Since VERTEX-COVER <span class=\"m\">\\le_p X</span> and <span class=\"m\">X\\in</span> P, VERTEX-COVER <span class=\"m\">\\in</span> P, and because VERTEX-COVER is NP-complete, <span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>When a software requirement turns out to be NP-complete (optimal timetabling, bin packing of virtual machines onto servers, minimal test suites), engineers switch to approximation algorithms, integer programming solvers or SAT/SMT solvers, and stop searching for an exact polynomial algorithm. Recognising NP-completeness early saves enormous effort.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Exact inference in general Bayesian networks, finding the maximum a posteriori assignment in a Markov random field, and training a 3-node neural network to zero error are NP-hard. These results explain why ML relies on approximate inference (variational methods, sampling) and on local optimisation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Airlines assigning crews to flights, universities scheduling exams without clashes, and delivery firms loading trucks all face NP-complete problems. They succeed in practice with good heuristics and solvers, not by finding a polynomial algorithm.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">To prove a new problem <span class=\"m\">B</span> is NP-hard, you show...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a known NP-complete problem reduces to <span class=\"m\">B</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">B</span> reduces to a known NP-complete problem</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">B</span> has an exponential algorithm</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The hardness flows from the known problem to <span class=\"m\">B</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A graph has 12 vertices. It has an independent set of size 5 exactly when it has a vertex cover of size...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">n - k = 12 - 5 = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which problem is NP-hard but not NP-complete?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">SAT</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">CLIQUE</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the halting problem</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It is NP-hard but undecidable, so not in NP.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If an NP-complete problem is shown to be in P, then...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{P} = \\text{NP}</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">every problem is decidable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">nothing changes</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Every NP problem reduces to it in polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The complete graph on 5 vertices has 10 edges. If <span class=\"m\">G</span> on 5 vertices has 6 edges, how many edges does <span class=\"m\">\\overline G</span> have?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">10 - 6 = 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An NP-complete problem must be...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">in NP and NP-hard</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">outside NP</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">solvable in polynomial time</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both conditions are required.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "cook-levin": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-domino\" data-h=\"3\">The first domino</h3>\n<p>Reductions let us spread NP-completeness from one problem to another, but the chain has to start somewhere. Some problem must be shown NP-hard <strong>directly</strong>, by reducing every NP problem to it at once. In 1971 Stephen Cook proved this for SAT, the satisfiability problem for Boolean formulas; Leonid Levin found the same result independently in the Soviet Union. The <strong>Cook-Levin theorem</strong> is the first domino, and every later NP-completeness proof leans on it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-a-film-strip\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine a film strip</h3>\n<p>Imagine filming a Turing machine at work, one frame per step. Each frame shows the tape, the head position and the state. A computation is correct if the first frame shows the input, each frame follows legally from the previous one, and some frame shows the accept state. Checking that a film is legitimate is a <strong>local</strong> job: you only ever compare a small patch of one frame with the patch just below it. The Cook-Levin proof writes these local checks as a Boolean formula. The formula is satisfiable exactly when some legitimate accepting film exists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-we-need-to-show\" data-h=\"3\">What we need to show</h3>\n<p>SAT is in NP: the certificate is a satisfying assignment, checked by evaluating the formula. The hard part is NP-hardness. Take any language <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP, decided by a nondeterministic TM <span class=\"m\">N</span> in time <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> (we can arrange <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> exactly, by padding). For each input <span class=\"m\">w</span> of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>, we must build, in polynomial time, a formula <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_w</span> such that\n<span class=\"mb\">\\varphi_w \\text{ is satisfiable}\\iff N \\text{ accepts } w.</span></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-tableau\" data-h=\"3\">The tableau</h3>\n<p>An accepting computation of <span class=\"m\">N</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span> takes at most <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> steps and so uses at most <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> tape cells. Write its configurations as the rows of an <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> table, the <strong>tableau</strong>: row 1 is the start configuration, row <span class=\"m\">i+1</span> follows from row <span class=\"m\">i</span> by one move of <span class=\"m\">N</span>, and each row is a configuration written as a string with the state symbol placed just before the head's cell, framed by a boundary symbol <span class=\"m\">\\#</span> at each end. If <span class=\"m\">N</span> accepts earlier, the last configuration is simply repeated.</p>\n<p>Each cell holds one symbol from <span class=\"m\">C = Q\\cup\\Gamma\\cup\\{\\#\\}</span>. For every cell <span class=\"m\">(i,j)</span> and symbol <span class=\"m\">s\\in C</span> we create a Boolean variable <span class=\"m\">x_{i,j,s}</span> meaning \"cell <span class=\"m\">(i,j)</span> contains <span class=\"m\">s</span>\". With an <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> tableau there are <span class=\"m\">n^{2k}\\cdot|C|</span> variables. For example, if <span class=\"m\">n^k = 10</span> and <span class=\"m\">|C| = 20</span>, there are <span class=\"m\">100</span> cells and <span class=\"m\">2{,}000</span> variables. Since <span class=\"m\">N</span> is fixed, <span class=\"m\">|C|</span> is a constant, so the number of variables is polynomial in <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Tableau</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> table whose rows are the successive configurations of a nondeterministic TM on input <span class=\"m\">w</span>, from the start configuration to (possibly) an accepting one.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-four-parts-of-the-formula\" data-h=\"3\">The four parts of the formula</h3>\n<p><span class=\"m\">\\varphi_w = \\varphi_{\\text{cell}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{start}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{move}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span>.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}}</span>: each cell holds exactly one symbol.</strong> For each cell, \"at least one symbol\" is the clause <span class=\"m\">\\bigvee_{s\\in C}x_{i,j,s}</span>, and \"at most one\" is <span class=\"m\">\\bigwedge_{s\\ne t}(\\lnot x_{i,j,s}\\lor\\lnot x_{i,j,t})</span>. That is <span class=\"m\">O(|C|^2)</span> clauses per cell, so <span class=\"m\">O(n^{2k})</span> in total.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{start}}</span>: the first row is the start configuration.</strong> It is a conjunction of single variables: <span class=\"m\">x_{1,1,\\#}\\land x_{1,2,q_0}\\land x_{1,3,w_1}\\land\\cdots\\land x_{1,n+2,w_n}\\land x_{1,n+3,\\sqcup}\\land\\cdots</span>, ending with <span class=\"m\">\\#</span>. That is <span class=\"m\">n^k</span> variables.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span>: the accept state appears somewhere.</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\bigvee_{i,j}x_{i,j,q_{\\text{acc}}}</span>, one big clause of <span class=\"m\">n^{2k}</span> variables.</p>\n<p><strong><span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{move}}</span>: each row follows legally from the one above.</strong> This is the clever part. Look at every <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> <strong>window</strong> of the tableau: three adjacent cells in one row and the three cells directly below. A window is <strong>legal</strong> if it could appear in a correct computation according to <span class=\"m\">N</span>'s transition function. For example, if <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q_1, a)</span> includes <span class=\"m\">(q_2, b, R)</span>, then the window with top row <span class=\"m\">q_1\\,a\\,c</span> and bottom row <span class=\"m\">b\\,q_2\\,c</span> is legal, and a window away from the head, such as <span class=\"m\">a\\,b\\,a</span> above <span class=\"m\">a\\,b\\,a</span>, is legal because cells away from the head do not change. The key fact is that <strong>if the top row is a configuration and every window is legal, then each row is a legal successor of the row above it</strong>. Since <span class=\"m\">N</span> is fixed, there are only finitely many legal windows, and for each window position we write \"the window is one of the legal ones\" as a formula of constant size. There are <span class=\"m\">O(n^{2k})</span> window positions.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Legal window</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> block of tableau cells that is consistent with the transition function: it could occur in two consecutive configurations of the machine.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">N</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>, take an accepting branch and fill in its tableau: setting <span class=\"m\">x_{i,j,s}</span> true exactly for the symbols in that tableau satisfies all four parts. Conversely, a satisfying assignment picks one symbol per cell (<span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}}</span>), the first row is the start configuration (<span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{start}}</span>), each row legally follows the previous one (<span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{move}}</span>), and the accept state appears (<span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span>). So it describes an accepting computation of <span class=\"m\">N</span> on <span class=\"m\">w</span>. The whole formula has size <span class=\"m\">O(n^{2k})</span>, and it can be written down by a simple program in polynomial time, which makes the construction a polynomial-time reduction from <span class=\"m\">A</span> to SAT.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3sat\" data-h=\"3\">3SAT</h3>\n<p>The formula above can be put into <strong>conjunctive normal form</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{start}}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span> already are, and each constant-size window formula can be converted to CNF with constant blow-up. Long clauses can be split into clauses of exactly three literals using new variables: <span class=\"m\">(a\\lor b\\lor c\\lor d)</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">(a\\lor b\\lor z)\\land(\\lnot z\\lor c\\lor d)</span>, which is satisfiable exactly when the original is. So <strong>3SAT</strong>, satisfiability of formulas in CNF with three literals per clause, is also NP-complete. This is the most popular starting point for further reductions. By contrast, <strong>2SAT</strong> is in P, solvable in linear time using implication graphs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The theorem does not say that SAT is hard to solve on typical formulas; modern SAT solvers handle huge industrial instances. It is a worst-case statement. The reduction depends on the fixed machine <span class=\"m\">N</span> for the language <span class=\"m\">A</span>, so constants like <span class=\"m\">|C|</span> are fixed while <span class=\"m\">n</span> varies. Checking only that consecutive rows agree away from the head is not enough; the <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> windows must also capture the head's moves, which is why the window is 3 cells wide. And splitting a long clause into 3-clauses requires fresh variables for each clause; reusing them can change satisfiability.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The Cook-Levin theorem states that SAT is NP-complete. For any language decided by a nondeterministic machine in time <span class=\"m\">n^k</span>, the reduction encodes a possible computation as an <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> tableau with Boolean variables <span class=\"m\">x_{i,j,s}</span>, and builds a formula of polynomial size stating that cells hold one symbol each, the first row is the start configuration, every <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> window is legal, and the accept state appears. The formula is satisfiable exactly when the machine accepts. With a conversion to three literals per clause, 3SAT is NP-complete too.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;binary increment (+1)&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: 1011}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Each step is one row of the tableau. Only the cells next to the head can change, which is why checking 2×3 windows is enough.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Step the machine and, for each step, write the tape as one row of a table, with the state written just before the head's cell.\n2. Compare two consecutive rows: mark the cells that changed.\n3. Repeat for several steps, including the carry steps that move left.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> between consecutive rows, at most the cell under the head and its immediate neighbours change: the symbol written, and where the state marker moves. Everything else is copied. That is exactly why legal <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> windows, checked at every position, are enough to certify that the whole row follows legally. The Cook-Levin formula is a list of such local checks for every window of an <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> table.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\varphi_w = \\varphi_{\\text{cell}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{start}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{move}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}} = \\bigwedge_{i,j}\\Big[\\Big(\\bigvee_{s\\in C}x_{i,j,s}\\Big)\\land\\bigwedge_{s\\ne t}(\\lnot x_{i,j,s}\\lor\\lnot x_{i,j,t})\\Big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{variables} = n^{2k}\\cdot|C|;\\qquad |\\varphi_w| = O(n^{2k})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(a\\lor b\\lor c\\lor d)\\ \\leadsto\\ (a\\lor b\\lor z)\\land(\\lnot z\\lor c\\lor d)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Cook-Levin theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>SAT is NP-complete.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>SAT is in NP: a satisfying assignment is a certificate checkable in linear time. For NP-hardness, let <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP be decided by an NTM <span class=\"m\">N</span> in time <span class=\"m\">n^k</span>. Map <span class=\"m\">w</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_w</span> as constructed. If <span class=\"m\">N</span> accepts <span class=\"m\">w</span>, the tableau of an accepting branch gives a satisfying assignment. If <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_w</span> is satisfied, the true variables define a tableau with one symbol per cell whose first row is the start configuration, every window legal (hence every row a legal successor of the previous one), and containing <span class=\"m\">q_{\\text{acc}}</span>: an accepting computation. The formula has <span class=\"m\">O(n^{2k})</span> size and is produced by a polynomial-time procedure, so <span class=\"m\">A\\le_p</span> SAT.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">3SAT is NP-complete</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Satisfiability of CNF formulas with exactly three literals per clause is NP-complete.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>3SAT is in NP. Reduce SAT (in the CNF form produced by Cook-Levin) to 3SAT: a clause <span class=\"m\">(\\ell_1\\lor\\cdots\\lor\\ell_m)</span> with <span class=\"m\">m &gt; 3</span> becomes <span class=\"m\">(\\ell_1\\lor\\ell_2\\lor z_1)\\land(\\lnot z_1\\lor\\ell_3\\lor z_2)\\land\\cdots\\land(\\lnot z_{m-3}\\lor\\ell_{m-1}\\lor\\ell_m)</span> with fresh <span class=\"m\">z</span>s; shorter clauses are padded by repeating a literal. If some <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i</span> is true, set the <span class=\"m\">z</span>s to satisfy the other clauses of the chain; if all <span class=\"m\">\\ell_i</span> are false, the chain forces a contradiction. The output is linear in the input size.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Count the variables and cells for a small tableau</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Suppose <span class=\"m\">n^k = 8</span> and <span class=\"m\">|C| = 15</span>.</li>\n<li>Cells: <span class=\"m\">8\\times8 = 64</span>.</li>\n<li>Variables: <span class=\"m\">64\\times15 = 960</span>.</li>\n<li>Clauses in <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}}</span> per cell: <span class=\"m\">1 + \\binom{15}2 = 1 + 105 = 106</span>, so <span class=\"m\">64\\times106 = 6{,}784</span> in total. Polynomial in <span class=\"m\">n</span>, as required.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Split a 5-literal clause into 3SAT</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Clause: <span class=\"m\">(a\\lor b\\lor c\\lor d\\lor e)</span>, <span class=\"m\">m = 5</span>, so <span class=\"m\">m - 3 = 2</span> new variables.</li>\n<li>Output: <span class=\"m\">(a\\lor b\\lor z_1)\\land(\\lnot z_1\\lor c\\lor z_2)\\land(\\lnot z_2\\lor d\\lor e)</span>.</li>\n<li>If <span class=\"m\">c</span> is true: set <span class=\"m\">z_1</span> = true (satisfies clause 1), clause 2 holds by <span class=\"m\">c</span>, set <span class=\"m\">z_2</span> = false (clause 3 holds by <span class=\"m\">\\lnot z_2</span>).</li>\n<li>If all five are false: clause 1 forces <span class=\"m\">z_1</span>, clause 2 then forces <span class=\"m\">z_2</span>, and clause 3 fails. Satisfiability is preserved.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Decide whether a window is legal</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Transition: <span class=\"m\">\\delta(q, 0) = \\{(r, 1, R)\\}</span>.</li>\n<li>Window top: <span class=\"m\">0\\ q\\ 0</span>; bottom: <span class=\"m\">0\\ 1\\ r</span>. The head was on the second 0, it wrote 1 and moved right, so the state symbol now sits before the third cell: legal.</li>\n<li>Window top: <span class=\"m\">0\\ q\\ 0</span>; bottom: <span class=\"m\">1\\ 1\\ r</span>. The left cell changed although the head was not on it: illegal.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In the Cook-Levin construction, explain why <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{move}}</span> uses windows of size <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> rather than <span class=\"m\">2\\times1</span> (single cells), and estimate the total size of <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_{\\text{move}}</span> for an <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> tableau.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>A single cell cannot tell whether it should change: that depends on whether the head (the state symbol) is next to it, and which way the head moves. A width-3 window contains the head's cell and both neighbours, so it sees the state, the symbol read, and where the state symbol goes; a <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> window is the smallest that captures one move of the head.\nThere are <span class=\"m\">(n^k-1)(n^k-2) = O(n^{2k})</span> window positions, and for each the formula \"the window is one of the legal windows\" has constant size (the number of legal windows depends only on the fixed machine <span class=\"m\">N</span>). So <span class=\"m\">|\\varphi_{\\text{move}}| = O(n^{2k})</span>, polynomial.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Cook-Levin idea, encoding computations as formulas, is the basis of <strong>bounded model checking</strong> and symbolic execution: hardware and software are unrolled for <span class=\"m\">k</span> steps, turned into a SAT formula, and handed to a SAT solver to find bugs. Intel, AMD and Amazon use such tools to verify chips and cloud security policies.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Neurosymbolic methods and verification tools encode a neural network with ReLU activations as constraints and ask a SAT or SMT solver whether an input exists that changes the output. This is NP-hard, as the Cook-Levin style encoding suggests, but practical for small networks.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Sudoku, scheduling and product-configuration puzzles (which options of a car can be combined?) are solved in practice by translating them into SAT formulas and running a SAT solver, the same translation idea the Cook-Levin theorem uses in reverse.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">What is the first problem proved NP-complete by the Cook-Levin theorem?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">SAT</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">CLIQUE</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">HAMPATH</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Other problems follow by reductions from SAT or 3SAT.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">An <span class=\"m\">n^k\\times n^k</span> tableau with <span class=\"m\">n^k = 10</span> has how many cells?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"100\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">10\\times10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With <span class=\"m\">100</span> cells and <span class=\"m\">|C| = 20</span> symbols, how many variables <span class=\"m\">x_{i,j,s}</span> are there?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2000\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>One per cell and symbol.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is a <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> window enough to check a move?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">only cells next to the head can change in one step</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the tape has only three cells</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">each configuration has three states</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The window sees the head's cell and both neighbours.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">How many new variables are needed to split a clause with 6 literals into 3-literal clauses?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">m - 3 = 3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which satisfiability problem is in P?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">3SAT</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">2SAT</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">SAT</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>2SAT is solvable in linear time with implication graphs.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "np-complete-reductions": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"spreading-the-hardness\" data-h=\"3\">Spreading the hardness</h3>\n<p>With 3SAT known to be NP-complete, we can prove other problems NP-complete by reducing 3SAT to them. The art lies in designing <strong>gadgets</strong>: small pieces of graph or number that behave like variables (\"choose true or false\") and like clauses (\"at least one of these must hold\"). This lesson walks through the classic reductions from Karp's 1972 paper, which turned NP-completeness from a curiosity into a central idea of computer science.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-translating-a-puzzle\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine translating a puzzle</h3>\n<p>Suppose you can only solve jigsaw puzzles, and someone hands you a logic puzzle. If you could design jigsaw pieces so that the jigsaw can be completed exactly when the logic puzzle has a solution, you could solve logic puzzles too. A reduction is that translation. The pieces have to force the right behaviour: a \"variable piece\" fits in exactly two ways, and a \"clause piece\" fits only if a neighbouring variable piece is turned the right way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3sat-to-clique\" data-h=\"3\">3SAT to CLIQUE</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> be a 3-CNF formula with <span class=\"m\">k</span> clauses. Build a graph <span class=\"m\">G</span> with <span class=\"m\">3k</span> vertices, one for each literal occurrence, grouped into <span class=\"m\">k</span> triples, one per clause. Join two vertices by an edge <strong>unless</strong> they are in the same clause, or they are contradictory literals such as <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lnot x</span>. Ask whether <span class=\"m\">G</span> has a clique of size <span class=\"m\">k</span>.</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> has a satisfying assignment, pick one true literal from each clause. These <span class=\"m\">k</span> vertices lie in different clauses, and no two are contradictory (they are all true), so they form a <span class=\"m\">k</span>-clique. Conversely, a <span class=\"m\">k</span>-clique must use exactly one vertex per clause (vertices in the same clause are not joined), and contains no contradictory pair, so we can set all its literals true consistently, satisfying every clause. The construction takes <span class=\"m\">O(k^2)</span> time.</p>\n<p>Example: <span class=\"m\">\\varphi = (x_1\\lor x_1\\lor x_2)\\land(\\lnot x_1\\lor\\lnot x_2\\lor\\lnot x_2)\\land(\\lnot x_1\\lor x_2\\lor x_2)</span> has <span class=\"m\">k = 3</span> clauses, so <span class=\"m\">G</span> has 9 vertices. The assignment <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> = false, <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> = true makes <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> (clause 1), <span class=\"m\">\\lnot x_1</span> (clause 2) and <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> (clause 3) true; these three vertices are pairwise joined, a 3-clique.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gadget</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A small component of a reduction that simulates one part of the source problem, such as a variable's choice of value or a clause's requirement.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"3sat-to-vertex-cover\" data-h=\"3\">3SAT to VERTEX-COVER</h3>\n<p>For each variable <span class=\"m\">x</span>, create two vertices <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lnot x</span> joined by an edge (a variable gadget: a cover must take at least one of them). For each clause, create a triangle of three vertices, one per literal (a clause gadget: a cover must take at least two of its three corners). Join each triangle corner to the variable vertex with the same literal. With <span class=\"m\">m</span> variables and <span class=\"m\">l</span> clauses, there are <span class=\"m\">2m + 3l</span> vertices, and we ask for a vertex cover of size <span class=\"m\">k = m + 2l</span>.</p>\n<p>Given a satisfying assignment, put the true literal of each variable gadget into the cover (<span class=\"m\">m</span> vertices). In each triangle, some corner is joined to a true literal, whose edge is already covered; put the other two corners in the cover (<span class=\"m\">2l</span> vertices). Every edge is covered. Conversely, a cover of size exactly <span class=\"m\">m + 2l</span> must use exactly one vertex per variable edge and two per triangle, and the uncovered corner of each triangle must have its outside edge covered by a variable vertex, which tells us a true literal in that clause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3sat-to-subset-sum\" data-h=\"3\">3SAT to SUBSET-SUM</h3>\n<p>Numbers can simulate logic too. Write numbers in decimal with one digit position per variable and one per clause. For each variable <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> make two numbers <span class=\"m\">y_i</span> (for <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> true) and <span class=\"m\">z_i</span> (for <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> false), both with a 1 in variable position <span class=\"m\">i</span>; <span class=\"m\">y_i</span> also has a 1 in each clause position where <span class=\"m\">x_i</span> appears, and <span class=\"m\">z_i</span> a 1 in each clause position where <span class=\"m\">\\lnot x_i</span> appears. For each clause add two \"slack\" numbers with a 1 in its position. The target <span class=\"m\">t</span> has 1 in every variable position and 3 in every clause position. Choosing exactly one of <span class=\"m\">y_i, z_i</span> is forced by the variable digits; a clause digit can reach 3 with the slack numbers only if at least one true literal contributes. Because no digit position ever sums to more than 5, there are no carries, and digits act independently.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hampath-and-3-colouring\" data-h=\"3\">HAMPATH and 3-COLOURING</h3>\n<p><strong>HAMPATH</strong> is NP-complete by a reduction in which each variable becomes a \"diamond\" chain of vertices that a Hamiltonian path can traverse left-to-right (true) or right-to-left (false), and each clause becomes a single vertex that can be visited by a detour only from a chain traversed in the matching direction. From HAMPATH, the <strong>travelling salesman</strong> decision problem (\"is there a tour of length at most <span class=\"m\">L</span>?\") follows easily.</p>\n<p><strong>3-COLOURING</strong> (can the vertices be coloured with 3 colours so that adjacent vertices differ?) is NP-complete via a gadget with a \"palette\" triangle of colours True, False and Base; each variable is joined to Base so it must be True or False, and a small OR-gadget per clause can be coloured only if one of its literals is True. In contrast, 2-COLOURING is in P: it is just testing whether a graph is bipartite with BFS.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-family-tree\" data-h=\"3\">The family tree</h3>\n<p>These reductions form a tree rooted at SAT: SAT <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> 3SAT <span class=\"m\">\\to</span> {CLIQUE, VERTEX-COVER, SUBSET-SUM, HAMPATH, 3-COLOURING}, with INDEPENDENT-SET reached from CLIQUE, TSP from HAMPATH, and KNAPSACK and PARTITION from SUBSET-SUM. Thousands of problems hang off this tree in scheduling, biology, games and logistics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>A reduction must work in <strong>both directions</strong>: satisfiable formulas must map to yes-instances and unsatisfiable ones to no-instances. Always check the size: the output must be polynomial in the input, and parameters such as <span class=\"m\">k = m + 2l</span> must be computed, not guessed. In SUBSET-SUM, the digits must not carry, which is why a large enough base is used. And always prove the target problem is in NP; a reduction alone only gives NP-hardness.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Classic NP-completeness proofs reduce 3SAT to new problems using gadgets. For CLIQUE, literal occurrences become vertices joined unless they conflict, and a <span class=\"m\">k</span>-clique picks one true literal per clause. For VERTEX-COVER, variable edges and clause triangles give a cover of size <span class=\"m\">m + 2l</span> exactly when the formula is satisfiable. SUBSET-SUM encodes variables and clauses as decimal digits, HAMPATH uses direction-choosing chains, and 3-COLOURING uses a palette triangle. Together they make a large family of natural problems NP-complete.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"truth-table\" data-args='{&quot;expr&quot;: &quot;(x_\\u2228_y_\\u2228_z)_\\u2227_(\\u00acx_\\u2228_\\u00acy_\\u2228_z)_\\u2227_(x_\\u2228_\\u00acz_\\u2228_y)&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A 3-CNF formula with 3 clauses. Each satisfying row picks a true literal in every clause, which is a 3-clique in the CLIQUE reduction graph.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Find a row where the formula is true.\n2. For that row, pick one true literal in each of the three clauses.\n3. Check that your three literals are not contradictory, so they would be joined pairwise in the 9-vertex reduction graph.\n4. Find a false row and see which clause has no true literal.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> every true row gives a choice of one true literal per clause, and such a choice never contains both <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\lnot x</span>, so it is a 3-clique in the reduction graph. A false row leaves some clause with all literals false, so no clique can use that clause's triple. The table and the graph answer the same question in two languages, which is exactly what the reduction promises.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{3SAT}\\to\\text{CLIQUE}:\\ 3k \\text{ vertices},\\ \\text{edge unless same clause or } x,\\lnot x;\\ \\text{target } k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{3SAT}\\to\\text{VERTEX-COVER}:\\ 2m + 3l \\text{ vertices},\\ \\text{target } k = m + 2l</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SUBSET-SUM target}: \\ t = \\underbrace{1\\cdots1}_{m}\\underbrace{3\\cdots3}_{l}\\ (\\text{decimal})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SAT}\\to\\text{3SAT}\\to\\{\\text{CLIQUE, VC, SUBSET-SUM, HAMPATH, 3COL}\\}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">CLIQUE is NP-complete</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>CLIQUE is in NP, and <span class=\"m\">\\text{3SAT}\\le_p\\text{CLIQUE}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A <span class=\"m\">k</span>-clique is a certificate checked with <span class=\"m\">\\binom k2</span> edge lookups, so CLIQUE <span class=\"m\">\\in</span> NP. Map <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> with <span class=\"m\">k</span> clauses to <span class=\"m\">\\langle G,k\\rangle</span> as described. A satisfying assignment gives one true literal per clause; these are in distinct triples and non-contradictory, so pairwise adjacent. A <span class=\"m\">k</span>-clique has at most one vertex per triple, hence exactly one, and no complementary pair, so setting its literals true is consistent and satisfies every clause. <span class=\"m\">G</span> is built in <span class=\"m\">O(k^2)</span> time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Karp's NP-complete problems</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>VERTEX-COVER, INDEPENDENT-SET, SUBSET-SUM, HAMPATH, TSP (decision version) and 3-COLOURING are NP-complete.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Size of the CLIQUE reduction for a 5-clause formula</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">k = 5</span> clauses, so <span class=\"m\">3k = 15</span> vertices.</li>\n<li>Vertices in the same clause are never joined: each vertex has at most <span class=\"m\">15 - 3 = 12</span> possible neighbours.</li>\n<li>Target clique size: <span class=\"m\">k = 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Size of the VERTEX-COVER reduction</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Formula with <span class=\"m\">m = 4</span> variables and <span class=\"m\">l = 3</span> clauses.</li>\n<li>Vertices: <span class=\"m\">2m + 3l = 8 + 9 = 17</span>.</li>\n<li>Edges: <span class=\"m\">m</span> variable edges, <span class=\"m\">3l</span> triangle edges and <span class=\"m\">3l</span> connecting edges: <span class=\"m\">4 + 9 + 9 = 22</span>.</li>\n<li>Target: <span class=\"m\">k = m + 2l = 4 + 6 = 10</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Build the SUBSET-SUM instance for <span class=\"m\">(x_1\\lor\\lnot x_2\\lor x_2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Digit positions: <span class=\"m\">x_1, x_2, c_1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">y_1 = 101</span> (<span class=\"m\">x_1</span> true, appears in <span class=\"m\">c_1</span>); <span class=\"m\">z_1 = 100</span>. <span class=\"m\">y_2 = 011</span> (<span class=\"m\">x_2</span> appears in <span class=\"m\">c_1</span>); <span class=\"m\">z_2 = 011</span> (<span class=\"m\">\\lnot x_2</span> appears in <span class=\"m\">c_1</span>).</li>\n<li>Slack: <span class=\"m\">001, 001</span>. Target <span class=\"m\">t = 113</span>.</li>\n<li>For the assignment <span class=\"m\">x_1</span> true, <span class=\"m\">x_2</span> false, take <span class=\"m\">y_1 + z_2 = 101 + 011 = 112</span>. The clause digit is 2, so add one slack number: <span class=\"m\">112 + 001 = 113 = t</span>. The target is reached exactly, as the reduction promises for a satisfying assignment.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The problem DOMINATING-SET asks: does a graph have a set <span class=\"m\">D</span> of at most <span class=\"m\">k</span> vertices such that every vertex is in <span class=\"m\">D</span> or adjacent to a vertex of <span class=\"m\">D</span>? Show it is NP-complete by reduction from VERTEX-COVER (assume the graph has no isolated vertices).</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>In NP: <span class=\"m\">D</span> is a certificate, checked by scanning each vertex's neighbours.\nReduction: given <span class=\"m\">\\langle G,k\\rangle</span>, form <span class=\"m\">G'</span> by adding, for every edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span>, a new vertex <span class=\"m\">w_{uv}</span> joined to <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">v</span>. Output <span class=\"m\">\\langle G',k\\rangle</span>.\nIf <span class=\"m\">C</span> is a vertex cover of <span class=\"m\">G</span> with <span class=\"m\">|C|\\le k</span>: every original vertex has an edge, whose covering endpoint is in <span class=\"m\">C</span> and adjacent to it (or it is in <span class=\"m\">C</span>); every <span class=\"m\">w_{uv}</span> is adjacent to <span class=\"m\">u</span> or <span class=\"m\">v</span>, one of which is in <span class=\"m\">C</span>. So <span class=\"m\">C</span> dominates <span class=\"m\">G'</span>.\nIf <span class=\"m\">D</span> dominates <span class=\"m\">G'</span> with <span class=\"m\">|D|\\le k</span>: replace each <span class=\"m\">w_{uv}\\in D</span> by <span class=\"m\">u</span>; still dominating and no larger. Now each <span class=\"m\">w_{uv}</span> is dominated by <span class=\"m\">u</span> or <span class=\"m\">v\\in D</span>, so <span class=\"m\">D</span> covers every edge <span class=\"m\">uv</span>: a vertex cover of size <span class=\"m\">\\le k</span>. The construction is polynomial, so DOMINATING-SET is NP-complete.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Recognising a disguised NP-complete problem is a key skill: register allocation is graph colouring, assigning frequencies to radio towers is colouring, and finding a minimal set of servers that touches every service is vertex cover. Libraries and solvers (integer programming, SAT, constraint programming) are the practical response.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Feature selection under a budget, finding a minimal set of training examples that explains a model's predictions, and optimal sparse coding are NP-hard via reductions like these, typically from set cover or exact cover. This is why ML uses greedy selection and <span class=\"m\">\\ell_1</span> relaxations with approximation guarantees.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A sports league scheduling matches so that no team plays twice in a week, with venue and TV constraints, faces a problem that reduces from graph colouring. League planners use solvers that handle such instances well in practice, despite NP-completeness.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In the 3SAT-to-CLIQUE reduction, a formula with 4 clauses gives a graph with how many vertices?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"12\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3k = 12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Two vertices of the CLIQUE reduction graph are NOT joined when...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">they are in the same clause or are contradictory literals</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">they are in different clauses</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">they are both positive literals</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>All other pairs are joined.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For VERTEX-COVER with <span class=\"m\">m = 3</span> variables and <span class=\"m\">l = 2</span> clauses, what is the target <span class=\"m\">k</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"7\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">k = m + 2l = 3 + 4 = 7</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why does the SUBSET-SUM reduction use digits that never carry?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">so each digit position can be analysed independently</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">so the numbers are small</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">because subset sums must be even</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Without carries, each variable and clause digit acts as a separate constraint.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which colouring problem is in P?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">2-COLOURING</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">3-COLOURING</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">both</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>2-colourability is bipartiteness, checked by BFS.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Every reduction proof of NP-completeness also needs...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a proof that the problem is in NP</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">an exponential algorithm</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a reduction to 2SAT</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Reductions alone give NP-hardness.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "space-complexity": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"memory-as-a-resource\" data-h=\"3\">Memory as a resource</h3>\n<p>Time is not the only cost of computing. A phone has limited memory, a router processes packets with a tiny buffer, and a database may be far too large to load at once. <strong>Space complexity</strong> measures how many tape cells a computation uses. Space behaves very differently from time, because space can be <strong>reused</strong>: a cell can be overwritten again and again, while a step, once taken, is gone. This lesson introduces the main space classes and fits them into one picture with P and NP.</p>\n<h3 id=\"imagine-solving-a-maze-with-chalk\" data-h=\"3\">Imagine solving a maze with chalk</h3>\n<p>Imagine exploring a huge maze. One explorer draws chalk marks everywhere she has been, so her \"memory\" is spread over the whole maze. Another carries only a small notebook with room for a few numbers, and repeatedly asks \"can I get from here to there in at most so many steps?\", erasing and reusing the notebook. The second explorer is much slower but uses far less memory. Savitch's theorem, below, is exactly this second strategy.</p>\n<h3 id=\"definitions\" data-h=\"3\">Definitions</h3>\n<p>The <strong>space</strong> used by a decider <span class=\"m\">M</span> on inputs of length <span class=\"m\">n</span> is the maximum number of tape cells it scans on any such input, written <span class=\"m\">s(n)</span>. For a nondeterministic machine, we take the maximum over all branches.\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SPACE}(f(n)) = \\{L : L \\text{ decided by a deterministic TM using } O(f(n)) \\text{ space}\\},</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NSPACE}(f(n)) = \\{L : L \\text{ decided by a nondeterministic TM using } O(f(n)) \\text{ space}\\}.</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{PSPACE} = \\bigcup_k\\text{SPACE}(n^k),\\qquad \\text{NPSPACE} = \\bigcup_k\\text{NSPACE}(n^k).</span></p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Space complexity</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a decider, the function <span class=\"m\">s(n)</span> giving the maximum number of tape cells used on any input of length <span class=\"m\">n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">PSPACE</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The class of languages decidable by a deterministic Turing machine using a polynomial amount of space.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"space-versus-time\" data-h=\"3\">Space versus time</h3>\n<p>A machine that runs for <span class=\"m\">t</span> steps can visit at most <span class=\"m\">t</span> cells, so <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(f(n))\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f(n))</span>. In particular <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE}</span>, and also <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE}</span>: a deterministic machine can try every certificate one after another, <strong>reusing the same space</strong> for each attempt. SAT, for instance, needs only enough space to hold the current assignment and evaluate the formula.</p>\n<p>In the other direction, a machine using space <span class=\"m\">s</span> has a limited number of distinct configurations: with <span class=\"m\">|Q|</span> states, <span class=\"m\">s</span> cells and <span class=\"m\">|\\Gamma|</span> tape symbols, at most <span class=\"m\">|Q|\\cdot s\\cdot|\\Gamma|^s</span>. A decider can never repeat a configuration (it would loop), so its running time is at most that number, which is <span class=\"m\">2^{O(s)}</span>. For example, with <span class=\"m\">|Q| = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">s = 10</span> and <span class=\"m\">|\\Gamma| = 2</span> there are at most <span class=\"m\">5\\cdot10\\cdot2^{10} = 51{,}200</span> configurations. So <span class=\"m\">\\text{SPACE}(f(n))\\subseteq\\text{TIME}(2^{O(f(n))})</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\text{PSPACE}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"savitch-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Savitch's theorem</h3>\n<p>For time, nondeterminism seems to cost an exponential. For space it costs only a <strong>square</strong>. <strong>Savitch's theorem</strong>: for <span class=\"m\">f(n)\\ge\\log n</span>,\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NSPACE}(f(n))\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f(n)^2).</span>\nThe idea is the notebook explorer. Let <span class=\"m\">\\textsc{CanYield}(c_1,c_2,t)</span> ask whether the nondeterministic machine can go from configuration <span class=\"m\">c_1</span> to <span class=\"m\">c_2</span> in at most <span class=\"m\">t</span> steps. If <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>, check directly. Otherwise, try every possible middle configuration <span class=\"m\">c_m</span> and recursively ask <span class=\"m\">\\textsc{CanYield}(c_1,c_m,t/2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\textsc{CanYield}(c_m,c_2,t/2)</span>, reusing the space between the two calls. Each level of recursion stores one configuration, <span class=\"m\">O(f(n))</span> cells, and since <span class=\"m\">t\\le2^{O(f(n))}</span> the recursion depth is <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 t = O(f(n))</span>. Total space: <span class=\"m\">O(f(n))\\cdot O(f(n)) = O(f(n)^2)</span>. As a consequence, <span class=\"m\">\\text{PSPACE} = \\text{NPSPACE}</span>, since the square of a polynomial is a polynomial.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pspace-completeness-quantified-formulas-and-games\" data-h=\"3\">PSPACE-completeness: quantified formulas and games</h3>\n<p>A problem is <strong>PSPACE-complete</strong> if it is in PSPACE and every PSPACE problem reduces to it in polynomial time. The standard example is <strong>TQBF</strong>, true quantified Boolean formulas: formulas with <span class=\"m\">\\forall</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\exists</span> over Boolean variables, such as\n<span class=\"mb\">\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,[(x\\lor y)\\land(\\lnot x\\lor\\lnot y)].</span>\nThis is true: whatever <span class=\"m\">x</span> is, choose <span class=\"m\">y = \\lnot x</span>. Swapping the quantifiers gives <span class=\"m\">\\exists y\\,\\forall x\\,[\\dots]</span>, which is false, since no single <span class=\"m\">y</span> works for both values of <span class=\"m\">x</span>. A recursive evaluator decides TQBF in linear space by trying both values of each variable in turn and reusing the space.</p>\n<p>Quantifiers alternate like moves in a <strong>game</strong>: \"there exists a move for me such that for every reply of my opponent there exists a move for me...\" That is why many two-player games are PSPACE-complete when generalised to <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> boards, including generalised geography, Hex and Othello, while some, like generalised chess and Go with certain rules, are even EXPTIME-complete.</p>\n<h3 id=\"log-space-l-and-nl\" data-h=\"3\">Log space: L and NL</h3>\n<p>Sublinear space needs care, since the input itself has length <span class=\"m\">n</span>. We use a two-tape model: a read-only input tape and a work tape, and count only the work tape. Then <span class=\"m\">\\text{L} = \\text{SPACE}(\\log n)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\text{NL} = \\text{NSPACE}(\\log n)</span>. Logarithmic space is enough for a constant number of counters and pointers into the input: for example, <span class=\"m\">\\{0^n1^n\\}</span> is in L, by counting the 0s and the 1s in binary and comparing.</p>\n<p>PATH (is there a path from <span class=\"m\">s</span> to <span class=\"m\">t</span> in a directed graph?) is in NL: guess the path one vertex at a time, keeping only the current vertex and a step counter, both <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> bits. PATH is <strong>NL-complete</strong> under log-space reductions. By Savitch, NL <span class=\"m\">\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(\\log^2 n)</span>. A configuration graph argument shows NL <span class=\"m\">\\subseteq</span> P. Surprisingly, the Immerman-Szelepcsényi theorem shows <strong>NL = coNL</strong>: there is also a log-space nondeterministic way to certify that <strong>no</strong> path exists.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-picture\" data-h=\"3\">The big picture</h3>\n<p>Putting it together:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{L}\\subseteq\\text{NL}\\subseteq\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE} = \\text{NPSPACE}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}.</span>\nThe hierarchy theorems tell us that <span class=\"m\">\\text{NL}\\ne\\text{PSPACE}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\ne\\text{EXPTIME}</span>, so at least one inclusion in each stretch is strict. But which ones are strict is unknown: nobody has even proved <span class=\"m\">\\text{P}\\ne\\text{PSPACE}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Space can be reused but time cannot, which is why brute-force search over exponentially many certificates fits in polynomial space. Do not count the read-only input tape in log-space classes. Savitch's theorem squares the space; it does not show that nondeterminism is free. And PSPACE-complete problems are believed to be harder than NP-complete ones, though this, too, is unproved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Space complexity counts tape cells. PSPACE contains P and NP and is contained in EXPTIME. Savitch's theorem shows nondeterministic space <span class=\"m\">f(n)</span> can be simulated in deterministic space <span class=\"m\">f(n)^2</span>, so PSPACE = NPSPACE. TQBF and many generalised games are PSPACE-complete. Logarithmic space gives the classes L and NL, with PATH NL-complete and NL = coNL. All these classes fit into one chain from L to EXPTIME, in which some inclusions must be strict, but the exact picture remains open.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g11-tm\" data-args='{&quot;machine&quot;: &quot;decide palindromes&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;0110&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Watch how many cells the machine uses versus how many steps it takes. Time grows like n², but space stays about n.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Run <code>0110</code> and note the step count and the number of tape cells the head ever visits.\n2. Run <code>01111110</code> (twice as long) and compare both numbers.\n3. Run <code>0101010110101010</code> (length 16) and compare again.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the head never leaves the input plus one blank on each side, so the space is about <span class=\"m\">n</span> cells, while the step count roughly quadruples each time the length doubles, like <span class=\"m\">n^2</span>. The machine keeps reusing the same few cells, erasing the symbols it has matched. That is the asymmetry the article describes: space can be reused, time cannot, which is why <span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(f)\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f)</span> but the reverse inclusion costs an exponential.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{TIME}(f)\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f)\\subseteq\\text{TIME}(2^{O(f)})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{configurations}\\le|Q|\\cdot s\\cdot|\\Gamma|^s</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Savitch}: \\ \\text{NSPACE}(f)\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f^2)\\ \\ (f\\ge\\log n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{L}\\subseteq\\text{NL}\\subseteq\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE} = \\text{NPSPACE}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Savitch's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For any space-constructible <span class=\"m\">f(n)\\ge\\log n</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\text{NSPACE}(f(n))\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f(n)^2)</span>. In particular <span class=\"m\">\\text{PSPACE} = \\text{NPSPACE}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">N</span> decide <span class=\"m\">A</span> in nondeterministic space <span class=\"m\">f(n)</span>; it has at most <span class=\"m\">2^{df(n)}</span> configurations for some constant <span class=\"m\">d</span>, so if it accepts, it does so within <span class=\"m\">t = 2^{df(n)}</span> steps. (We may assume a unique accepting configuration.) The recursive procedure <span class=\"m\">\\textsc{CanYield}(c_1,c_2,t)</span> tries every <span class=\"m\">c_m</span> and checks both halves with <span class=\"m\">t/2</span>, reusing space. Recursion depth is <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 t = df(n)</span>, and each level stores <span class=\"m\">O(f(n))</span> cells, so the total is <span class=\"m\">O(f(n)^2)</span>. Accept iff <span class=\"m\">\\textsc{CanYield}(c_{\\text{start}},c_{\\text{accept}},t)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">TQBF is PSPACE-complete</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The language of true fully quantified Boolean formulas is in PSPACE, and every PSPACE language reduces to it in polynomial time.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Space hierarchy theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For space-constructible <span class=\"m\">g</span> with <span class=\"m\">f(n) = o(g(n))</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\text{SPACE}(f(n))\\subsetneq\\text{SPACE}(g(n))</span>. Hence <span class=\"m\">\\text{NL}\\subsetneq\\text{PSPACE}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Evaluate <span class=\"m\">\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,[(x\\lor y)\\land(\\lnot x\\lor\\lnot y)]</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Case <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>: need <span class=\"m\">y</span> with <span class=\"m\">(y)\\land(1)</span>: take <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>. True.</li>\n<li>Case <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>: need <span class=\"m\">y</span> with <span class=\"m\">(1)\\land(\\lnot y)</span>: take <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>. True.</li>\n<li>Both cases succeed, so the formula is true. The evaluator only ever stores the current values of <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>: linear space.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Bound the number of configurations</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Machine with <span class=\"m\">|Q| = 4</span> states, tape alphabet <span class=\"m\">|\\Gamma| = 3</span>, using <span class=\"m\">s = 5</span> cells.</li>\n<li>Configurations: <span class=\"m\">4\\cdot5\\cdot3^5 = 20\\cdot243 = 4{,}860</span>.</li>\n<li>A decider using this space halts within 4,860 steps, since a repeated configuration would mean an infinite loop.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Space used by Savitch's simulation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Suppose <span class=\"m\">f(n) = 20</span> cells per configuration and <span class=\"m\">t = 2^{20}</span> steps.</li>\n<li>Recursion depth: <span class=\"m\">\\log_2 t = 20</span>.</li>\n<li>Space: about <span class=\"m\">20\\times20 = 400</span> cells, compared with <span class=\"m\">2^{20}\\approx10^6</span> steps of running time. Space squares; time explodes.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) Explain why SAT <span class=\"m\">\\in</span> PSPACE using only linear space. (b) Explain why <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE}</span> in general. (c) Is <span class=\"m\">\\exists x\\,\\forall y\\,[(x\\lor y)\\land(x\\lor\\lnot y)]</span> true?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) Try assignments one at a time in binary-counter order, evaluating the formula for each and erasing between tries. Only the current assignment (one bit per variable) and the evaluation workspace are stored: <span class=\"m\">O(n)</span> space, although the time is exponential.\n(b) For <span class=\"m\">A\\in</span> NP with verifier <span class=\"m\">V</span> and certificates of length <span class=\"m\">p(n)</span>, loop over all certificates, reusing the same <span class=\"m\">p(n)</span> cells, and run <span class=\"m\">V</span> (which uses polynomial time, hence polynomial space) on each. Total space is polynomial.\n(c) Take <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>: then <span class=\"m\">(1\\lor y)\\land(1\\lor\\lnot y)</span> is true for both values of <span class=\"m\">y</span>. So the formula is true.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Streaming algorithms, which process data in one pass using polylogarithmic memory (counting distinct elements, finding frequent items), live at the low end of the space hierarchy. At the high end, PSPACE-completeness of games and of model checking temporal logic explains why verifying concurrent systems and solving games are so demanding.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Memory, not time, is often the binding constraint in deep learning: activation checkpointing trades extra computation for lower memory, exactly the time-space trade-off of this lesson, and game-playing systems such as AlphaZero tackle PSPACE-hard games by learning to search only a tiny part of the game tree.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Solving a large maze by marking every visited junction uses memory proportional to the maze; following a smarter rule that only remembers your current position and a counter uses tiny memory but may take much longer. Choosing between them is a space-time trade-off.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Why is <span class=\"m\">\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">certificates can be tried one at a time, reusing the same space</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">NP problems use no memory</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">PSPACE allows nondeterminism</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Space can be reused across attempts; time cannot.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Savitch's theorem says <span class=\"m\">\\text{NSPACE}(f)\\subseteq</span>...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{SPACE}(f)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{SPACE}(f^2)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\text{TIME}(f^2)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The recursion has depth <span class=\"m\">O(f)</span> with <span class=\"m\">O(f)</span> space per level.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A machine has 5 states, a 2-symbol tape alphabet and uses 10 cells. How many configurations can it have, at most?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"51200\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">5\\cdot10\\cdot2^{10} = 51{,}200</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which formula is true?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\forall x\\,\\exists y\\,[x\\ne y]</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\exists y\\,\\forall x\\,[x\\ne y]</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>For every <span class=\"m\">x</span> choose <span class=\"m\">y = \\lnot x</span>; but no single <span class=\"m\">y</span> differs from both values.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which problem is NL-complete?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">PATH in directed graphs</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">TQBF</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">SAT</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Guess the path one vertex at a time with <span class=\"m\">O(\\log n)</span> memory.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using Savitch's simulation with <span class=\"m\">f(n) = 30</span>, roughly how many cells are used?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"900\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f^2 = 30^2 = 900</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "alphabets-strings-languages", "t": "Alphabets, strings and languages", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">|uv| = |u| + |v|, \\qquad |w^k| = k\\,|w|, \\qquad (uv)^R = v^R u^R</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|\\Sigma^k| = |\\Sigma|^k, \\qquad \\Sigma^* = \\bigcup_{k\\ge0} \\Sigma^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{binary strings of length} \\le n:\\ \\ 1 + 2 + \\cdots + 2^n = 2^{n+1} - 1</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1L_2 = \\{xy : x\\in L_1, y\\in L_2\\}, \\qquad L^* = \\bigcup_{k\\ge0} L^k,\\ L^0 = \\{\\varepsilon\\}</span></p>"}, {"l": "dfa", "t": "Deterministic finite automata (DFAs)", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">M = (Q, \\Sigma, \\delta, q_0, F), \\qquad \\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma \\to Q</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\hat\\delta(q,\\varepsilon) = q, \\qquad \\hat\\delta(q, wa) = \\delta(\\hat\\delta(q,w), a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(M) = \\{w \\in \\Sigma^* : \\hat\\delta(q_0, w) \\in F\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{mod-}m \\text{ DFA: } \\delta(r, b) = (2r + b) \\bmod m</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{product DFA: } Q = Q_1 \\times Q_2,\\ \\ \\delta((p,q),a) = (\\delta_1(p,a), \\delta_2(q,a))</span></p>"}, {"l": "nfa", "t": "Nondeterministic finite automata (NFAs)", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma_\\varepsilon \\to \\mathcal P(Q)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S_0 = E(\\{q_0\\}), \\qquad S_{k+1} = E\\Big(\\bigcup_{q\\in S_k} \\delta(q, a_{k+1})\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">N \\text{ accepts } a_1\\cdots a_n \\iff S_n \\cap F \\ne \\varnothing</span></p>"}, {"l": "subset-construction", "t": "The subset construction (NFA to DFA)", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">Q_D \\subseteq \\mathcal P(Q), \\qquad |Q_D| \\le 2^{|Q|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">q_D = E(\\{q_0\\}), \\qquad \\delta_D(S,a) = E\\Big(\\bigcup_{q\\in S}\\delta(q,a)\\Big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F_D = \\{S \\subseteq Q : S\\cap F \\ne\\varnothing\\}</span></p>"}, {"l": "regular-expressions", "t": "Regular expressions", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">L(R_1\\cup R_2) = L(R_1)\\cup L(R_2), \\quad L(R_1R_2) = L(R_1)L(R_2), \\quad L(R^*) = L(R)^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{precedence: } {}^* \\ &gt; \\ \\text{concatenation} \\ &gt; \\ \\cup</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R\\cup\\varnothing = R,\\quad R\\varepsilon = R,\\quad R\\varnothing = \\varnothing,\\quad \\varnothing^* = \\varepsilon,\\quad (R^*)^* = R^*,\\quad R^+ = RR^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">R(S\\cup T) = RS\\cup RT, \\qquad (S\\cup T)R = SR\\cup TR</span></p>"}, {"l": "kleene-theorem", "t": "Kleene's theorem and closure properties", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{state elimination: } p \\xrightarrow{R_1} q \\xrightarrow{R_2 \\text{ (loop)}} q \\xrightarrow{R_3} r \\ \\leadsto\\ p \\xrightarrow{R_1R_2^*R_3 \\,\\cup\\, R_4} r</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{complement: } F' = Q\\setminus F \\quad(\\text{complete DFA})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{product: } Q = Q_1\\times Q_2,\\ \\ \\delta((p,q),a) = (\\delta_1(p,a),\\delta_2(q,a)),\\ \\ F = F_1\\times F_2</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L_1\\cap L_2 = \\overline{\\overline{L_1}\\cup\\overline{L_2}}</span></p>"}, {"l": "pumping-lemma-regular", "t": "The pumping lemma for regular languages", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ regular} \\Rightarrow \\exists p\\ \\forall w\\in L,\\ |w|\\ge p:\\ \\exists\\, w = xyz,\\ |y|&gt;0,\\ |xy|\\le p,\\ \\forall i\\ge 0:\\ xy^iz\\in L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p \\le \\text{number of states of any DFA for } L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{reading } p \\text{ symbols visits } p+1 \\text{ states} \\Rightarrow \\text{a repeat (pigeonhole)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "dfa-minimization", "t": "DFA minimization and the Myhill-Nerode theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">p\\equiv q \\iff \\forall z\\in\\Sigma^*:\\ \\big(\\hat\\delta(p,z)\\in F \\iff \\hat\\delta(q,z)\\in F\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{split } p,q \\text{ if } \\exists a:\\ [\\delta(p,a)] \\ne [\\delta(q,a)]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x\\equiv_L y \\iff \\forall z:\\ (xz\\in L\\iff yz\\in L)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{states of minimal DFA} = \\text{number of classes of } \\equiv_L</span></p>"}, {"l": "context-free-grammars", "t": "Context-free grammars", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">G = (V,\\Sigma,R,S), \\quad A \\to w,\\ A\\in V,\\ w\\in(V\\cup\\Sigma)^*</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(G) = \\{w\\in\\Sigma^* : S\\Rightarrow^* w\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S\\to 0S1\\mid\\varepsilon:\\ \\ L = \\{0^n1^n\\},\\quad 0^n1^n \\text{ takes } n+1 \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{DFA} \\to \\text{CFG}: \\ R_q \\to aR_{\\delta(q,a)},\\ \\ R_q\\to\\varepsilon \\ (q\\in F)</span></p>"}, {"l": "parse-trees-ambiguity", "t": "Parse trees and ambiguity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{yield}(T) = \\text{leaves of } T \\text{ read left to right}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{ambiguous} \\iff \\exists w:\\ w \\text{ has} \\ge 2 \\text{ parse trees} \\iff \\ge 2 \\text{ leftmost derivations}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{trees of } a{+}a{+}\\cdots{+}a \\ (n \\text{ pluses}) = C_n = \\tfrac{1}{n+1}\\tbinom{2n}{n}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">E\\to E+T\\mid T,\\quad T\\to T\\times F\\mid F,\\quad F\\to(E)\\mid a</span></p>"}, {"l": "chomsky-normal-form", "t": "Chomsky normal form and the CYK algorithm", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A\\to BC,\\quad A\\to a,\\quad S\\to\\varepsilon \\ (\\text{start only})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CNF derivation of } w,\\ |w| = n\\ge1: \\ \\text{exactly } 2n-1 \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">T[i,i] = \\{A : A\\to w_i\\},\\qquad T[i,j] = \\{A : A\\to BC,\\ B\\in T[i,k],\\ C\\in T[k+1,j],\\ i\\le k&lt;j\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CYK time} = O(n^3\\cdot|G|)</span></p>"}, {"l": "pushdown-automata", "t": "Pushdown automata", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\delta: Q\\times\\Sigma_\\varepsilon\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon\\to\\mathcal P(Q\\times\\Gamma_\\varepsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">a,\\ X\\to Y:\\ \\text{read } a,\\ \\text{pop } X,\\ \\text{push } Y\\quad(\\text{any may be } \\varepsilon)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFG} \\to \\text{PDA}:\\ \\text{top } A \\Rightarrow \\text{replace by } w \\ (A\\to w);\\ \\ \\text{top } a \\Rightarrow \\text{match input}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{regular} \\subsetneq \\text{DCFL} \\subsetneq \\text{CFL}</span></p>"}, {"l": "cfl-pumping-lemma", "t": "The pumping lemma for context-free languages", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ CFL} \\Rightarrow \\exists p\\ \\forall s\\in L,\\ |s|\\ge p:\\ s = uvxyz,\\ |vy|&gt;0,\\ |vxy|\\le p,\\ \\forall i\\ge0:\\ uv^ixy^iz\\in L</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{height } h \\Rightarrow \\le b^h \\text{ leaves};\\qquad p = b^{|V|+1}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\{a^nb^nc^m\\}\\cap\\{a^mb^nc^n\\} = \\{a^nb^nc^n\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFL}\\cap\\text{regular} = \\text{CFL}</span></p>"}, {"l": "turing-machines", "t": "Turing machines", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">M = (Q,\\Sigma,\\Gamma,\\delta,q_0,q_{\\text{acc}},q_{\\text{rej}}),\\qquad \\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma\\to Q\\times\\Gamma\\times\\{L,R\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{configuration } u\\,q\\,v:\\ \\text{tape } uv,\\ \\text{state } q,\\ \\text{head on first symbol of } v</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{outcomes: accept, reject, loop};\\qquad \\text{decidable}\\subseteq\\text{recognisable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{regular}\\subsetneq\\text{CFL}\\subsetneq\\text{decidable}\\subsetneq\\text{recognisable}</span></p>"}, {"l": "tm-variants", "t": "Variants of Turing machines", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">k\\text{-tape}: \\ \\delta: Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\to Q\\times\\Gamma^k\\times\\{L,R,S\\}^k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multitape time } t(n) \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{single-tape time } O(t(n)^2)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NTM time } t(n),\\ \\le b \\text{ choices} \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{deterministic time } 2^{O(t(n))}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">U(\\langle M,w\\rangle) = M(w)\\quad(\\text{accept / reject / loop})</span></p>"}, {"l": "church-turing-thesis", "t": "The Church-Turing thesis", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{algorithm} \\ \\overset{\\text{thesis}}{\\Longleftrightarrow}\\ \\text{Turing machine that halts on every input}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{TM} \\equiv \\lambda\\text{-calculus} \\equiv \\mu\\text{-recursive functions} \\equiv \\text{RAM} \\equiv \\text{any general programming language}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S(1)=1,\\ S(2)=6,\\ S(3)=21,\\ S(4)=107,\\ S(5)=47{,}176{,}870</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{extended thesis: model time } t \\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{TM time } t^{O(1)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "decidability", "t": "Decidable problems", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{DFA}},\\ A_{\\text{NFA}},\\ A_{\\text{REX}},\\ E_{\\text{DFA}},\\ EQ_{\\text{DFA}},\\ A_{\\text{CFG}},\\ E_{\\text{CFG}}\\ \\text{are decidable};\\quad EQ_{\\text{CFG}} \\text{ is undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(B) = L(C) \\iff \\big(L(B)\\cap\\overline{L(C)}\\big)\\cup\\big(\\overline{L(B)}\\cap L(C)\\big) = \\varnothing</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L \\text{ decidable} \\iff L \\text{ and } \\overline L \\text{ are recognisable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">|\\{\\text{TMs}\\}| = \\aleph_0 &lt; 2^{\\aleph_0} = |\\mathcal P(\\Sigma^*)|</span></p>"}, {"l": "halting-problem", "t": "The halting problem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ accepts } w\\},\\quad \\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}} = \\{\\langle M,w\\rangle : M \\text{ halts on } w\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D(\\langle M\\rangle) = \\begin{cases}\\text{reject} &amp; \\text{if } M \\text{ accepts } \\langle M\\rangle\\\\ \\text{accept} &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D \\text{ accepts } \\langle D\\rangle \\iff D \\text{ does not accept } \\langle D\\rangle</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}} \\text{ recognisable, undecidable};\\quad \\overline{A_{\\text{TM}}} \\text{ not recognisable}</span></p>"}, {"l": "reductions", "t": "Reductions", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B \\iff \\exists \\text{ computable } f\\ \\forall w:\\ w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B,\\ B \\text{ decidable} \\Rightarrow A \\text{ decidable};\\qquad A\\le_m B,\\ A\\text{ undecidable}\\Rightarrow B\\text{ undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_m B \\iff \\overline A\\le_m\\overline B;\\qquad A\\le_m B,\\ B\\le_m C\\Rightarrow A\\le_m C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\textit{HALT}_{\\text{TM}},\\quad A_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m\\overline{E_{\\text{TM}}},\\quad E_{\\text{TM}}\\le_m EQ_{\\text{TM}}</span></p>"}, {"l": "rice-theorem", "t": "Rice's theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">P \\text{ semantic}: \\ L(M_1) = L(M_2)\\Rightarrow\\big(\\langle M_1\\rangle\\in L_P\\iff\\langle M_2\\rangle\\in L_P\\big)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P \\text{ non-trivial}: \\ \\exists\\, L_1\\in P,\\ L_2\\notin P \\ \\ (L_1, L_2 \\text{ recognisable})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Rice}: \\ P \\text{ semantic and non-trivial}\\Rightarrow L_P = \\{\\langle M\\rangle : L(M)\\in P\\} \\text{ undecidable}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{reduction: } L(S) = \\begin{cases}L_0\\in P &amp; M \\text{ accepts } w\\\\ \\varnothing\\notin P &amp; \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}</span></p>"}, {"l": "time-complexity", "t": "Time complexity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">t_M(n) = \\max_{|w| = n}\\ \\#\\text{steps of } M \\text{ on } w</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f = O(g)\\iff \\exists c,n_0\\ \\forall n\\ge n_0:\\ f(n)\\le c\\,g(n);\\qquad f = o(g)\\iff f(n)/g(n)\\to0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{TIME}(t(n)) = \\{L : L \\text{ decided in time } O(t(n))\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{multitape } t \\Rightarrow \\text{single-tape } O(t^2);\\qquad \\text{NTM } t\\Rightarrow\\text{DTM } 2^{O(t)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "class-p", "t": "The class P", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{P} = \\bigcup_{k\\ge1}\\text{TIME}(n^k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{PATH: BFS in } O(v+e);\\qquad \\gcd(x,y) = \\gcd(y, x\\bmod y) \\text{ in } O(\\log x) \\text{ steps}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{CFL}\\subseteq\\text{P}\\ (\\text{CYK } O(n^3));\\qquad \\text{PRIMES}\\in\\text{P}\\ (\\text{AKS, 2002})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{poly}(\\text{poly}(n)) = \\text{poly}(n)</span></p>"}, {"l": "class-np", "t": "The class NP", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A\\in\\text{NP}\\iff\\exists\\ \\text{poly-time } V,\\ \\text{polynomial } p:\\ w\\in A\\iff\\exists c,\\ |c|\\le p(|w|),\\ V(\\langle w,c\\rangle) = \\text{accept}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{NP} = \\bigcup_k\\text{NTIME}(n^k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME};\\qquad \\text{coNP} = \\{\\overline A : A\\in\\text{NP}\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{brute force over certificates}: \\ 2^{p(n)}\\cdot\\text{poly}(n)</span></p>"}, {"l": "np-completeness", "t": "NP-completeness", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">A\\le_p B\\iff\\exists f \\text{ poly-time}:\\ w\\in A\\iff f(w)\\in B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">A\\le_p B,\\ B\\in\\text{P}\\Rightarrow A\\in\\text{P};\\qquad A\\le_p B\\le_p C\\Rightarrow A\\le_p C</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">B \\text{ NP-complete} \\iff B\\in\\text{NP}\\ \\land\\ \\forall A\\in\\text{NP}: A\\le_p B</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">S \\text{ independent}\\iff V\\setminus S \\text{ vertex cover};\\qquad \\text{clique in } G\\iff\\text{independent set in }\\overline G</span></p>"}, {"l": "cook-levin", "t": "The Cook-Levin theorem", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\varphi_w = \\varphi_{\\text{cell}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{start}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{move}}\\land\\varphi_{\\text{accept}}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\varphi_{\\text{cell}} = \\bigwedge_{i,j}\\Big[\\Big(\\bigvee_{s\\in C}x_{i,j,s}\\Big)\\land\\bigwedge_{s\\ne t}(\\lnot x_{i,j,s}\\lor\\lnot x_{i,j,t})\\Big]</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{variables} = n^{2k}\\cdot|C|;\\qquad |\\varphi_w| = O(n^{2k})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">(a\\lor b\\lor c\\lor d)\\ \\leadsto\\ (a\\lor b\\lor z)\\land(\\lnot z\\lor c\\lor d)</span></p>"}, {"l": "np-complete-reductions", "t": "Classic NP-complete reductions", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{3SAT}\\to\\text{CLIQUE}:\\ 3k \\text{ vertices},\\ \\text{edge unless same clause or } x,\\lnot x;\\ \\text{target } k</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{3SAT}\\to\\text{VERTEX-COVER}:\\ 2m + 3l \\text{ vertices},\\ \\text{target } k = m + 2l</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SUBSET-SUM target}: \\ t = \\underbrace{1\\cdots1}_{m}\\underbrace{3\\cdots3}_{l}\\ (\\text{decimal})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{SAT}\\to\\text{3SAT}\\to\\{\\text{CLIQUE, VC, SUBSET-SUM, HAMPATH, 3COL}\\}</span></p>"}, {"l": "space-complexity", "t": "Space complexity", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\text{TIME}(f)\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f)\\subseteq\\text{TIME}(2^{O(f)})</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\#\\text{configurations}\\le|Q|\\cdot s\\cdot|\\Gamma|^s</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{Savitch}: \\ \\text{NSPACE}(f)\\subseteq\\text{SPACE}(f^2)\\ \\ (f\\ge\\log n)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{L}\\subseteq\\text{NL}\\subseteq\\text{P}\\subseteq\\text{NP}\\subseteq\\text{PSPACE} = \\text{NPSPACE}\\subseteq\\text{EXPTIME}</span></p>"}]});
